1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Raymond I, Count of Pallars and Ribagorza
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_I,_Count_of_Pallars_and_Ribagorza
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond I (Catalan: Ramon, Spanish: Raimundo) (fl. 884–920) was the first independent count of Pallars and Ribagorza from 872 until his death. Early speculation made him a scion of the counts of Toulouse,[1] but he is certainly th
2 CONC e "Count Raymond, son of Count Lupus ... in the country of Pallars" (Regimundo comite, filio Luponi comiti ... in pago Paliarensi) of a 920 document.[2] His father may have been the Basque Count Lupus I of Bigorre.[3] During his l
2 CONC ifetime Pallars represented the easternmost extent of the Basque language.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rise to power
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond was a local nobleman who in the aftermath of the assassination of Bernard II of Toulouse by partisans of Bernard Plantapilosa (872) seized authority for himself in Pallars and Ribagorza.[5] The exact circumstances surround
2 CONC ing his rise are obscure, but he had probably held lands south of the Pyrenees from the County of Toulouse prior to gaining independence.[6] Neither is it clear what part the leading men of the regions played in his elevation, but
2 CONC  after the fact Pallars and Ribagorza were not even nominally attached to Francia.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rule
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond immediately made overtures of peace and alliance with the Banu Qasi governors of Huesca and Zaragoza, in 884 even purchasing Zaragoza, only to have it immediately reconquered by the Caliphate. In the end, a policy of Recon
2 CONC quista had to be adopted. Consequentially, his reign saw the encastellation of Pallars of Ribagorza and the proliferation of turres (defensive towers).[7] Castles were built at Leovalles, Castellous, and Lemignano.[8] Raymond also
2 CONC  consolidated his de facto independence from any superior authority by creating a new diocese of Pallars, enabling himself to control the local church. Raymond also established an alliance with the Jiménez dynasty of Navarre. He m
2 CONC arried his sister Dadildis to García Jiménez of Pamplona, and in 905 he collaborated with Alfonso III of Asturias and Abd Allah ibn Lubb al-Qasawi in a coup that unseated Fortún Garcés of Pamplona in favor of Raymond's nephew, San
2 CONC cho Garcés I.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Later years
2 CONT
2 CONT In 904 he was attacked by Lubb ibn Muhammad al-Qasawi, who took 700 prisoners, including Raymond's son Isarn. He lost much of Ribagorza, including Roda and Montpedrós, to Muhammad al-Tawil of Huesca in 907 and thereafter ruled mai
2 CONC nly just Pallars, which had always been his political base. His wife may be the Giniguentes, daughter of Aznar Dat, mentioned in the Códice de Roda, but the text is ambiguous and probably refers to the wife of his son, Isarn.[10] 
2 CONC Pallars was inherited by his two youngest sons, Isarn and Llop, while mostly-overrun Ribagorza went to his two eldest, Miro and Bernard Unifred. Another son of Raymond was Ato, Bishop of Pallars, who appears as brother of both Ber
2 CONC nard and Isarn in contemporary documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramón_I_de_Ribagorza_y_Pallars#Nupcias_y_descendientes Wives and children] (Spanish Wikipedia)
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in first nuptials to a lady of unknown origin named Guinigenta. From this marriage were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Bernardo (died after 956), Count of Ribagorza with his brother Miró.
2 CONT # Isarno (died after 953), Count of Pallars.
2 CONT # Lope (died around 947), Count of Pallars.
2 CONT # Atón de Bigorra, Bishop of Pallars
2 CONT # Miró (died after 955), Count of Ribagorza with his brother Bernardo.
2 CONT # Ava
2 CONT
2 CONT In second nuptials he married the daughter of the Muslim leader Mutarrif ibn Lope, from whose union there were no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ARAGONESE%20NOBILITY.htm#RogerIIVicCarcassonnediedafter1240B (Accessed 24 January 2026)
2 CONT
2 CONT Brother and sister, parents not known: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         RAIMUNDO [I] (-after 893).  Ibn-Hayyân records that Muhammad ibn Lubb was obliged to sell Zaragoza to Raymond comte de Pallars in 884 but that the town was recaptured by the sultan[355].  Conde de Ribargorza.  “Rex Fo
2 CONC rtunio Garcianes” reviewed the territories of the monastery of San Julián de Labasal, at the request of “comite Galindo Asnar”, by charter dated 893, whose dating clause records “...Garcia Sanz in Gallias, Raimundus in Paliares, p
2 CONC agani...Mohomet Ebenlupu in Balleterra et Mohomet Atauel in Osca...”[356].  m ---.  The Codex de Roda records that “Regemondo accepit uxor ---“ and lists as their children “Uernardo, et domno Miro, ac domno Lope, seu domno Ysarno.
2 CONC ..”; the following sentence records that "Istius uxor domna Giniguentes Asnari Datus filia fuit"[357].  Lacarra suggests these sentences should be interpreted to indicate that “Giniguentes” was the wife of Raimundo´s son Isarn.  H
2 CONC e says that, if Conde Raimundo was the husband of “Giniguentes”, her name would have followed his in the first sentence and that it is a reasonable interpretation that the second sentence relates to the wife of Isarn, particularly
2 CONC  because “Giniguentes” is so similar to “Sinegentis” (recorded elsewhere as the name of Isarn´s wife)[358].  Raimundo [I] & his wife had five children:  
2 CONT ** a)         BERNARDO [I] [Unifredo] (-[Mar 950/Nov 956], bur Ovarra).  The Codex de Roda names "Uernardo et domno Miro ac domno Lope seu domno Ysarno" as the children of "Regemondo" and his wife[359].  Conde de Ribagorza.  -    
2 CONC     see below.  
2 CONT ** b)         MIRÓN (-after 3 Jul 954).  The Codex de Roda names "Uernardo et domno Miro ac domno Lope seu domno Ysarno" as the children of "Regemondo" and his wife[360].  “Miro comis” donated “tres villas...prope apendicio de cas
2 CONC tro Arinio...Frexanatum...Arias...Ginalbo” to the abbot of Alaon by charter dated 3 Jul 954[361].  m GEMO, daughter of ---.  “Guilgemus...chomis” donated “de castello Sangici et de Os...decimam” to Ovarra monastery, for the souls 
2 CONC of “mee Guilgelmo chomite et patrem meo Mirone chomite et matrem meam Gemo chomitissa”, by charter dated Nov 975[362].  Mirón & his wife had one child:  
2 CONT *** i)          GUILLEM (-after Nov 975).  Conde de Ribagorza.  A charter dated Jun 962 records testimony given “in judiciio Willelmo comite” relating to property of Alaon monastery[363].  The 11th century “Crónico de Domènec”, fr
2 CONC om the archives of Roda, names “Arnaldus, Wilelmus, Isarnus, Miro, Berengarius, Bego” as “comites” after “Unifredus comes filius Ragimundi” but does not specify the family relationships between them[364].  “Guilgemus...chomis” don
2 CONC ated “de castello Sangici et de Os...decimam” to Ovarra monastery, for the souls of “mee Guilgelmo chomite et patrem meo Mirone chomite et matrem meam Gemo chomitissa”, by charter dated Nov 975[365].  
2 CONT ** c)         ATO (-955).  Bishop of Pallars.  The 11th century “Crónico de Domènec”, from the archives of Roda, records that “Bernardus comes...Ripacurcensis...cum Ato episcopus frater eius” expelled (“expulit”) “Mauros...de Ripa
2 CONC curciensi” that they may expel them “de Paliarensi terra” (“expulliset”)[366].  The late 11th century/early 12th century “Cronicó II d´Alaó”, representing a continuation of the 11th century “Crónico de Domènec”, records that “in t
2 CONC emporibus Bernardi...comitis, Ato frater eius” held “episcopatum in Ripacurcia et in Aran et Paliaris et Superarbi”, adding that he also wished to be “episcopi in Ilerda”[367].  “Atus...episcopus...et...Guillermus...comis” donated
2 CONC  “ecclesia...in Spelunche” to the monastery of Santa Maria and San Pedro de Lavaix by charter dated Mar 947[368].  The testament of "Isarnus...comes Paliarensis et marchio", dated 13 Sep 953, bequeathes property to "fratris meo Ot
2 CONC to episcopo" and to “Sanctæ Mariæ et Sancti Vincentii Gerrensis cœnobii...cum consilio...Sinegentis comtissæ”, and places “filiam meam Ermengarde in mudeburdo Sanctæ Mariæ Gerrensis et Sancti Vincentii”[369].  
2 CONT ** d)         ISARN [Aznar] (-after 13 Sep 953).  The Codex de Roda names "Uernardo et domno Miro ac domno Lope seu domno Ysarno [Aznarium]" as the children of "Regemondo" and his wife, recording that Isarn was captured "in Tutela
2 CONC " and released by "rex Sanzio Garseanis"[370].  Conde de Pallars. -        CONDES de PALLARS.  
2 CONT **e)         LOPE (-after 947).  The Codex de Roda names "Uernardo et domno Miro ac domno Lope seu domno Ysarno" as the children of "Regemondo" and his wife[371].  A different version is provided by the end 13th century “Crònica d
2 CONC ´Alaó Renovada”, according to which “Lupum” was the son of “Borrellus Paliarensis comes”, son of Bernardo [I] Conde de Ribagorza (see above)[372]. -        CONDES de PALLARS.  
2 CONT * 2.         DADILDIS .  The Codex de Roda records that "Garsea Scemenonis" married secondly "domna Dadildi de Paliares soror Regimundi comitis"[373].  m as his second wife, GARCÍA II Jiménez King of Pamplona, son of [JIMENO] & hi
2 CONC s wife -- (-after 885).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT # ^ Serrano y Sans, 127;del Arco y Garay, 359
2 CONT # ^ d'Abadal y de Vinyals, I: 351
2 CONT # ^ d'Abadal y de Vinyals, I: 121
2 CONT # ^ Lewis, 112 n104.
2 CONT # ^ Jump up to: a b Lewis, 110.
2 CONT # ^ Lewis, 112.
2 CONT # ^ Lewis, 131–132.
2 CONT # ^ Lewis, 229–230.
2 CONT # ^ Pérez de Urbel, 297
2 CONT # ^ Lacarra, 234. In concluding a paragraph that describes both Raymond and Isarn, the Codex relates that "This [man] married Giniguentes, daughter of Aznat Dat," and could be referring to either father or son, though the author h
2 CONC ad left the name of Raymond's wife blank earlier in the paragraph, suggesting this statement refers to Isarn. Isarn had a documented wife, Senegundia, perhaps representing the same name as Giniguentes.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
