1 OCCU Rey de Asturias entre el 2 de febrero 850  y el 27 de mayo 866)     Roi, des Asturies, de Galice, Rey de León, Rey de Léon, Rey de Astúrias (850-866), King of Asturias, León & Galicia, 11.º  Rei de Oviedo e da Galiza.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ordoño I de Asturias
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordo%C3%B1o_I_de_Asturias
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño I de Asturias (Oviedo, 821 — Íb., 866). Rey de Asturias entre los años 850 y 866. Fue hijo del rey Ramiro I de Asturias, y padre de Alfonso III el Magno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrajo matrimonio con Nuña, de quien se desconocen sus orígenes familiares. Fruto de su matrimonio nacieron, por lo menos, seis hijos:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Alfonso III el Magno (c.848-910). Rey de Asturias. Heredó el trono de Asturias a la muerte de su padre y contrajo matrimonio con Jimena de Asturias, con quien tuvo descendencia. Fue sepultado en el Panteón de reyes de la Catedr
2 CONC al de Oviedo.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Vermudo. Cuando su hermano Alfonso III subió al trono, se rebeló contra él, y fue el único de los hermanos del rey que se libró de ser cegado, debido al hecho de que buscó refugio entre los musulmanes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Nuño. Se rebeló junto con sus hermanos contra Alfonso III el Magno, y éste último ordenó cegarlo.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Fruela. Se rebeló junto con sus hermanos contra Alfonso III el Magno, y éste último ordenó cegarlo.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Odoario. Se rebeló junto con sus hermanos contra Alfonso III el Magno, y éste último ordenó cegarlo.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Leodegundia, casada con un infante de la casa real de Pamplona, posiblemente García Íñiguez de Pamplona.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño I (Ordoño of the Beni Alfons), 831–27 May 866) became the King of Asturias in 850. He was born in Oviedo, where he spent his early life in the court of Alfonso II. He was probably associated with the crown from an early age
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT When just nine years old, he moved to Lugo, capital of the province of Galicia, of which his father, Ramiro, had been named governor. There he was educated, even in the military arts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño was named governor of Galicia when his father went off to Vardulias to marry Paterna (his second wife). While away, Alfonso died and Ramiro was proclaimed king. However, Ramiro still away, the impatient nobles elected Count
2 CONC  Nepocian in opposition. Ordoño immediately began to raise an army to assist his father in putting down the usurper. He could not leave his post in Galici to help, however, and his army went unused. When his father finally prevail
2 CONC ed, he confirmed Ordoño in his heretofore provisional position.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 847, at the age of 26, Ordoño married Muniadona, probably a sister of the count of Bierzo. She was to give him six children, including his successor, Alfonso III.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 1, 850, Ordoño succeeded his father as king. He was the first king of Asturias to ascend the throne without election, he was the heir of his father. His first confrontation was with the Basques, who rebelled with the su
2 CONC pport of the Banu Qasi of Zaragoza. While returning to Oviedo after defeating the rebels, he received news of an impending Moorish assault on Vardulias. Before the Moors could attack, he met them near the Ebro and defeated them. T
2 CONC hese victories meant little in the long run, for the governor of Zaragoza, Musa ibn Musa fortified the city of Albaida (Albelda). In 859, Ordoño defeated him at Albelda and besieged and razed the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT He supported the mozarab rebels, which cost him at Guadacelete in 854. But this fiasco only incited him to consolidate the "Desert of the Duero", the depopulated region between the Asturian cordillera and the river Duero. He direc
2 CONC ted the Repoblación, by which were repopulated the towns of León, Astorga, Tui, and Amaya.
2 CONT
2 CONT He intended to advance against the governor of Tudela and thus control all access to the Navarre and the Basque Country, but the Emirate of Córdoba responded with an invasion of Álava and a sack of the eponymous city. At Bureba, t
2 CONC he Arabs defeated Rodrigo, first count of Castile, and set back the Reconquista for years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño died in Oviedo and was succeeded by his eldest son.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño I (Arabic: أردون بن إذفنش‎ (Urdūn ibn Iḍfunš : Ordoño of the Beni Alfons), 831–27 May 866) became the King of Asturias in 850. He was born in Oviedo, where he spent his early life in the court of Alfonso II. He was probably
2 CONC  associated with the crown from an early age.
2 CONT
2 CONT When just nine years old, he moved to Lugo, capital of the province of Galicia, of which his father, Ramiro, had been named governor. There he was educated, even in the military arts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño was named governor of Galicia when his father went off to Vardulias to marry Paterna (his second wife). While away, Alfonso died and Ramiro was proclaimed king. However, Ramiro still away, the impatient nobles elected Count
2 CONC  Nepocian in opposition. Ordoño immediately began to raise an army to assist his father in putting down the usurper. He could not leave his post in Galici to help, however, and his army went unused. When his father finally prevail
2 CONC ed, he confirmed Ordoño in his heretofore provisional position.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 847, at the age of 26, Ordoño married Muniadona, probably a sister of the count of Bierzo. She was to give him six children, including his successor, Alfonso III.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 1, 850, Ordoño succeeded his father as king. He was the first king of Asturias to ascend the throne without election, he was the heir of his father. His first confrontation was with the Basques, who rebelled with the su
2 CONC pport of the Banu Qasi of Zaragoza. While returning to Oviedo after defeating the rebels, he received news of an impending Moorish assault on Vardulias. Before the Moors could attack, he met them near the Ebro and defeated them. T
2 CONC hese victories meant little in the long run, for the governor of Zaragoza, Musa ibn Musa fortified the city of Albaida (Albelda). In 859, Ordoño defeated him at Albelda and besieged and razed the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT He supported the mozarab rebels, which cost him at Guadacelete in 854. But this fiasco only incited him to consolidate the "Desert of the Duero", the depopulated region between the Asturian cordillera and the river Duero. He direc
2 CONC ted the Repoblación, by which were repopulated the towns of León, Astorga, Tui, and Amaya.
2 CONT
2 CONT He intended to advance against the governor of Tudela and thus control all access to the Navarre and the Basque Country, but the Emirate of Córdoba responded with an invasion of Álava and a sack of the eponymous city. At Bureba, t
2 CONC he Arabs defeated Rodrigo, first count of Castile, and set back the Reconquista for years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ordoño died in Oviedo and was succeeded by his eldest son.
2 CONT
