2 CONT Berengar Raymond I the Crooked, also called the Hunchback (in Catalan, Berenguer Ramon I el Corbat; and in Spanish, Berenguer Ramón I el Corvado or el Curvo) (1005-26 May 1035) was the count of Barcelona, Girona, and Ausona from 1
2 CONC 018 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Raymond Borrell, count of Barcelona, Girona, and Ausona and his wife Ermesinde of Carcassonne. He accepted the suzerainty of Sancho the Great of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1021, he married Sancha Sánchez, daughter of Sancho I Garcés, count of Castile, with whom he had two sons: his successor, Raymond Berengar (1023), and a son Sancho. In 1027, he married secondly Guisla of Lluca, with whom he had
2 CONC  a son, William in 1028. Two daughters have also been tentatively assigned to this couple, Clemencia who married Ermengol III and a Beatrix of Barcelona, married to Henry of Burgundy, an ancestor of the kings of Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengar Raymond as a historical figure is enigmatic, shrouded in incomprehensible contradictions and ambiguities. First, he was a man of peace, and throughout his reign peace ruled. He pacified his neighbours as well, bringing to
2 CONC  heel the count of Urgell, Ermengol II. He reestablished amicable relations with Hugh I, count of Empúries and maintained them with William I of Besalú and Wilfred II of Cerdagne. He was a son of the church who maintained relation
2 CONC s with the papacy and pilgrimaged to Rome in 1032. On many occasions he travelled to Zaragoza and Navarre to discuss with Sancho III the Great, king of Navarre their mutual stance against the counts of Toulouse. His confidantes an
2 CONC d councillors were the Abbot Oliva, the judge Ponç Bofill, Gombau de Besora, and the bishops Pedro of Girona y Deudado of Barcelona. In 1025, he decreed that the proprietors of entails (men holding land in fee tail) were free from
2 CONC  taxation.
