1 NOTE Rey de Navarra (1035-1054)
1 NOTE García Sánchez III, sometimes García III, IV, V, or VI (also García of Nájera, from Spanish: García el de Nájera, 1016-1054), was king of Navarre from 1035 to 1054. He was the eldest legitimate son and heir of Sancho the Gr
2 CONC eat, born November 1016, and he succeeded his father to the crown of Navarre, becoming feudal overlord over two of his brothers: Ramiro, who was given lands that would serve as the basis for the kingdom of Aragón; and Gonzalo, wh
2 CONC o received the counties of Sobrarbe and Ribagorza. Likewise, he had some claim to suzerainty over brother Ferdinand, who under their father had served as Count of Castile, nominally subject to the Kingdom of León but brought unde
2 CONC r the personal control of Sancho III.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1037, Ferdinand requested García's aid against his brother-in-law, Bermudo III of León, in battle near Pisuerga. The two brothers defeated Bermudo, who died in battle, the final king of the male line of Pedro de Cantabria, an
2 CONC d Ferdinand succeeded in León.
2 CONT
2 CONT By aiding Ferdinand, García received his brother's favour and, in a repartition of Castile, he expanded Navarre to the bay of Santander, incorporating the entire Basque Country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon he was confronted by his brother Ramiro at Tafalla (1043) and defeated him, but this victory resulted in the effective independence of Ramiro.
2 CONT
2 CONT García was one of the Christian kings to profit greatly from the weakened taifa kingdoms that arose through the disintegration of central control by the Caliphate of Córdoba. In 1045, he conquered Calahorra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations eventually soured with Ferdinand and war broke out between the fraternal kingdoms, García dying in the Battle of Atapuerca, 15 September, 1054.
2 CONT
2 CONT His nickname comes from his foundation of the monastery of Santa María la Real of Najera.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT He was married, in 1038, to Estefanía, possibly daughter of Ramon Borrell, Count of Barcelona (her dowry was the Cameros), and they produced nine children (four sons, five daughters):
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho "El de Peñalén", king of Navarre, married Placencia 
2 CONT Ramiro (d.1083), lord of Calahorra, married Teresa, daughter of count Gonzalo Salvadórez de Lara[citation needed] 
2 CONT Ferdinand, lord of Bucesta, married Nuña de Vizcaya 
2 CONT Raymond the Fraticide (Ramón el Fratricida), lord of Murillo and Cameros 
2 CONT Ermesinda, married Fortún Sánchez de Yarnoz 
2 CONT Mayor, married Guy II of Masón. 
2 CONT Urraca (d.1108), married Castilian count García Ordóñez 
2 CONT Jimena 
2 CONT Mencia (d.1106), married Lope de Nájera 
