1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Aubry was the son of Liétaud II de Mâcon, comte de Bourgogne, comte de Mâcon, and his third wife Richilde/Richeut d'Autun. In 966 he succeeded his father as comte de Mâcon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About 970 Aubry married Ermentrud/Irmgard de Roucy, daughter of Ragenold/Renaud, comte de Roucy, and Alverade de Lorraine
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * _ de Macon b: ABT 970 in Macon, Saone-et-Loire, Bourgogne, Franc
2 CONT
2 CONT ===notes
2 CONT
2 CONT By hypothesizing a marriage between Eudes of Poitou and a daughter of Aubri II and Ermentrude, Mathieu brings together the various names, Ebles, Eudes and Letaud, with the various titles, Rumigny, Coucy, Reims, and most importantl
2 CONC y Roucy. (It would also recapitulate a common pattern, in which brothers William and Ebles of Poitou married half-sisters daughters of Ermentrude.) The solution is not without problems - most importantly, Ebles and his wife Beatri
2 CONC ce of Hainaut would both be great-grandchildren of William I (III) (assuming the correct identification of Adelaide, wife of Hugh Capet). Mathieu suggests that this marriage, which produced just two daughters, may have been ended 
2 CONC in premature separation due to consanginity.
2 CONT
2 CONT That, then, is the Mathieu hypothesis.
2 CONT
2 CONT ——-
2 CONT * http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00036198&tree=LEO (does not list her)
