2 CONT Parents unknown.  Seen as daughter of https://www.geni.com/people/Evancio/6000000173588306156?through=6000000000038164697
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Recciberga, queen consort of the Visigothic Kingdom
2 CONT * Predecessor	Theodora
2 CONT * Successor	Liubigoton
2 CONT * Died	between 18 October 646 and 13 November 657
2 CONT * Burial: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Rom%C3%A1n_de_Hornija San Roman de Ornija]
2 CONT * Spouse: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chindasuinth Chindasuinth] 
2 CONT * Children: 1) Recceswinth 2) Theodofred
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recciberga '''Recciberga''']  (also spelled: Reciberga or Reciverga, Latin: Rekiverga; died between October 18, 646 and November 13, 657) was Queen of the Visigoths (not later than 646 - not later th
2 CONC an 657) by marriage to Chindasuinth or Recceswinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Her existence is confirmed by two sources. The first is a royal charter.[1] dated 646, and the second is her epitaph[2][3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Recciberga's lineage is not mentioned in any of the documents. She could have been from an important Visigoth noble family, and she could have been possibly related to Rikimir.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 18, 646 King Chindasuinth issued a charter to the abbot of a monastery near El Bierzo, mentioning Recciberga as a queen.[1] There is still some dispute between medievalists as to the authenticity of this document, thoug
2 CONC h. The charter appears as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT >“Domnis sanctis gloriosissimis mihique post Deum fortissimis patronis sanctorum martyrum Iusti et Pastoris sive sancte Marie et sancti Martini episcopi, quorum basilica, vel monasterium situm est juxta rivulum quod dicitur Molina
2 CONC , sub monte Irago, in confinio Vergidensi, et est fundamentum ipsum monasterium a tibi Fructuoso Abbate , ego Chindasvintux rex and Reciverga regina"
2 CONT
2 CONT Her epitaph, written by Archbishop Eugene of Toledo, records her as having been married to the king for seven years.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no current consensus among historians about which of the rulers of the Visigothic kingdom was married to Recciberga. Some medievalists consider her the wife of King Chindasuinth,[2] who ruled the Visigoths in 642-653, and
2 CONC  suggest that Recciberga was the mother of his eldest son and heir, Recceswinth. Other historians call her the wife of Recceswinth. They believe that the first mention of her as a queen dates back to the time when her husband was 
2 CONC co-ruler with his father, and also had a royal title. In this case, Recciberga should have died long before her husband, who died on September 1, 672. There is no evidence of the married life of King Recceswinth in medieval source
2 CONC s, and as so it is assumed he died childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died abruptly at the age of twenty-two years and eight months,[2][4] according to her epitaph. The exact date of her death is unknown,[5] but it is assumed that she should have died no earlier than 646, when she was mentioned 
2 CONC in the charter, and no later than November 13, 657, the date of the death of Eugene of Toledo.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT She was buried in the monastery of San Roman de Ornija, located in the village of the same name . Later, King Chindasuinth was also buried there, his epitaph written by the same Archbishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm#_ftnref288 "'''SPAIN: VANDALS, SUEVI & VISIGOTHS'''"]. fmg.ac. Retrieved 2023-01-12
2 CONT
2 CONT CHINDASVINTO (-Toledo 1 Oct 653).  He was elected to succeed in 642 as CHINDASVINTO King of the Visigoths after deposing King Tulga … m '''REKIBERGA,''' daughter of --- (-after 18 Oct 646).  "Chindasvintux rex et Reciverga regina"
2 CONC  donated property to "monasterium…iuxta rivulum…Molina sub monte Irago" by charter dated 18 Oct 646[296].  Nothing is known about the family origin of King Chindasvinto´s wife, or whether this named wife was the mother of both his
2 CONC  children who are named below.  The estimated birth date of the king´s son Theodefredo suggests that he may have been the son of Rekiberga.  
2 CONT
2 CONT King Chindasvinto and [his wife] had two children:
2 CONT *a)         RECESVINTO (-Villa of Gérticos [Wamba, Valladolid][297] 1 Sep 672, bur [Gerticos], transferred 13th century to Toledo[298]).  Fredegar names "Chyntasindus…filium suum Richysindum"[299].  His father associated him as jo
2 CONC int king, and abdicated in his favour in 649 whereupon he was elected to succeed as RECESVINTO King of the Visigoths.  
2 CONT *b)         THEODOFREDO ([645/53]-after 702).  The Chronicle of Alfonso III names "Theodefred…the son of King Chindasuinth, his father abandoning him at a young age", adding that King Egica ordered him to be blinded when he reache
2 CONC d maturity[306], presumably aiming to exclude him from the succession in line with the practice in the Eastern Empire, where blindness was considered an impediment to ruling and therefore constituted an effective way of disempower
2 CONC ing an opponent short of killing him (although many did succumb as a result of the treatment).  It is not recorded whether the punishment was carried out.  The same source records that Theodofredo settled in Córdoba.  The Chronicl
2 CONC e of Alfonso III records that he was murdered by Witiza King of the Visigoths[307].  m (after 687) RICILO, daughter of ---.  The Chronicle of Alfonso III records that Theodefredo "chose a wife…Ricilo, from a noble family" during h
2 CONC is exile[308].  Theodofredo & his wife had [four] children:  …
2 CONT
