2 CONT ===Chindasuinth, King of the Visigoths
2 CONT * Reign	17 April 642 – 30 September 653
2 CONT * Predecessor	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulga Tulga]
2 CONT * Successor	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recceswinth Recceswinth]
2 CONT * Co-king	Recceswinth (20 January 649 – 30 September 653)
2 CONT * Born	c. 563
2 CONT * Died	September 30, 653 (aged 89–90)
2 CONT * Burial	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/San_Rom%C3%A1n_de_Hornija Monastery of San Román de Hornija] 
2 CONT * Wife	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recciberga Recciberga]
2 CONT * Religion	[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalcedonian_Christianity Chalcedonian Christianity]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chindasuinth '''Chindasuinth'''] (also spelled Chindaswinth, Chindaswind, Chindasuinto, Chindasvindo, or Khindaswinth (Latin: Chintasvintus, Cindasvintus; c. 563 – 30 September 653) was Visigothic Ki
2 CONC ng of Hispania, from 642 until his death in 653. He succeeded Tulga, from whom he took the throne in a coup. He was elected by the nobles and anointed by the bishops on April 30, 642.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Edward Gibbon, during his reign, Muslim raiders began harassing Iberia: "As early as the time of Othman (644–656), their piratical squadrons had ravaged the coast of Andalusia".[2] However, this reading poses a proble
2 CONC m difficult to overcome: the Muslim Rashiduns were still struggling to conquer Tripolitania in present-day Libya. Chindasuinth spent the last years of his life, as so many mediaeval monarchs did, in acts of piety for the sake of h
2 CONC is immortal soul. He commissioned St Fructuosus to build the monastery of San Román de Hornija on the Douro, with the intention of having it house his tomb. His remains rest there next to those of his wife, '''''Riciberga'''''. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, Eugene II, bishop of Toledo, provided a judgment on the life of this king by writing the following inscription:
2 CONT
2 CONT I, Chindasuinth, ever the friend of evil deeds: committer of crimes Chindaswinth I, impious, obscene, ugly and wicked; not seeking the best, valuing the worst.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Chindasuinth was succeeded by his eldest son, '''''Recceswinth''''', who continued his reforms. A younger son, ''''Theodofred'''', was blinded by Wamba.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SPAIN: VANDALS, SUEVI & VISIGOTHS'''". [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/VANDALS,%20SUEVI,%20VISIGOTHS.htm#_ftnref288 fmg.ac.] Retrieved 2023-01-11
2 CONT
2 CONT *1.         [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chindasuinth CHINDASVINTO] (-Toledo 1 Oct 653).  He was elected to succeed in 642 as CHINDASVINTO King of the Visigoths after deposing King Tulga, an act which was condoned by the seventh
2 CONC  council of Toledo convened by the new king specially for the purpose. …  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Chindusvinthus” reigned alone for six years, eight months and eleven days, and with “filio suo Reccesvintho” 
2 CONC four years, eight months and eleven days, and died “pridie Kal Oct 691”[295].  m [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recciberga REKIBERGA], daughter of --- (-after 18 Oct 646).  "Chindasvintux rex et Reciverga regina" donated property 
2 CONC to "monasterium…iuxta rivulum…Molina sub monte Irago" by charter dated 18 Oct 646[296].  Nothing is known about the family origin of King Chindasvinto´s wife, or whether this named wife was the mother of both his children who are 
2 CONC named below.  The estimated birth date of the king’s son Theodefredo suggests that he may have been the son of Rekiberga.  King Chindasvinto and [his wife] had two children:
2 CONT ** a)         RECESVINTO (-Villa of Gerticos [Wamba, Valladolid][297] 1 Sep 672, bur [Gérticos], transferred 13th century to Toledo[298]).  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Chindusvinthus” reigned with “filio suo Rec
2 CONC cesvintho” four years, eight months and eleven days, and that Recesvinto ruled alone 23 years, seven months and eleven days, and died “Kal Sep A.D. 672”[305]. 
2 CONT **b)         THEODOFREDO ([645/53]-after 702).  The Chronicle of Alfonso III names "Theodefred…the son of King Chindasuinth, his father abandoning him at a young age", adding that King Egica ordered him to be blinded when he reach
2 CONC ed maturity[306], m (after 687) RICILO, daughter of ---.  The Chronicle of Alfonso III records that Theodefredo "chose a wife…'''Ricilo''', from a noble family" during his exile[308].  Theodofredo & his wife had [four] children: …
2 CONT
