2 CONT '''''Issue'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Urraca''''' '''''Sancho Alfónsez''''' '''''Infanta Sancha''''' '''''Elvira, Queen of Sicily''''' '''''Elvira, Countess of Toulouse '''''Theresa, Countess of Portugal'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''House'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''House of Jiménez''''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Father: Ferdinand I of León and Castile'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Mother: Sancha of León'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Alfonso VI nicknamed the Brave (El Bravo) or the Valiant, was King of León from 1065, King of Castile and de facto King of Galicia from 1072. After the conquest of Toledo he also self-proclaimed victoriosissimo rege in Toleto
2 CONC , et in Hispania et Gallecia Much romance has gathered around his name.'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Family'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Alfonso was the second son of Ferdinand the Great and Sancha of León, the daughter of Alfonso the Noble and sister to Bermudo III of León. His father was King of Castile-León and Emperor of Spain. Alfonso was allotted León wh
2 CONC en the kingdom was divided following his father's death. Castile was given to his elder brother Sancho and Galicia to his younger brother García.'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Accession'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Political situation in the Northern Iberian Peninsula around 1065:'''''  '''''Garcia II´s domains (Galicia)''''' '''''Badajoz, owing tribute to Garcia''''' '''''Seville, owing tribute to Garcia''''' '''''Alfonso VI´s domains 
2 CONC (León)'''''  '''''Toledo, owing tribute to Alfonso''''' '''''Sancho II´s domains (Castile)''''' '''''Zaragoza, owing tribute to Sancho'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''As the middle of three sons of King Ferdinand I of León and Sancha of León, Alfonso was allotted León when the kingdom was divided following his father's death, while Castile was given to his elder brother Sancho, Galicia to 
2 CONC younger brother García, and sisters Urraca and Elvira given the cities of Zamora and Toro respectively. Each of the brothers was also assigned a sphere of influence among the Taifa states. Alfonso appears to have taken the first s
2 CONC tep in violating this division, in 1068 invading the Galician client Taifa of Badajoz and extorting tribute. In response, Sancho attacked and defeated Alfonso at Llantada but three years later in 1071 they joined forces against Ga
2 CONC rcía. Sancho over-marched Alfonso's León to conquer García's northern lands, while Alfonso himself is found issuing charters in the southern part of the Galician realm. García fled to taifa Seville, and the remaining brothers then
2 CONC  turned on each other. This conflict culminated in the Battle of Golpejera in early January, 1072. Sancho proved victorious and Alfonso himself was forced to flee to his client Taifa of Toledo. Later that year as Sancho was moppin
2 CONC g up the last of the resistance, besieging his sister Urraca at Zamora in October, he was assassinated, opening the way for Alfonso to return to claim Sancho's crown. García, induced to return from exile, was imprisoned by Alfonso
2 CONC  for life, leaving Alfonso in uncontested control of the reunited territories of their father. In recognition of this and his role as the preeminent Christian monarch on the peninsula, in 1077 Alfonso proclaimed himself "Emperor o
2 CONC f all Spain".'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''In the cantar de gesta The Lay of the Cid, he plays the part attributed by medieval poets to the greatest kings, and to Charlemagne himself. He is alternately the oppressor and the victim of heroic and self-willed nobles — th
2 CONC e idealized types of the patrons for whom the jongleurs and troubadours sang. He is the hero of a cantar de gesta which, like all but a very few of the early Spanish songs, like the cantar of Bernardo del Carpio and the Infantes o
2 CONC f Lara, exists now only in the fragments incorporated in the chronicle of Alfonso the Wise or in ballad form.'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''His flight from the monastery of Sahagún (Safagún in Leonese language), where his brother Sancho endeavoured to imprison him, his chivalrous friendship for his host Almamun of Toledo, caballero aunque moro, "a knight although
2 CONC  a Moor", the passionate loyalty of his vassal, Pero (Pedro) Ansúrez, and his brotherly love for his sister Urraca of Zamora, may owe something to the poet who took him as a hero.'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''They are the answer to the poet of the nobles who represented the king as having submitted to taking a degrading oath at the hands of Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar (El Cid) to deny intervention in his brother's death in the church of
2 CONC  Santa Gadea at Burgos, and as having then persecuted the brave man who defied him.'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''Strong fighter'''''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''''When every allowance is made, Alfonso VI stands out as a strong man fighting as a king whose interest was law and order, and who wa
