1 NAME Bernard-Aton III /Trencavel/ Vicomte d'Albi, de Razès, de Béziers & de Nîmes
2 GIVN Bernard-Aton III
2 SURN Trencavel
2 NSFX Vicomte d'Albi, de Razès, de Béziers & de Nîmes
2 _MARNM Trencavel
2 NICK Bernard-Aton d'Albi
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1010
2 PLAC Nimes, Gard, Languedoc, France
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1057
2 PLAC Albi, Tarn, Languedoc, France
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Comte, d'Albi, Vicomte d'Albi et de Nîmes
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~havens5/p35790.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Vicomte Bernard-Aton III of Trencavel Vicomte of Nimes1
2 CONT
2 CONT b. circa 1010, d. circa 1060, #35790
2 CONT
2 CONT Pop-up Pedigree
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	  	Vicomte Aton II Trencavel de Nimes1 b. before 975
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth* 	circa 1010 	Bernard-Aton III of was born circa 1010 at Nimes, Gard, Languedoc, France.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage* 	circa 1032 	Vicomte Bernard-Aton III of Trencavel Vicomte of Nimes married Rangearde de la Marche, daughter of Bernard I Comte Marche and Amelie de Rasez, circa 1032.1  
2 CONT
2 CONT Death* 	circa 1060 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernard-Aton III of died circa 1060 at Albi, at Tarn, at Languedoc at France.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 	  	Rangearde de la Marche b. circa 1017, d. before 1069
2 CONT
2 CONT Children  	1. 	Rangearde Trencavel+ b. c 1033, d. 10771
2 CONT
2 CONT   	2. 	Raimond Bernard II of Trencavel Vicomte of Nimes+ b. c 1036, d. 1074
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trencavel
2 CONT
2 CONT and in French:  http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maison_Trencavel
2 CONT
2 CONT The Trencavel were an important noble family in Languedoc  during the 10th through 13th centuries. The name "Trencavel," originally a nickname and later a family name, may derive from the Occitan words for "nutcracker" (trenca ave
2 CONC lana). The name was traditionally restricted in actual use only to those family members named Raymond, but the last Trencavel viscount, Raymond II, preferred the surname over his given name and adopted it for his charters.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The first well-known member of the family was Ato I, viscount of Albi in the early tenth century. He was followed by five generations of viscounts of Albi in direct father-to-son descent. During this same period the family came to
2 CONC  dominate the episcopacy of Languedoc. Each of the viscounts from Ato II on had a younger brother named Frotarius (or Frothaire) who was a bishop, be it of Albi, Cahors, or Nîmes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1069 the three daughters of Peter II of Carcassonne sold their rights to the Counties of Carcassonne and Razès and the Béziers and Agde to Raymond Berengar I of Barcelona for 4,000 mancusos. By marriage to Ermengard, daughter o
2 CONC f Peter II, Raymond Bernard, son of Bernard Ato III, became viscount of Carcassonne, having already acquired Nîmes. By 1070 he was viscount of Béziers. His son Bernard Ato IV was viscount of Albi, Béziers, Carcassonne, Nîmes, and 
2 CONC Razès. He thus held all the lands of the counts of Carcassonne, but never assumed the comital title. Bernard Ato was formally proclaimed viscount after the death of his mother in 1101.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Bernard Ato IV divided their inheritance. The eldest, Roger I, took Albi, Carcassonne, and Razès, but had no children.[2] The second, Raymond I took Béziers and Agde.[2] The youngest, Bernard Ato V, inherited Nîmes and
2 CONC  married Guilhelma, daughter of William VI of Montpellier. In 1132 Roger and Raymond agreed that on the event of Roger's death without heirs, Carcassonne would pass to Raymond.[2] In 1150 Roger died and his three viscounties all p
2 CONC assed to Raymond. After a series of disputes, the viscounty of Agde was divided between Raymond and Bernard Ato, with the latter holding the title.[2] The elder branch of Béziers-Albi-Carcassonne-Razès and the younger of Nîmes-Agd
2 CONC e were to remain separate for the remainder of the family's existence. The practical capital of the elder branch was Béziers.
2 CONT
2 CONT During this period there was considerable urban unrest as the growing cities tried to assert their independence. Raymond I was killed during one such revolt in Béziers. There was also revolts in Carcassonne in 1107 and 1120–1124, 
2 CONC during which four years the Trencavel were expelled from the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their complex of lands in the centre of Languedoc gave the Trencavel considerable power in the eleventh and twelfth centuries. The counts of Barcelona and Toulouse both had large territories to the east and west, and valued a pote
2 CONC ntial alliance with a family that stood in the middle. For the most part the Trencavels allied with Barcelona against Toulouse. But as a result of the Albigensian Crusade, the last Trencavels lost their lands and titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger II, son of Raymond I, inherited his father's four viscounties. His son, Raymond Roger also held them, and was captured after the fall of Carcassonne to the Crusaders. He died in prison at the end of 1209, a watershed for Lan
2 CONC guedoc. Meanwhile, Bernard Ato VI, son of Bernard Ato V, ceded his rights as viscount of Nîmes and Agde to the Crusading leader Simon de Montfort in 1214. Raymond Roger's son, Raymond II, formally ceded his titles in 1210, though 
2 CONC he reclaimed Carcassonne in 1224, only to lose it to Louis VIII of France in 1226. He continued to call himself viscount until 1247, when he once again formally ceded his rights, this time to Louis IX, and symbolically broke his v
2 CONC icecomital seals, after several failed attempts to recover his patrimony. He and his sons are the last known Trencavels, and they ruled only the castle of Limoux.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] List of ruling members
