1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancho_Sánchez
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Sánchez (fl. 1075–1127) was an important magnate of the Kingdom of Aragon in the late 11th and early 12th centuries, during the reigns of Sancho Ramírez, Peter I and Alfonso I. He was governor of the important Navarrese ten
2 CONC ancies of Erro (from 1080), the castle of San Esteban de Deyo (1084), the capital city of Pamplona (1092), Aibar and Tafalla (1098) and Falces and Leguín (1112). In Aragon proper, he governed the important fortress of El Castellar
2 CONC  overlooking Muslim Zaragoza from 1091 and the town of Ejea from 1113.[1] He held the rank of count (Latin comes) from 1085, before that he was a lord (senior).[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Historia Roderici, he was captured in the battle of Morella on 14 August 1084 by the forces of Yusuf al-Mu'taman ibn Hud, king of Zaragoza, and Rodrigo Díaz de Vivar.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1087 and 1113 he was responsible for the County of Navarre, the interior of the old Kingdom of Pamplona which had passed to King Sancho upon Pamplona's division in 1076. Nonetheless, Sancho Ramírez had done homage for Nava
2 CONC rre to King Alfonso VI of Castile and so Sancho Sánchez acted in fact as Alfonso's vassal in Navarre.[1] The latter is never referred to as Count of Navarre in royal documents, but only in documents of local origin. This is perhap
2 CONC s a sign that the status of Navarre proper between Aragon and Castile was not completely settled.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1092, Sancho was still lord of San Esteban de Deyo, but by October he had been replaced by the bishop of Pamplona, Pedro de Roda.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 14th-century Chronicle of San Juan de la Peña, in 1094 the inhabitants of Huesca in the taifa of Zaragoza, threatened by Sancho Ramírez, asked Alfonso VI for assistance in return for tribute. In response, Alfonso 
2 CONC "sent Count Sancho with all of his forces to their aid, and the Castilian army moved up to Vitoria. As soon as the king of Aragon learned of this, he marched against the count with his men and his sons Pedro and Alfonso. The count
2 CONC  did not expect him and was forced back into Castile."[7] Francisco Bautista suggests that this episode would be better located in 1090 or in 1091, when Sancho Ramírez was building the castle of El Castellar between April and May.
2 CONC [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho made donations to the cathedral of Pamplona (between 1101 and 1104) and the Order of Saint John of Jerusalem.[9] He appears in a contemporary document as count of Erro in 1113.[2] In 1127, he issued a carta de arras for his
2 CONC  wife, Elvira. This is his last appearance in the record and he probably died not long after.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT Sancho was the son of Sancho Macerátiz, lord of Álava and Oca, and his wife, Andregoto.[11] She was distantly related to the royal family, referring in a charter to avuncula mea regina domna Endrigoto (my [great-]aunt Queen Andreg
2 CONC oto).[12] From the time of José de Moret, writing in the 18th century, Sancho "Macerátiz" (a Basque nickname referring to physical deformity) has been erroneously identified with Sancho Garcés, an illegitimate son of King García I
2 CONC II of Pamplona,[1][11][13][14][15] thereby making Sancho Sánchez and his wife first cousins, but chronology proves the two to have been distinct men.[16] An alternate theory once proposed was that Sancho Sánchez was a son of Sanch
2 CONC o Fortuñones and brother of Fortún Sánchez, husband of Ermesenda, daughter of García III.[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho was brother-in-law of the Castilian count Gonzalo Salvadórez who married his sister Sancha, while his nephew, their son Gómez González, became the premier nobleman of queen Urraca of León and by tradition her lover.[18]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho's first marriage or marriages are unrecorded; they are known only from the existence of legitimate children not by his only known wife. His only known wife was Elvira, the second daughter of Count García Ordóñez and his wif
2 CONC e, Urraca, daughter of King García III. They were married by 1094, when a charter refers to Sancho as a son-in-law of García Ordóñez.[19] Sancho had a legitimate son, Gil Sánchez, and a daughter, María Sánchez, who married Count D
2 CONC iego López I de Haro.[9] Sancho had eight other illegitimate children: Ramiro, Fernando, Andregoto, Sancha (Sancia de Aizuin), Sancha (Sanza d'Estaldun), Fortún, García and Fernando (Ferrando de Estaldum). All ten of his known son
2 CONC s and daughters shared in his inheritance.[10] There were probably other children who died before Sancho and are not recorded in the limited surviving records.[20]
