1 NOTE {geni:about_me} - https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berta_di_Savoia_(1075-1111)_
2 CONT -----
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SAVOY.htm#Berthediedbefore1111<br/>
2 CONT  Comte Pierre I & his wife had [three] children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         AGNES de Savoie ([1066/70]-after 13 Mar 1110)... ...
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         [ALIX de Savoie (-after 21 Dec 1099)... ...
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         ['''BERTHE''' ([1075]-before 1111).  Szabolcs de Vajay[102] suggests that Berta, second wife of Pedro I King of Aragon, was the daughter of Comte Pierre, although there appears to be no direct proof that this is correct
2 CONC .  He further suggests that, when Emperor Heinrich IV captured Tuscany in 1092, Berthe could have taken refuge at her mother's court at Poitiers from where her marriage was arranged, her future husband's first wife having been her
2 CONC  mother's first cousin.  Zurita who, while in relation to Queen Berta stating that “no se escrive cuya hija fuesse”, comments that “en las historias de Castilla...el Rey Don Alonso que ganó a Toledo tuvo...una [muger] llamada Bert
2 CONC a que era natural de Toscana” [third wife of Alfonso VI King of Castile, see the document CASTILE KINGS for the primary sources on which this statement is based], that “el Emperador Henrico avia casado con Berta hija de Otho Marqu
2 CONC es de Italia”, and that “parece verisimil que esta Reyna Berta sucediesse de aquella casa de los Marqueses de Italia”[103].  Berta is named in charters in Aragon from 16 Aug 1097 to 1105[104].  “Berta...regina” donated “tres campo
2 CONC s...et uno orto” in Huesca to “don Bruno mea magistro” by charter dated Jul [1101][105].  Ubieto Arteta points out that “Bruno” was “[un] nombre completamente desconocido en la omomástica aragonesa del siglo XI e importado indudab
2 CONC lemente de Italia o de Francia” and suggesting that this could suggest an Italian origin for Queen Berta, although recognising that “el argumento...apenas tiene fuerza, pues se da el nombre de Bruno en el Midi y en las poblaciones
2 CONC  de francos”[106].  Szabolcs de Vajay identifies the grantee Bruno as the son of Friedrich Graf, Herr von Lützelburg (see the document BAR) and his wife Agnes de Savoie (who was recorded as the daughter of Pierre Comte de Savoie a
2 CONC nd therefore would have been the sister of Queen Berta if her Savoy affiliation was correct)[107].  This may appear unlikely considering that the son of Graf Friedrich cannot have been born much earlier than [1086] and therefore w
2 CONC ould have been no more than 10 years old at the most at the time of King Pedro's marriage in 1097.  Pedro I King of Aragon donated “villa...Buniales” to “abbate don Galindo”, at the request of “uxoris mee Berta regina”, by charter
2 CONC  dated Jan 1103[108].  “Berta regina” granted “in Sangarren...kasas que fuerunt domni Hormat Ibn Motert Ibn Alabe” to “Enneco Banzones” by charter dated 1105, the dating clause of which records “cognatum meum rege domni Antefonso,
2 CONC  me sub gratia domnum meum rege domno Petro...et cum amore...cognatum meum in Auguero et in Moriello, in Arrigolis et in Merquerlo, in Aiierve et in Sangarren et in Kalgen” [Agüero, Murillo, Riglos, Marcuello, Ayerbe, Sangarren an
2 CONC d Callén][109].  “Berta regina” donated “almunia...inter Berbegal et Monte Rog” to the monastery of Alaon by charter dated to [1105], the dating clause of which records “Antefonso cognato meo in Pampilona et in Aragona, in Superar
2 CONC vi et in Ripacurcia, me autem regnante in Murello et in Auvero et in Aierb...”[110].  Ubieto Arteta refers to the areas named in these two charters as governed by Queen Berta as “el ‘reino’ que llamamos de los Mallos”, adding that
2 CONC  “la existencia del pequeño Estado...fué efimera” and that “antes de 1111...estaba a punto de desaparecer” (he does not cite the primary source on which he bases this date)[111].  Szabolcs de Vajay assumes that it disappeared when
2 CONC  the queen died[112].  Although the area is called “reino” and “Estado” by Ubieto Arteta, there appears no reason to suppose from the wording of these two charters that the territory was anything other than a señorío granted by Ki
2 CONC ng Pedro as dower for his wife, and that it was governed in the same way as any other señorío in the kingdom of Aragon.  Berta would have continued to bear the title queen after her husband´s death, and the phrase “me autem regnan
2 CONC te” in the second document should not be interpreted other than as the normal way in which administration of a territory by a queen could be described without implying that the area had become a “kingdom”.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Huesca 16 Aug 1097) as his second wife, PEDRO I King of Aragon and Navarre, son of SANCHO I Ramírez King of Aragon and Navarre & his first wife Isabel de Urgel ([1066/18 Aug 1068]-Valle de Aran 27 Sep 1104, bur Monastery 
2 CONC of San Juan de la Peña).] 
