2 CONC alada). He inherited the counties of Besalú (1111) and Cerdanya (1117) and in between married Douce, heiress of Provence (1112). His dominions then stretched as far east as Nice.
2 CONT
2 CONT In alliance with the Count of Urgell, Ramon Berenguer conquered Barbastro and Balaguer. In 1118 he captured and rebuilt Tarragona, which became the metropolitan seat of the church in Catalonia (before that, Catalans had depended e
2 CONC cclesiastically on the archbishopric of Narbonne). He also established relations with the Italian maritime republics of Pisa and Genoa and in 1114 and 1115 attacked with Pisa the then-Muslim islands of Majorca and Ibiza. They beca
2 CONC me his tributaries and many Christian slaves there were recovered and set free. Ramon Berenguer also raided mainland Muslim dependencies with Pisa's help, such as Valencia, Lleida and Tortosa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Toward the end of his life Ramon Berenguer became a Templar. He gave his five Catalonian counties to his eldest son Ramon Berenguer IV and Provence to the younger son Berenguer Ramon.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ramon Berenguer's marriages and descendants First wife, María Rodríguez de Vivar, second daughter of Cid, died ca. 1105  María, married Bernat III, Count of Besalú (d. 1111) Jimena, also known as Eixemena, married Roger III
2 CONC , Count of Foix Second wife, Almodis Third wife, Douce or Dolça de Gévaudaun, heiress of Provence, d. ca. 1127  Almodis, married Ponce de Cervera, mother of Agalbursa, who married Barisone II of Arborea Berenguela or Berengaria, b
2 CONC . 1116, d. 1149, married Alfonso VII of Castile Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, b. 1115, d. 1162 Berenguer Ramon I, Count of Provence, b. ca. 1115, d. 1144 Bernat, died young
2 CONT
