1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Bertrand of Toulouse or Bertrand of Tripoli (died 1112) was count of Toulouse, and was the first count of Tripoli to rule in Tripoli itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Raymond IV of Toulouse, and had ruled Toulouse since Raymond left on the First Crusade in 1095 - although, between 1098 and 1100, he was dispossessed by his cousin Philippa and her husband Duke William IX 
2 CONC of Aquitaine, who marched into Toulouse and captured it, before mortgaging it back to Bertrand in 1100 to fund Duke William's expedition to the Holy Land. Bertrand officially became count of Toulouse when Raymond died in 1105, and
2 CONC  in 1108 he headed to Tripoli to take control there as well. He deposed Raymond's nephew William-Jordan as nominal count of Tripoli in 1109, and with Baldwin I, king of Jerusalem, and a fleet of Genoese ships he captured Tripoli o
2 CONC n June 10.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertrand married in June 1095, Helie of Burgundy, daughter of Eudes I. Bertrand ruled in Tripoli until his death in 1112. He was succeeded by his son Pons in Tripoli, and by his brother Alphonse-Jordan in Toulouse - although Toulo
2 CONC use was at that point again lost to Philippa and William.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertrand_of_Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bertrand of Toulouse or Bertrand of Tripoli (died 1112) was count of Toulouse, and was the first count of Tripoli to rule in Tripoli itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Raymond IV of Toulouse, and had ruled Toulouse since Raymond left on the First Crusade in 1095 - although, between 1098 and 1100, he was dispossessed by his cousin Philippa and her husband Duke William IX 
2 CONC of Aquitaine, who marched into Toulouse and captured it, before mortgaging it back to Bertrand in 1100 to fund Duke William's expedition to the Holy Land. Bertrand officially became count of Toulouse when Raymond died in 1105, and
2 CONC  in 1108 he headed to Tripoli to take control there as well. He deposed Raymond's nephew William-Jordan as nominal count of Tripoli in 1109, and with Baldwin I, king of Jerusalem, and a fleet of Genoese ships he captured Tripoli o
2 CONC n June 10.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertrand married in June 1095, Helie of Burgundy, daughter of Eudes I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertrand ruled in Tripoli until his death in 1112. He was succeeded by his son Pons in Tripoli, and by his brother Alphonse-Jordan in Toulouse - although Toulouse was at that point again lost to Philippa and William.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by Raymond IV Count of Toulouse 1105—1112 Succeeded by Alphonse I  Count of Tripoli 1109—1112 Succeeded by Pons 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertrand_of_Toulouse"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bertrand of Toulouse From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Bertrand of Toulouse or Bertrand of Tripoli (died 1112) was count of Toulouse, and was the first count of Tripoli to rule in Tripoli itself. He was the eldest son of Raymond IV of Toulouse, and had ruled Toulouse since Raymond lef
2 CONC t on the First Crusade in 1095 - although, between 1098 and 1100, he was dispossessed by his cousin Philippa and her husband Duke William IX of Aquitaine, who marched into Toulouse and captured it, before mortgaging it back to Ber
2 CONC trand in 1100 to fund Duke William's expedition to the Holy Land. Bertrand officially became count of Toulouse when Raymond died in 1105, and in 1108 he headed to Tripoli to take control there as well. He deposed Raymond's nephew 
2 CONC William-Jordan as nominal count of Tripoli in 1109, and with Baldwin I, king of Jerusalem, and a fleet of Genoese ships he captured Tripoli on June 10. Bertrand married in June 1095, Helie of Burgundy, daughter of Eudes I. Bertran
2 CONC d ruled in Tripoli until his death in 1112. He was succeeded by his son Pons in Tripoli, and by his brother Alphonse-Jordan in Toulouse - although Toulouse was at that point again lost to Philippa and William.
