2 CONT Notes
2 CONT Individual Note
2 CONT También conocido como Bazco Azcon. Bacalla, Briocalla, Bachalla, Bacahalla o Bacalha de Luna (? - 1115 o 1120) fue un caballero aragonés de origen navarro, conquistador de la villa de Luna de donde tomó el apellido y fue el primer
2 CONC o del Linaje de los Luna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aparece citado en fuentes antiguas como hijo de un Lope Ferrench y de su esposa Ximena Martínez, hija de Martín Gómez y de su esposa. Se le atribuye en dichas fuentes haber participado en la reconquista de Luna, tras lo que habría
2 CONC  en premio sido nombrado su primer tenente y, según se dice en la Crónica de San Juan de la Peña, haber luchado junto a su hijos Lope Ferrench I de Luna y Gómez I de Luna en la batalla de Alcoraz el 15 de noviembre de 1096.1​ Esto
2 CONC s datos son sin embargo dudosos para la historiografía moderna, ya que desde los trabajos de Antonio Ubieto Arteta se ha podido constatar que la mayoría de los personajes mencionados no coinciden con la documentación del periodo, 
2 CONC siendo en cambio mencionados por primera vez en obras siglos posteriores a los hechos .2​
2 CONT
2 CONT Las fuentes primarias conservadas mencionan en cambio que tras la conquista de Luna por Sancho Ramírez, la localidad fue puesta bajo el merino real Banzo Azcones, al que algunos identifican como el Bacalla histórico.3​ En tal caso
2 CONC  habría permanecido en Luna durante el reinado de Sancho Ramírez y sus hijos Pedro y Alfonso, recibiendo recompensas y donaciones reales por su papel en la organización de la localidad y en la construcción de defensas en ese secto
2 CONC r de la frontera con los musulmanes
2 CONT
2 CONT Va ser un cavaller aragonès d'origen navarrès, conqueridor de la vila de Luna d'on prengué el cognom i fou primer del llinatge dels Luna. 1r Senyor de Luna.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Google translate 
2 CONT
2 CONT Also known as Bazco Azcon, Bacalla, Briocalla, Bachalla, Bacahalla, or Bacalha de Luna (? - 1115 or 1120) was an Aragonese knight of Navarrese origin, conqueror of the town of Luna, from which he took his surname, and the first of
2 CONC  the Luna lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in ancient sources as the son of Lope Ferrench and his wife Ximena Martínez, daughter of Martín Gómez and his wife. He is credited in these sources with having participated in the reconquest of Luna, after which he
2 CONC  was named its first lieutenant as a reward and, according to the Chronicle of San Juan de la Peña, with having fought alongside his sons Lope Ferrench I de Luna and Gómez I de Luna in the Battle of Alcoraz on November 15, 1096. 1
2 CONC  These data are however doubtful for modern historiography, since the work of Antonio Ubieto Arteta has shown that most of the characters mentioned do not coincide with the documentation of the period, being mentioned for the firs
2 CONC t time in works centuries after the events. 2
2 CONT
2 CONT The primary sources preserved mention, however, that after the conquest of Luna by Sancho Ramírez, the town was placed under the royal merino Banzo Azcones, whom some identify as the historical Bacalla. 3 In this case, he would ha
2 CONC ve remained in Luna during the reign of Sancho Ramírez and his sons Pedro and Alfonso. receiving royal rewards and donations for his role in organizing the town and building defenses in that sector of the border with the Muslims
2 CONT
2 CONT He will be an Aragonese cavaller of Navarrese origin, conqueror of the town of Luna with the recognition and first of the land of Luna. 1st Lord of Luna.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://luna.es/heraldica/
2 CONT Google translate 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1091, Don Bacalla participated in the conquest of the ancient Gallícolis, which was renamed Luna since, according to legend, the assault was at night and to the eyes of the Christians the population seemed to be inside the star
2 CONC  and the glare of its light frightened the defenders by making them look like a ghostly army.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, he also participated in the Battle of Alcoraz (1096), which led to the capture of Huesca. King Sancho Ramírez gave Luna to Don Bacalla, who adopted the name as his family name.
2 CONT
2 CONT When heraldic emblems were developed, the Luna family adopted as their coat of arms or blazon for their lineage a shield with a gules field and a silver crescent cross, and the shield's tip also silver. Overall, it is simple, with
2 CONC  a symbolism that identifies the family's origins and evokes its history:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Gules = red, comes from the coat of arms of Navarre, where it symbolizes the bloody defeat of the Moors. On the Luna coat of arms, it identifies the Navarrese origin of Don Bacalla, founder of the family.
2 CONT * reversed crescent = defeated crescent moon or half moon with points down represents the defeated Muslim symbol, a graphic expression of victory over the infidel.
2 CONT * Silver = The color of the metal symbolizes the white of the moon that illuminated the attackers during the night of victory. 
2 CONT *The point = This shape at the bottom, an honorable part of the shield, also identifies Don Bacalla's origins, as it belongs to Navarrese heraldry, and can be interpreted as the overcoming of difficulties.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sons of Don Bacalla who founded the three branches of the family were Don Lope Ferrench de Luna, Don Martín de Luna and Don Lope de Luna.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original coat of arms of the Luna family would endure in the Martínez de Luna branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Pedro Martínez de Luna, Pope Luna, belonged to the Martínez de Luna branch, the Illueca and Gotor line.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
