2 CONT *1.         [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chindasuinth CHINDASVINTO] (-Toledo 1 Oct 653).  He was elected to succeed in 642 as CHINDASVINTO King of the Visigoths after deposing King Tulga, an act which was condoned by the seventh
2 CONC  council of Toledo convened by the new king specially for the purpose. …  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Chindusvinthus” reigned alone for six years, eight months and eleven days, and with “filio suo Reccesvintho” 
2 CONC four years, eight months and eleven days, and died “pridie Kal Oct 691”[295].  m [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recciberga REKIBERGA], daughter of --- (-after 18 Oct 646).  "Chindasvintux rex et Reciverga regina" donated property 
2 CONC to "monasterium…iuxta rivulum…Molina sub monte Irago" by charter dated 18 Oct 646[296].  Nothing is known about the family origin of King Chindasvinto´s wife, or whether this named wife was the mother of both his children who are 
2 CONC named below.  The estimated birth date of the king’s son Theodefredo suggests that he may have been the son of Rekiberga.  King Chindasvinto and [his wife] had two children: ..
2 CONT ** a)         RECESVINTO (-Villa of Gerticos [Wamba, Valladolid][297] 1 Sep 672, bur [Gerticos], transferred 13th century to Toledo[298]). 
2 CONT ** b)         THEODOFREDO ([645/53]-after 702).  
2 CONT * 2.         ([https://www.geni.com/people/N-N/6000000190762510860?through=6000000190689359869 - -]) m. [ - -] One child: 
2 CONT **a)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Goda/6000000173612831254?through=6000000173612428879 daughter]. The Chronicle of Alfonso III records the marriage of "Ardabastus…from Greece" and the niece of King Chindasvinto[330].  The 
2 CONC marriage must have taken place very early in the reign of King Chindasvinto given the tight possible birth date range of the couple´s granddaughter Cixilo.  m [642] [https://www.geni.com/people/Ardevaste-the-Greek-Balthes-Prince-o
2 CONC f-the-Visigoths/6000000189160702845 ARDABASTO], son of --- .  The Chronicle of Alfonso III records that "Ardabastus came from Greece after being expelled from his country by the emperor…arrived in Spain" and was "received…magnific
2 CONC ently" by King Chindasvinto who gave him his niece in marriage[356].  His parentage is unknown, although this passage suggests that he must have been a person of importance in Byzantium.  Salazar y Castro, in his genealogical tabl
2 CONC e of the Visigothic kings, shows "Atanagildo" as the son of Hermenegildo, as well as his marriage to "Flavia Juliana hija de Pedro Augusto, hermano del Emperador Mauricio", and their sons "Paulo" and "Ardavasto", as well as the la
2 CONC tter´s marriage to "prima, hija o hermana del Rey Cindasuindo"[357] (see above).  The primary sources on which these statements are based are not specified.  The table includes numerous errors and the information should therefore 
2 CONC be viewed with caution.  The name "Ardabasto" suggests a connection with Armenia, "Artavazd" being one of the names used frequently in the Mamikonian family of Armenia, holders of the hereditary position "sparapet" [supreme genera
2 CONC l] between the 5th and 8th centuries[358].  There could also be a connection with Artabasdos (-before 772), strategos of the theme of Armenia, who in 717 married Anna, daughter of Emperor Leon III "the Isaurian". …   Ardabasto and
2 CONC  his wife had [two] children:  
