2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berenguela de Barcelona
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berenguela or Berengaria of Barcelona (1116 – January 15, 1149) was Queen consort of Castile, León and Galicia (1128–1149)
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the daughter of Raimon III of Barcelona and Dulce Aldonza Milhaud. Berenguela was the sister of Ramon Berenguer IV who was the ruler of the Kingdom of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 10/17 1128 in Saldaña, she married Alfonso VII, King of Castile, León and Galicia (1127–1157). Their children were:
2 CONT  1.Sancho III of Castile (1134–1158)
2 CONT  2.Ramon, living 1136, died in infancy
2 CONT  3.Ferdinand II of León (1137–1188)
2 CONT  4.Constance (c. 1138–1160), married Louis VII of France
2 CONT  5.Sancha (c. 1139–1179), married Sancho VI of Navarre
2 CONT  6.García (c. 1142–1145/6)
2 CONT  7.Alfonso (c. 1144–c. 1149)
2 CONT
2 CONT In her lifetime a new political entity was formed in the northeast Iberian Peninsula: Portugal seceded from León in the west, giving more balance to the Christian kingdoms of the Iberian peninsula. Her brother Ramon Berenguer succ
2 CONC essfully pulled Aragon out of its pledged submission to Castile, aided no doubt by the beauty and charm of his sister Berenguela, wife of Alfonso the Emperor, for which she was well known in her time. Her niece (daughter of Ramon 
2 CONC Berenguer) Dulce of Aragon (1160–1198), married in 1175 king Sancho I from Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Palencia, and was buried at the Cathedral of Santiago de Compostela.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appearance and Character
2 CONT
2 CONT "She was a small girl, very beautiful, and very chaste and truthful. She loved all who fear God." From the Chronica Adefonsi imperatoris of Arnaldo of Astorga.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria of Barcelona (1116 – 15 January 1149), called in Spanish Berenguela de Barcelona and also known as Berengaria of Provence, was Queen consort of Castile, León and Galicia. She was the daughter of Ramon Berenguer III, Cou
2 CONC nt of Barcelona, and Douce I, Countess of Provence.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10/17 November 1128 in Saldaña, Berengaria married Alfonso VII, King of Castile, León and Galicia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134–1158)
2 CONT Ramon, living 1136, died in infancy
2 CONT Ferdinand II of León (1137–1188)
2 CONT Constance (c. 1138–1160), married Louis VII of France
2 CONT Sancha (c. 1139–1179), married Sancho VI of Navarre
2 CONT García (c. 1142–1145/6)
2 CONT Alfonso (c. 1144–c. 1149)
2 CONT According to a description, "She was a very beautiful and extremely graceful young girl who loved chastity and truth and all God-fearing people."[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT She died in Palencia, and was buried at the Cathedral of Santiago de Compostela.
2 CONT
2 CONT In fiction
2 CONT A parody version of queen Berengaria and king Alfonso is presented in the tragicomedy La venganza de Don Mendo by Pedro Muñoz Seca. In its film version, Lina Canalejas played Berengaria.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Reilly 1995, p. 168.
2 CONT  Chronica Adefonsi Imperatoris, Book 1 Chapter 12, trans. Simon Barton and Richard Fletcher in The World of El Cid: Chronicles of the Reconquest, (Manchester University Press, 2000) page 168.
2 CONT  Juan Payán, Miguel (2007). La historia de España a través del cine (in Spanish). Cacitel. ISBN 978-84-96613-10-2. Retrieved 31 December 2020.
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Reilly, Bernard F. (1995). The Contest of Christian and Muslim Spain, 1031-1157. Blackwell Publishing Inc.
