1 NOTE William I (of Sicily) (1120-66), Norman king of Sicily (1154-66). The son 
2 CONT of King Roger II, William continued his father's policy of excluding the 
2 CONT principal nobles from participation in the central government. Encouraged 
2 CONT by Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I (Barbarossa) and Byzantine Emperor 
2 CONT Manuel I Comnenus, the nobles rose in rebellion, but by 1160 William 
2 CONT succeeded in crushing them. In 1159 he had also helped to install Pope 
2 CONT Alexander III in the Holy See against Frederick's opposition and the 
2 CONT claims of several antipopes.
