1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.         GAUTHIER [I] de Brienne (-[1090]).    m EUSTACHIE de Tonnerre, daughter of MILO [V] Comte de Tonnerre et de Bar-sur-Seine & his wife Aceka ---.  1072/[1100/05].  Ctss de Bar-sur-Seine.  " Comte Gauthier 
2 CONC [I] & his wife had five children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         ENGELBERT de Brienne (-after 1082).  
2 CONT b)         ERARD [I] de Brienne (-[1114/25], bur Montier-en-Der).  
2 CONT c)         MILON de Brienne (-[1126]).  
2 CONT d)         '''daughter .  A manuscript genealogy, dated to the early 12th century, records "Windesmode Ingelbertus et Witdo de Upione, ex Ingelberto Walterius comes de Brena, ex Walterio filia nata est ista quam Fulco comes noster
2 CONC  uxorem duxerat"[45].  "Airardus comes Brinensis" confirmed donations of "pater meus Walterius comes" to the abbey of Molesme with the consent of "mater mea et due sorores mee" by charter dated to [1085/95][46].  A genealogy prese
2 CONC nted by Foulques IV "le Réchin" Comte d'Anjou to the bishop of Angers in [1085], justifying the annulment of his fourth marriage with the daughter of Gauthier Comte de Brienne, lists "ex Letaldo, Albericus natus est, ex Alberico, 
2 CONC Beatrix, ex Beatrice, Gosfredus de Castello Landonensi, ex Gaufrido, Gaufridus et Fulco presens"[47].  m (after 1080, divorced before 1089) as his fourth wife, FOULQUES IV "le Réchin" Comte d'Anjou, son of GEOFFROY II Comte de Gât
2 CONC inais, Seigneur de Château-Landon & his wife Ermengarde d'Anjou (1043-14 Apr 1109).''' 
2 CONT e)         MANTIA de Brienne .  https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/chambrien.htm#GautierIBriennedied1090
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT C.      COMTES d'ANJOU 1060-1189 (COMTES de GATINAIS)
2 CONT GEOFFROY [II] de Gâtinais, son of HUGUES du Perche Comte de Gâtinais & his wife Beatrix de Mâcon (-30 Apr [1043/47]).   
2 CONT m ([1035]) as her first husband, ERMENGARDE d'Anjou, daughter of FOULQUES III "Nerra" Comte d'Anjou & his second wife Hildegard [de Metz] ([1015/20]-[church of Fleury-sur-Ouche, Côte d’Or 18 Mar 1076& his wife had three children:
2 CONT 3.         FOULQUES de Château-Landon (1043-14 Apr 1109, bur Anjou Sainte-Trinité).  The Historiæ Andegavensis names "Goffridi de Castro Landono et Ermengardis filia Fulconis Comitis Andegavensis" as parents of "Fulco comes Andega
2 CONC vensis"[223].  "Gaufredus comitatus Andecavensis naturalis heres" made donations to Marmoutier dated 1055 in which he names "nepotibus meis…Fulcone vincocinensium comite naturali, Gaufredo et altero Fulcone"[224].  He succeeded, a
2 CONC fter deposing his brother, as FOULQUES IV "le Rechin" Comte d'Anjou.  The Chronicon Vindocinense records that "Fulconi fratri Gaufridi comitis Andegavorum" captured "Salmuri castri…Kal Mar" in 1067 and "II Kal Apr" captured and im
2 CONC prisoned "fratrem suum…comes Gaufredus junior…Barbatum" until his death, although another paragraph of the Chronicon dates the capture to 1068[225].  He ceded Château-Landon and Gâtinais to Philippe I King of France in 1069 in ret
2 CONC urn for the king's recognition of his accession as count[226].  He expelled the Normans from Maine but peace was imposed by William I King of England[227].  The necrology of Angers Cathedral records the death "XVIII Kal Mai" of "F
2 CONC ulco…comes Andagavensis nepos Gaufridi prioris Martelli"[228].  The necrology of Vendôme La Trinité records the death "XVIII Kal Mai" of "Fulco comes Andegavorum"[229].  A list of anniversaries of Vendôme La Trinité records the de
2 CONC ath "XVIII Kal Mai" of "Fulconis comitis qui iacet in Aquaria"[230].  The Chronicon Vindocinense records the death "XVIII Kal Mai" in 1109 of "Fulco comes Andegavorum…frater comitis Gosfridi…Barbatus" and his burial "in monasterio
2 CONC  nostro Andegavense S. Trinitatis"[231].  m firstly ([1068]) HILDEGARDE de Baugency, daughter of LANCELIN [II] de Baugency & his wife --- (-before 1070).  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum refers to the first wife of "Fulco Rechin" a
2 CONC s "filiam Lancelini de Baugenciaco"[232].  The primary source which confirms her name has not been identified.  m secondly (1070, divorced) as her first husband, ERMENGARDE de Bourbon, daughter of ARCHAMBAUD [IV] "le Fort" Seigneu
2 CONC r de Bourbon & his wife Béliarde ---.  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names "Ermengardim filiam Archenbaldi Fortis de Borbone" as second wife of "Fulco Rechin", recording that he divorced her[233].  She married secondly Guillaume 
2 CONC Seigneur de Jaligny.  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum names her second husband "Guillelmo Jalinniaci ortam" when recording the marriage of her daughter by this second marriage[234].  m thirdly (21 Jan 1076, divorced 1080) ORENGARDE
2 CONC  de Châtelaillon, daughter of ISEMBART Seigneur de Châtelaillon & his wife ---.  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the dating clause of a charter dated 21 Jan "1070" (redated to 1076) which records this as the date when 
2 CONC "comes Fulco" married "Aurengarde, filia Isemberti de Castello Allione"[235].  Comte Foulque "Rechin" donated property to Saint-Nicolas d’Angers, for the salvation of "sa femme Orengarde", by charter dated 17 May 1076[236].  She b
2 CONC ecame a nun after her divorce.  '''m fourthly (after 1080, divorced before 1089) --- de Brienne, daughter of GAUTHIER [I] Comte de Brienne & his wife Eustachie Ctss de Bar-sur-Saône .  A manuscript genealogy, dated to the early 12
2 CONC th century, records "Windesmode Ingelbertus et Witdo de Upione, ex Ingelberto Walterius comes de Brena, ex Walterio filia nata est ista quam Fulco comes noster uxorem duxerat"[237].  A genealogy presented by Foulques IV "le Réchin
2 CONC " Comte d'Anjou to the bishop of Angers in [1085], justifying the annulment of his fourth marriage with the daughter of Gauthier Comte de Brienne, lists "ex Letaldo, Albericus natus est, ex Alberico, Beatrix, ex Beatrice, Gosfredu
2 CONC s de Castello Landonensi, ex Gaufrido, Gaufridus et Fulco presens"[238].'''  m fifthly (1089, divorced [15 May 1092]) as her first husband, BERTRADE de Montfort, daughter of SIMON [I] Comte de Montfort-l'Amaury & his third wife Ag
2 CONC nès d'Evreux (-Fontevrault end-1115/1116, bur church of the priory of Hautes-Bruyères, Saint-Remy-l’Honoré, Yvelines).  Her parentage is recorded by Orderic Vitalis[239].  The Gesta Consulum Andegavorum refers to the "third wife" 
2 CONC of "Fulco Rechin" as "sororem Amalrici de Monte Forti"[240].  She married secondly (15 May 1092, repudiated 1104) as his second wife, Philippe I King of France (-29 Aug 1108).  Orderic Vitalis records that “Bertrada...Andegavorum 
2 CONC comitissa”, fearing that her husband was about to treat her like his previous two wives, sought protection from “Philippo regi Francorum” who repudiated his own wife and married her, the ceremony being conducted by “Odo Bajocensis
2 CONC  episcopus”[241].  In another passage, Orderic Vitalis records that King Philippe abducted Bertrade from her first husband and married her bigamously[242].  The De Genere Comitum Flandrensium, Notæ Parisienses names "Fulconi Richi
2 CONC n Andegavensi comiti uxorem suam nomine Bertradam" as second wife of King Philippe whom he abducted from her first husband after repudiating his first wife[243].  William of Tyre records this marriage[244].  Pope Urban II at the C
2 CONC ouncil of Autun excommunicated the king 16 Oct 1094, confirmed at the Council of Clermont 18/28 Nov 1095[245].  The church finally admitted the validity of the marriage after the Council of Paris 2 Dec 1104[246].  Orderic Vitalis 
2 CONC alleges that Bertrade tried to poison her stepson Louis so her own sons could succeed to the throne[247].  "Fulco iunior Andegavensium comes Fulconis comitis filius" donated property to the abbey of Fontevraud with the consent of 
2 CONC "Bertrade regina matre meo, Philipo fratre meo" by charter dated to [1109/1112/13][248].  Foulques IV & his first wife had one child: https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ANJOU,%20MAINE.htm#FoulquesIVdied1109
