1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wulgrin II (also Vulgrin or Bougrin), called Taillifer or Rudel, was the Count of Angoulême from 1120 to his death on 16 November 1140.[1] He was a son of Count William III and he married Pontia de la Marche, daugh
2 CONC ter of Roger the Poitevin and Almodis, the daughter of count Aldebert II of La Marche. They had only one son, William IV of Angoulême. After the death of his first wife, Wulgrin remarried to Amable de Châtellerault and had three c
2 CONC hildren: Fulk, Geoffrey "Martel" and an unnamed daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT He retook Blaye from William X of Aquitaine in 1127 and reconstructed the castle there in 1140.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The troubadour Jaufré Rudel may be possibly his son or his son-in-law.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Colonial and revolutionary lineages of America. p. 319.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ a b The poetry of Cercamon and Jaufré Rudel. p. 96. Garland. 1983.
2 CONT
