1 NOTE William VI of Montpellier was the elder son of William V and his wife Ermessende (daughter of Peter of Melgueil). William succeeded his father in the lordship of Montpellier; he inherited it in 1120,while still a minor, under his 
2 CONC mother's guardianship. William of Aumelas was his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT William VI's wife was named Sibylle, but her origin is uncertain. According to documents adduced at the annulment of the marriage of Marie of Montpellier, her great-granddaughter, she was the daughter of Boniface del Vasto and the
2 CONC refore the sister of Manfred I of Saluzzo, but this cannot be confirmed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inconsolable at the recent death of Sibylle, William VI made his will in 1146 and took holy orders, entering the Cistercian monastery of Grandselve in the diocese of Toulouse[1] in early 1147. He died at some date after 1161, havi
2 CONC ng settled, in that year, an inheritance dispute between his sons William (VII) and Gui.
2 CONT
2 CONT William VI and Sibylle had five sons and three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillelme or Guillemette, who married Bernard Ato V, viscount of Nîmes and Agde, brother of Roger I Trencavel and Raimond I Trencavel. They had one son, Bernard Ato VI
2 CONT William VII of Montpellier
2 CONT William of Tortosa, who married Ermessende of Castries. He joined the Templars in 1157, undertook to remain in Jerusalem for a whole year and died there
2 CONT Raymond William, who became a Cluniac monk. He was William II, bishop of Béziers, until 1166, later abbot of Amiens until 1187, and bishop of Lodève after that date. He died in 1201
2 CONT Bernard William, who died before 1172
2 CONT Gui Guerrejat ("the warrior")
2 CONT Alais or Azalais, who married Eble III of Ventadorn
