1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Foundation for Medieval Genealogy:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''GUILLAUME d'Aquitaine''' (1099-Santiago de Compostela 9 Apr 1137, bur Santiago de Compostela).  The Chronicle of Saint-Maxence records the birth in 1099 of "Willelmo comiti…filius æquivoce Guillelmus"[479].  William of Tyre nam
2 CONC es him and his father[480].  Robert of Torigny names "Guillermum…pater…Alienor reginæ Anglorum" as the son of "Guillermus comes Pictavensis et dux Aquitanorum" & his wife "filia [comitis Tolosani fratris Raimundi comitis Sancti Æg
2 CONC idii]"[481].  He succeeded his father in 1126 as '''GUILLAUME X Duke of Aquitaine, GUILLAUME VIII Comte de Poitou'''.  “Guillelmus comes Pictaviensis et dux Aquitanorum” confirmed rights of “monachi Monasterii Novi Pictaviensis” g
2 CONC ranted by “Gaufredus avus et Guillelmus pater mei” by charter dated 1129[482].  “Willelmus…dux Aquitanorum” donated property to “ecclesiæ B. Hilarii de Cella” (La Celle, outside Poitiers) granted by “Gaufredus avus et Guillelmus p
2 CONC ater mei” by charter dated 3 Mar 1130, subscribed by “Willielmi ducis Aquitanorum, Aenordis comitissæ, Alienordis filiæ eorum, Wilelmi Aigres filii eorum”[483].  The Chronique de Guillaume de Nangis records in 1136 that "Guillaume
2 CONC  comte de Poitou et prince d'Aquitaine" died while on pilgrimage at "Saint-Jacques…la veille de Pâques" and was buried there[484].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly '''ELEONORE de Châtellerault''', daughter of AMAURY [I] Vicomte de Châtellerault & his wife Amauberge "Dangereuse"[485] --- (-after Mar 1130).  “Willelmus…dux Aquitanorum” donated property to “ecclesiæ B. Hilarii de Cell
2 CONC a” (La Celle, outside Poitiers) granted by “Gaufredus avus et Guillelmus pater mei” by charter dated 3 Mar 1130, subscribed by “Willielmi ducis Aquitanorum, Aenordis comitissæ, Alienordis filiæ eorum, Wilelmi Aigres filii eorum”[4
2 CONC 86].  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not so far been identified.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly (1136) as her second husband, '''EMMA de Limoges''', widow of BARDON de Cognac, daughter of ADEMAR [III] "le Barbu" Comte de Limoges & his [second wife Marie des Cars].  The Chronicon Gaufredi Vosiensis names "aliam fil
2 CONC iam [Ademari]…Ennoa (seu Emma)" stating that she married "Guillermus Dux, frater Raymundi Antiochiæ principis" after the death of her earlier husband "Bardoni de Coniaco", before being abducted by "Willelmus Sector-ferri, filius W
2 CONC lgrini Comitis Engolismensis"[487].  She married thirdly (after 1137) as his first wife, Guillaume d'Angoulême, who succeeded his father in 1140 as Guillaume VI "Taillefer" Comte d'Angoulême.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Guillaume X & his first wife had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         '''ELEONORE d'Aquitaine''' (Nieul-sur-Autize, Vendée or Château de Belin, Guyenne or Palais d’Ombrière, Bordeaux 1122-Abbaye de Fontevrault 1 Apr 1204, bur Abbaye de Fontevrault).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Font
2 CONC aines names "Alienor Guilielmi filia comits Pictavorum et Aquitanie ducis" as wife of "regi Francie Ludovico"[488].  “Willelmus…dux Aquitanorum” donated property to “ecclesiæ B. Hilarii de Cella” (La Celle, outside Poitiers) grant
2 CONC ed by “Gaufredus avus et Guillelmus pater mei” by charter dated 3 Mar 1130, subscribed by “Willielmi ducis Aquitanorum, Aenordis comitissæ, Alienordis filiæ eorum, Wilelmi Aigres filii eorum”[489].  She succeeded her father in 113
2 CONC 7 as ELEONORE Dss d’Aquitaine, Ctss de Poitou, Ctss de Saintonge, Angoûmois, Limousin, Auvergne, Bordeaux et Agen.  She left France with her husband in Jun 1147 on the Second Crusade[490].  She was crowned Queen Consort of England
2 CONC  with her husband 19 Dec 1154 at Westminster Abbey.  She supported the revolt of her sons against their father in 1173, was captured and imprisoned in the château de Chinon, later at Salisbury until 1179.  The Continuator of Flore
2 CONC nce of Worcester records the death "XII Kal Apr" [1204] of "regina Alienor" and her burial "ad Fontem Ebraldi"[491].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines records the burial of "uxor [regis Henrici] regina Alienordis" in th
2 CONC e same abbey as her husband[492].  m firstly (Bordeaux, Cathedral of Saint-André  22 Jul 1137, annulled for reasons of consanguinity Château de Beaugency 21 Mar 1152) as his first wife, LOUIS associate King of France, son of LOUIS
2 CONC  VI "le Gros/le Batailleur" King of France & his wife Adélaïde de Maurienne [Savoy] (1120-Paris, Palais Royal de la Cité 18/19 Sep 1180, bur Abbaye cistercienne de Notre-Dame-de-Barbeaux near Fontainebleau, transferred 1817 to l'é
2 CONC glise de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).  He succeeded his father in 1137 as LOUIS VII "le Jeune/le Pieux" King of France.  He was crowned Duke of Aquitaine, in right of his first wife, 8 Aug 1137 at Bordeaux.  m secondly (Poitie
2 CONC rs or Bordeaux Cathedral 18 May 1152) HENRI Duke of Normandy, Comte d'Anjou et du Maine, son of GEOFFROY "le Bel/Plantagenet" Comte d'Anjou et de Maine & his wife [Empress] Matilda [Maud] of England (Le Mans, Anjou 5 Mar 1133-Chât
2 CONC eau de Chinon 6 Jul 1189, bur Abbaye de Fontevrault).  He was recognised as HENRY II King of England after the death of Stephen 25 Oct 1154, he was crowned in Westminster Abbey 19 Dec 1154. 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         '''GUILLAUME d'Aquitaine''' (-[3 Mar 1130/9 Apr 1137]).  “Willelmus…dux Aquitanorum” donated property to “ecclesiæ B. Hilarii de Cella” (La Celle, outside Poitiers) granted by “Gaufredus avus et Guillelmus pater mei” by
2 CONC  charter dated 3 Mar 1130, subscribed by “Willielmi ducis Aquitanorum, Aenordis comitissæ, Alienordis filiæ eorum, Wilelmi Aigres filii eorum”[493]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)        ''' AELIS [Petronille] d'Aquitaine''' ([1125]-after 24 Oct 1151, bur St Arnould in Crépy).  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines specifies that "Alienor Guilielmi filia comits Pictavorum et Aquitanie ducis" had tw
2 CONC o sisters one of whom married "Radulfus…comes Perone et Veromandie", although he does not name them[494].  The Historiæ Tornacenses record the wife of "Radulfem comitem" as "germanam Alienore regine Francorum" but also do not name
2 CONC  her[495].  Robert of Torigny refers to the mother of the infant children of "Radulfus de Perrona comes Viromandorum" as "iuniore filia Willelmi ducis Aquitanorum" but he does not name her either[496].  The Chronique de Guillaume 
2 CONC de Nangis names "Eléonore et Pétronille" as the two daughters of "Guillaume comte de Poitou et prince d'Aquitaine", recording in 1142 that Pétronille married "Raoul comte de Vermandois" after he repudiated his first wife[497].  m 
2 CONC (1142) as his second wife, RAOUL I "le Vaillant" Comte de Vermandois, son of HUGUES "le Maisné" de France Comte de Vermandois & his wife Adelais Ctss de Vermandois, de Valois et de Crépy ([1094]-13 Oct 1152, bur Priory of Saint-Ar
2 CONC noul de Crépy).  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poitiers_Palais_de_justice.JPG#/media/File:Poitiers_Palais_de_justice.JPG ''Palais de Poitiers showing the donjon added in 1104 by  Count William IX on the town side. It is known as the tou
2 CONC r Maubergeon, after his mistress Amauberge ("the Dangerous").The rectangular keep is reinforced with four smaller square towers projecting from each corner.'']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_X,_Duke_of_Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT and in French:  http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guillaume_X_de_Poitiers
2 CONT
2 CONT William X (1099 – 9 April 1137), called the Saint, was Duke of Aquitaine, Duke of Gascony, and Count of Poitou (as William VIII) between 1126 and 1137. He was the son of William IX by his second wife, Philippa of Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born in Toulouse during the brief period when his parents ruled the capital. His birth is recorded in the Chronicle of Saint-Maixent for the year 1099: Willelmo comiti natus est filius, equivoce Guillelmus vocatus ("a 
2 CONC son was born to Count William, named William like himself"). Later that same year, much to his wife's ire, Duke William mortgaged Toulouse to Philippa's cousin, Bertrand of Toulouse, and then left on Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippa and her infant son were left in Poitiers. Long after Duke William's return, he took up with Dangereuse, the wife of one of his vassals, and set aside his rightful wife, Philippa. This caused strain between father and son,
2 CONC  until William married Aenor de Châtellerault, daughter of his father's mistress, in 1121. He had from her three children: Eleanor, who would later become heiress to the Duchy; Petronilla, who married Raoul I of Vermandois; and Wi
2 CONC lliam Aigret, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT As his father before him, William X was a patron of troubadours, music and literature. He was an educated man and strove to give his two daughters an excellent education, in a time when Europe's rulers were hardly literate.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Eleanor succeeded him as Duchess, she continued William's tradition and transformed the Aquitanian court into Europe's centre of knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was both a lover of the arts and a warrior. He became involved in conflicts with Normandy (which he raided in 1136, in alliance with Geoffrey le Bel of Anjou who claimed it in his wife's name) and France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even inside his borders, William faced an alliance of the Lusignans and the Parthenays against him, an issue resolved with total destruction of the enemies. In international politics, William X initially supported antipope Anaclet
2 CONC us II in the schism of 1130, opposite to Pope Innocent II, against the will of his own bishops. In 1134 Saint Bernard of Clairvaux convinced William to drop his support to Anacletus and join Innocent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 William joined the pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, but died of suspected food poisoning during the trip. On his deathbed, he expressed his wish to see king Louis VI of France as protector of his fifteen-year-old daug
2 CONC hter Eleanor, and to find her a suitable husband. Louis VI naturally accepted this guardianship and married the heiress of Aquitaine to his own son, Louis VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume X de Poitiers1, dit le Toulousain ou le Saint, né en 1099 à Toulouse est le dernier des comtes de Poitiers de la dynastie des Ramnulfides.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il règne de 1126 à 1137 sous le nom de Guillaume VIII, comte de Poitiers et duc d’Aquitaine sous le nom de Guillaume X. Il est le fils de Guillaume le Troubadour, auquel il succède, et de Philippa, fille du comte de Toulouse Guill
2 CONC aume IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il s’allie contre la Normandie au comte d’Anjou Geoffroy le Bel. Tranquille sur sa frontière nord, il doit par contre longtemps guerroyer au sud pour contraindre son vassal d’Aunis, Isembert de Châtelaillon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mal inspiré, il soutient avec le légat Girard d’Angoulême l’antipape Anaclet II, pendant cinq ans, à partir de 1130 et jusqu’à une entrevue avec Bernard de Clairvaux au château de Parthenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il meurt le 9 avril (jour du Vendredi saint 1137) au cours d’un pèlerinage à Saint-Jacques-de-Compostelle, il prie dans ses dernières volontés le roi de France Louis VI le Gros de bien vouloir consentir à marier son fils Louis à s
2 CONC a fille aînée, Aliénor d'Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il devient, à la fin du Moyen Âge, un personnage de légende, en partie confondu avec Guillaume de Gellone et saint Guillaume de Maleval, à l’origine de l’ordre des Guillemites.
2 CONT Unions et descendance
2 CONT
2 CONT Il épouse en 1118 ou en 1121, Aénor de Châtellerault, fille d'Aymeric Ier.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Aliénor d’Aquitaine (1122 ou 1124-1204)
2 CONT     Pétronille d’Aquitaine (1125-1153)
2 CONT     Guillaume Aigret (1126-1130)
2 CONT
2 CONT En secondes noces, il épouse Emma de Limoges, fille du vicomte Adémar III († 1139), dit "le Barbu", et veuve de Bardon de Cognac.
2 CONT
2 CONT =_____________________=
2 CONT
2 CONT William X of Aquitaine (1099 – April 9, 1137), nicknamed the Saint was duke of Aquitaine, duke of Gascony and count of Poitiers as William VIII of Poitiers between 1126 and 1137. He was the son of William, the troubadour by his se
2 CONC cond wife, Philippa of Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born in Toulouse during the brief period when his parents ruled the capital. Later that same year, much to his wife's ire, Duke William mortgaged Toulouse to Philippa's cousin, Bertrand of Toulouse, and then left on Cr
2 CONC usade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippa and her infant son were left in Poitiers. Long after Duke William's return, he took up with Dangereuse, the wife of one of his vassals, and set aside his rightful wife, Philippa. This caused strain between father and son,
2 CONC  until William married Ænor of Châtellerault, daughter of his father's mistress, in 1121.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had from her three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Aliaenor, or Eleanor, who would later become heiress to the Duchy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aelith, who married Raoul I of Vermandois 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Aigret, who died young 
2 CONT
2 CONT As his father before him, William X was a patron of troubadours, music and literature. He was an educated man and strove to give his two daughters an excellent education, in a time when Europe's rulers were hardly literate.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Eleanor succeeded him as Duchess, she continued William's tradition and transformed the Aquitanian court into Europe's centre of knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was both a lover of the arts and a warrior. He became involved in conflicts with Normandy (which he raided in 1136, in alliance with Geoffrey le Bel of Anjou who claimed it in his wife's name) and France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even inside his borders, William faced an alliance of the Lusignans and the Parthenays against him, an issue resolved with total destruction of the enemies. In international politics, William X initially supported antipope Anaclet
2 CONC us II in the schism of 1130, opposite to Pope Innocent II, against the will of his own bishops. In 1134 Saint Bernard of Clairvaux convinced William to drop his support to Anacletus and join Innocent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 William joined the pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, but died of suspected food poisoning during the trip. On his deathbed, he expressed his wish to see king Louis VI of France as protector of his fifteen-year-old daug
2 CONC hter Eleanor, and to find her a suitable husband. Louis VI naturally accepted this guardianship and married the heiress of Aquitaine to his own son, Louis VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT =<nowiki>-------------------------------</nowiki>=
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.languedoc-france.info/190202_aquitaine.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT William X, Duke of Aquitaine "the Saint" (1099 – April 9, 1137)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Name in Occitan. Click here to find out more about occitan. Guilhèm X duc d'Aquitània e de Gasconha, Guilhèm VIII comte de Peitieus
2 CONT
2 CONT The Name in French Guilaume X duc d'Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT William was Duke of Aquitaine and Gascony and Count of Poitiers as William VIII of Poitiers between 1126 and 1137.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born in Toulouse during the brief period when his parents ruled the capital. Later that same year, 1126, his father William IX mortgaged Toulouse to his wife's cousin, Bertrand of Toulouse. His wife, Philippa of Toulou
2 CONC se was less than pleased, and less pleased still when he then left on Crusade. Philippa and her infant son were left in Poitiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Long after William IX's return, he took up with the wife of one of his vassals, and set aside his wife, Philippa. This caused strain between father and son, although the strife seems to have been resolved when the younger William 
2 CONC married Ænor of Châtellerault (the daughter of his father's mistress) in 1121. The couple had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Aliaenor, or Eleanor, who would later become heiress to the Duchy;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Aelith ( aka Petronilla), who married Raoul I of Vermandois;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William Aigret, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT William's wife Ænor and their son William Aigret both died in 1130.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father before him, William X was a patron of troubadours, music and literature. He was an educated man and gave his two daughters an excellent education - just one example of the gap between the sophisticated culture of O
2 CONC ccitania and the rest of western Christendom (It was rare enough to give boys a good education, and generally considered "unnatural" and even blasphemous to educated girls. Senior churchmen objected loudly and often).
2 CONT
2 CONT William became involved in conflicts with Normandy and France. Inside his own borders he faced an alliance of the Lusignans and the Parthenays against him, happily resolved by total destruction of the enemies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137, Duke William X set out from Poitiers to Bordeaux, taking his daughters with him. In Bordeaux, he left Eleanor and Petronilla in the charge of the Archbishop of Bordeaux who could be entrusted with the safety of the Duke's
2 CONC  daughters. The Duke then set out for the Shrine of Saint James of Compostela in North-western Spain, in the company of other pilgrims; however, on 9th April (Good Friday) 1137 he was stricken with sickness, probably food poisonin
2 CONC g. He died that evening, having bequeathed Aquitaine to his fifteen-year-old daughter, Eleanor. On his deathbed, he expressed his wish to have King Louis VI of France as protector of Eleanor, and to charge him with finding her a s
2 CONC uitable husband. Louis VI, putting his own interests first, as ever, married Eleanor the new Duchess of Aquitaine to his own son, also called Louis, later King Louis VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_X_of_Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William X (1099 – 9 April 1137), called the Saint, was Duke of Aquitaine, Duke of Gascony, and Count of Poitou (as William VIII) between 1126 and 1137. He was the son of William IX by his second wife, Philippa of Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was born in Toulouse during the brief period when his parents ruled the capital. His birth is recorded in the Chronicle of Saint-Maixent for the year 1099: Willelmo comiti natus est filius, equivoce Guillelmus vocatus ("a 
2 CONC son was born to Count William, named William like himself"). Later that same year, much to his wife's ire, Duke William mortgaged Toulouse to Philippa's cousin, Bertrand of Toulouse, and then left on Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippa and her infant son were left in Poitiers. Long after Duke William's return, he took up with Dangereuse, the wife of one of his vassals, and set aside his rightful wife, Philippa. This caused strain between father and son,
2 CONC  until William married Ænor of Châtellerault, daughter of his father's mistress, in 1121. He had from her three children: Aliaenor, who would later become heiress to the Duchy; Aelith, who married Raoul I of Vermandois; and Willia
2 CONC m Aigret, who died young.
2 CONT
2 CONT As his father before him, William X was a patron of troubadours, music and literature. He was an educated man and strove to give his two daughters an excellent education, in a time when Europe's rulers were hardly literate.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Eleanor succeeded him as Duchess, she continued William's tradition and transformed the Aquitanian court into Europe's centre of knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was both a lover of the arts and a warrior. He became involved in conflicts with Normandy (which he raided in 1136, in alliance with Geoffrey le Bel of Anjou who claimed it in his wife's name) and France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Even inside his borders, William faced an alliance of the Lusignans and the Parthenays against him, an issue resolved with total destruction of the enemies. In international politics, William X initially supported antipope Anaclet
2 CONC us II in the schism of 1130, opposite to Pope Innocent II, against the will of his own bishops. In 1134 Saint Bernard of Clairvaux convinced William to drop his support to Anacletus and join Innocent.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 William joined the pilgrimage to Santiago de Compostela, but died of suspected food poisoning during the trip. On his deathbed, he expressed his wish to see king Louis VI of France as protector of his fifteen-year-old daug
2 CONC hter Eleanor, and to find her a suitable husband. Louis VI naturally accepted this guardianship and married the heiress of Aquitaine to his own son, Louis VII.
