1 OCCU Count of Tripoli, Comte de Tripoli
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pons of Tripoli (c. 1098 – 1137) was the son of Bertrand of Tripoli, and was count of Tripoli from 1112 to 1137.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pons married Cecile of France, the widow of his mentor Tancred, Prince of Galilee and daughter of Philip I of France. This marriage helped to reconcile the Norman and Provençal Crusaders, who had fallen out during the Siege of Ant
2 CONC ioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118 he allied with Baldwin II, the new king of Jerusalem, and in 1119 the two marched north to aid Roger of Salerno against an invasion by Il-ghazi. Roger decided not to wait for them, and he and his army were slaughtered at t
2 CONC he Battle of Ager Sanguinis; Baldwin II was later taken hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1124, after Baldwin had been released, Pons helped capture Tyre, one of the last coastal cities remaining in Muslim hands. In 1125 he aided in the Crusader victory at the Battle of Azaz. In 1131 Pons came into conflict with Ful
2 CONC k of Jerusalem, who had ascended to the throne that year, and was defeated at the Battle of Rugia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 Tripoli was invaded by the sultan of Damascus, and Pons was killed in battle. He was succeeded by his son Raymond II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pons_of_Tripoli
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Pons of Tripoli
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Pons of Tripoli (c. 1098 – 1137) was the son of Bertrand of Tripoli, and was count of Tripoli from 1112 to 1137.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pons married Cecile of France, the widow of his mentor Tancred, Prince of Galilee and daughter of Philip I of France. This marriage helped to reconcile the Norman and Provençal Crusaders, who had fallen out during the Siege of Ant
2 CONC ioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1118 he allied with Baldwin II, the new king of Jerusalem, and in 1119 the two marched north to aid Roger of Salerno against an invasion by Il-ghazi. Roger decided not to wait for them, and he and his army were slaughtered at t
2 CONC he Battle of Ager Sanguinis; Baldwin II was later taken hostage.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1124, after Baldwin had been released, Pons helped capture Tyre, one of the last coastal cities remaining in Muslim hands. In 1125 he aided in the Crusader victory at the Battle of Azaz. In 1131 Pons came into conflict with Ful
2 CONC k of Jerusalem, who had ascended to the throne that year, and was defeated at the Battle of Rugia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1137 Tripoli was invaded by the sultan of Damascus, and Pons was killed in battle. He was succeeded by his son Raymond II.
