2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI Garcés (c. 1133 – June 27, 1194), called the Wise (el Sabio), was the king of Navarre from 1150 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of King García Ramírez and Marguerite de l'Aigle, he was the first to use the title "King of Navarre" as the sole designation of his kingdom, dropping Pamplona out of titular use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was full of clashes with Castile and Aragón. He was a monastic founder and many architectural accomplishments date to his reign. He is also responsible for bringing his kingdom into the political orbit of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried to repair his kingdom's borders which had been reduced by the Treaties of Tudején and Carrión, which he had been forced to sign with Castile and Aragón in his early reign. By the Accord of Soria, Castile was eventually co
2 CONC nfirmed in its possession of conquered territories. He was hostile to Raymond Berengar IV of Aragón, but Raymond's son Alfonso II divided the lands taken from Murcia with him by treaty of Cazorla in 1179. In 1190, the two neighbou
2 CONC rs again signed a pact in Borja of mutual protection against Castilian expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 27, 1194, in Pamplona, where he is interred.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sancha of Castile in 1157, the daughter of Alfonso VII. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Sancho VII of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT #Ferdinand
2 CONT
2 CONT #Ramiro, Bishop of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT #Berengaria of Navarre (died 1230 or 1232), married Richard I of England
2 CONT
2 CONT #Constance
2 CONT
2 CONT #Blanca of Navarre, married Count Theobald III of Champagne, then acted as regent of Champagne, and finally as regent of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI Garcés (c. 1133 – June 27, 1194), called the Wise (el Sabio), was the king of Navarre from 1150 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of King García Ramírez and Marguerite de l'Aigle, he was the first to use the title "King of Navarre" as the sole designation of his kingdom, dropping Pamplona out of titular use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was full of clashes with Castile and Aragón. He was a monastic founder and many architectural accomplishments date to his reign. He is also responsible for bringing his kingdom into the political orbit of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried to repair his kingdom's borders which had been reduced by the Treaties of Tudellén and Carrión, which he had been forced to sign with Castile and Aragón in his early reign. By the Accord of Soria, Castile was eventually c
2 CONC onfirmed in its possession of conquered territories. He was hostile to Raymond Berengar IV of Aragón, but Raymond's son Alfonso II divided the lands taken from Murcia with him by treaty in 1163. In 1190, the two neighbours again s
2 CONC igned a pact in Borja of mutual protection against Castilian expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 27, 1194, in Pamplona, where he is interred.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sancha of Castile in 1157, the daughter of Alfonso VII. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VII of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramiro, Bishop of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria of Navarre (died 1230 or 1232), married Richard I of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca of Navarre, married Count Theobald III of Champagne, then acted as regent of Champagne, and finally as regent of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancho_VI_of_Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI Garcés (c.1133 – June 27, 1194), called the Wise (el Sabio), was the king of Navarre from 1150 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of King García VI Ramírez and Marguerite de l'Aigle, he was the first to use the title "King of Navarre" as the sole designation of his kingdom, dropping Pamplona out of titular use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was full of clashes with Castile and Aragón. He was a monastic founder and many architectural accomplishments date to his reign. He is also responsible for bringing his kingdom into the political orbit of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried to repair his kingdom's borders which had been reduced by the Treaties of Tudellén and Carrión, which he had been forced to sign with Castile and Aragón in his early reign. By the Accord of Soria, Castile was eventually c
2 CONC onfirmed in its possession of conquered territories. He was hostile to Raymond Berengar IV of Aragón, but Raymond's son Alfonso II divided the lands taken from Murcia with him by treaty in 1163. In 1190, the two neighbours again s
2 CONC igned a pact in Borja of mutual protection against Castilian expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 27, 1194, in Pamplona, where he is interred.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI Garcés (c. 1133 – June 27, 1194), called the Wise (el Sabio), was the king of Navarre from 1150 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of King García Ramírez and Marguerite de l'Aigle, he was the first to use the title "King of Navarre" as the sole designation of his kingdom, dropping Pamplona out of titular use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was full of clashes with Castile and Aragón. He was a monastic founder and many architectural accomplishments date to his reign. He is also responsible for bringing his kingdom into the political orbit of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried to repair his kingdom's borders which had been reduced by the Treaties of Tudellén and Carrión, which he had been forced to sign with Castile and Aragón in his early reign. By the Accord of Soria, Castile was eventually c
2 CONC onfirmed in its possession of conquered territories. He was hostile to Raymond Berengar IV of Aragón, but Raymond's son Alfonso II divided the lands taken from Murcia with him by treaty of Cazorla in 1179. In 1190, the two neighbo
2 CONC urs again signed a pact in Borja of mutual protection against Castilian expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 27, 1194, in Pamplona, where he is interred.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sancha of Castile in 1157, the daughter of Alfonso VII. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VII of Navarre 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramiro, Bishop of Pamplona 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria of Navarre (died 1230 or 1232), married Richard I of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca of Navarre, married Count Theobald III of Champagne, then acted as regent of Champagne, and finally as regent of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: Encyclopaedia Britannica Micropaedia, 1981, Vol VIII, p843, Sancho VI the Wise:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Died 1194, King of Navarre (Pamplona) from 1150 and son of Garcia V the Restorer. Sancho was the first to be called king of Navarre; previous kings were known as kings of Pamplona. In 1151 Castile and Aragon agreed to partition N
2 CONC avarre, but Sancho avoided the destruction of his kingdom by accepting Alfonso VII of Castile as his overlord and marrying Alfonso's daughter."
2 CONT
2 CONT d. June 27, 1194 
2 CONT
2 CONT byname SANCHO THE WISE, Spanish SANCHO EL SABIO, king of Navarre (Pamplona) from 1150 and son of García IV (or V) the Restorer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho was the first to be called king of Navarre; previous kings were known as kings of Pamplona. In 1151 Castile and Aragon signed at Tudillén a treaty for the partition of Navarre. By skilled diplomacy Sancho avoided the destru
2 CONC ction of his kingdom, accepting Alfonso VII of Castile as his overlord and marrying Alfonso's daughter. He himself intervened in Castilian affairs during the minority (1158-70) of Alfonso VIII. In 1176 both countries submitted the
2 CONC ir longstanding territorial disputes to Henry II of England as arbiter, who assigned Rioja to Castile. Sancho accepted this decision. He was a legislator of importance, conceding many municipal fueros (charters) and protecting Jew
2 CONC s and immigrants (francos) from the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT Copyright © 1994-2001 Encyclopædia Britannica, Inc.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI Garcés (c. 1133 – June 27, 1194), called the Wise (el Sabio), was the king of Navarre from 1150 until his death in 1194.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of King García Ramírez and Marguerite de l'Aigle, he was the first to use the title "King of Navarre" as the sole designation of his kingdom, dropping Pamplona out of titular use.
2 CONT
2 CONT His reign was full of clashes with Castile and Aragón. He was a monastic founder and many architectural accomplishments date to his reign. He is also responsible for bringing his kingdom into the political orbit of Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He tried to repair his kingdom's borders which had been reduced by the Treaties of Tudellén and Carrión, which he had been forced to sign with Castile and Aragón in his early reign. By the Accord of Soria, Castile was eventually c
2 CONC onfirmed in its possession of conquered territories. He was hostile to Raymond Berengar IV of Aragón, but Raymond's son Alfonso II divided the lands taken from Murcia with him by treaty of Cazorla in 1179. In 1190, the two neighbo
2 CONC urs again signed a pact in Borja of mutual protection against Castilian expansion.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on June 27, 1194, in Pamplona, where he is interred.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sancha of Castile in 1157, the daughter of Alfonso VII. Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VII of Navarre 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramiro, Bishop of Pamplona 
2 CONT
2 CONT Berengaria of Navarre (died 1230 or 1232), married Richard I of England 
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca of Navarre, married Count Theobald III of Champagne, then acted as regent of Champagne, and finally as regent of Navarre
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Mi nuevo libro, LA SORPRENDENTE GENEALOGÍA DE MIS TATARABUELOS está ya disponible en: amazon.com barnesandnoble.com  palibrio.com.  En el libro encontrarán muchos de sus ancestros y un resumen biográfico de cada uno, ya que tenemo
2 CONC s varias ramas en común. Les será de mucha utilidad y diversión.  Ramón Rionda
2 CONT
2 CONT My new book, LA SORPRENDENTE GENEALOGÍA DE MIS TATARABUELOS is now available at: amazon.com barnesandnoble.com  palibrio.com.  You will find there many of your ancestors and a biography summary of each of them. We have several bra
2 CONC nches in common. Check it up, it’s worth it. Ramón Rionda
