1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Rodrigo Gómez''' (died 1146) was a Castilian nobleman and military leader under Alfonso VII. He governed large parts of Asturias and northern Castile, was involved in the politics with neighbouring Navarre, to w
2 CONC hose royal family he was related by marriage, and took part in the Reconquista. Although he was rewarded for loyalty by his sovereign on more than one occasion, he did take part in one brief rebellion, led by a relative. His was a
2 CONC  branch of the Lara family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodrigo was a son of Gómez González and Urraca Muñoz.[1] He married Elvira, a daughter of the Navarrese infante Ramiro Sánchez, sometime before 1137, when they made a joint donation of their villa (palacio in contemporary records)
2 CONC  at Villaverde to the monastery of San Salvador de Oña.[2] In this donation Rodrigo expressed the wish that his gift would purchase eternal life for his lord, Alfonso VII.[3] Elvira outlived her husband by several years, making an
2 CONC other donation to Oña on 18 February 1161 just before her departure on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem.[1] The couple produced three sons, each of whom became counts: Álvaro, Gómez, and Gonzalo.[1][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT According to one historian, Rodrigo first appears in royal documents in 1119, eight years after his father's death at the Battle of Candespina (1111).[4] Another historian cites a document of 13 February 1121 in the archives of th
2 CONC e monastery of Sahagún, a favoured royal residence, as his first public appearance.[1] After the death of Queen Urraca on 8 March 1126, Rodrigo and his brother Diego were among the first to do homage to her successor, Alfonso VII.
2 CONC  In this connexion, they are the first Castilians listed by the Chronica Adefonsi imperatoris, which says of Rodrigo postea ab eo factus est consul ("later made count by him [the king]").[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 17 June 1126 Rodrigo made a donation to the Benedictine monastery of San Pedro de las Dueñas. He made many other donations to the Benedictine monastery at Oña, both before and after his marriage (e.g. 1142).[1] Preserved Romane
2 CONC sque arch from San Pedro de las Dueñas, sculpted in Rodrigo's own time. He made a donation to San Pedro in 1126.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in Alfonso's reign Rodrigo began to accrue positions in Castile. On 13 September 1129 he was holding Cervera and Mudá from the king.[1] On 16 November 1129 he was recorded as holding Briviesca.[1] After the rebellion of Rodr
2 CONC igo González de Lara in 1130, Rodrigo Gómez was his replacement as governor of Asturias de Santillana, although the first charter that records him as such dates to 2 December 1131. He continued to govern that region for the rest o
2 CONC f his life.[1] He was also the recipient of royal largesse in October 1130.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1 June 1131 Rodrigo had been given the title count (comes or consul), the highest dignity in the realm. At the same time he is first mentioned ruling Bureba, which he held onto until at least 25 February 1144.[1] His son Gonzal
2 CONC o would also rule Bureba for most of his career.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1132 Rodrigo supported his kinsman, Gonzalo Peláez, then in revolt in western Asturias, and rapidly fell from favour.[1][4] The king was assembling an army at Atienza for a war with Alfonso I of Aragon and Navarre when he learn
2 CONC ed of the insurrection. He changed plans and led his army into Asturias, where he "seized [Rodrigo Gómez], and he stripped him of his honor and sent him away," according to the Chronica Adefonsi (I, §30). There is some dispute whe
2 CONC ther the Rodrigo of the rebellion was the same as the count, but if he was the latter he was soon restored.[1] He was still at court on 28 May 1132 and had already returned to favour by January 1133.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 December 1135 Rodrigo and his sisters, Sancha and Estefanía, made a donation to Oña for the sake of their late brother Diego's soul.[3] On 21 November 1137 Alfonso VII granted Rodrigo a series of estates in gratitude for his
2 CONC  loyal service, the events of 1132 apparently notwithstanding.[1] By 14 September 1138 Rodrigo had been appointed governor of Belorado, a post which he held until 1144, at which time he also appeared briefly ruling Cerezo.[1] In 1
2 CONC 139 he participated in the Siege of Oreja throughout the summer and into the autumn.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 February 1140 Rodrigo was witness to a treaty between Alfonso VII and Raymond Berengar IV of Barcelona at Carrión de los Condes.[9] Later that year, Rodrigo, with Gutierre Fernández de Castro and a certain Lope López, waged 
2 CONC war on García Ramírez of Navarre on behalf of Alfonso VII.[10] García of Navarre was in fact Rodrigo's brother-in-law.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 27 January 1141 Rodrigo was recorded as ruling Castile, a reference to Castella Vetula (Old Castile) in the north of the kingdom, territory which bordered Asturias de Santillana. He continued to hold it until his death.[1] In 1
2 CONC 142 he was appointed tutor or guardian of García, the youngest son of Alfonso VII.[1] That same year Rodrigo confirmed the donation he and Elvira had made in 1137 of their villa at Villaverde to Oña, this time extending his hopes 
2 CONC to include the eternal life of his ward García.[3] The young García died in 1145.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1144 Rodrigo and Gutierre Fernández escorted the king of Navarre, against whom they had been fighting four years previous, from León to Pamplona with his new bride, Urraca, the daughter of Alfonso VII.[11] The escorts were rewa
2 CONC rded by García, as recounted in the Chronica Adefonsi (I, §94):
2 CONT
2 CONT     [At Pamplona] King García prepared a royal feast for the Castilians and for all the knights and officers of his kingdom. The celebration lasted several days. When it was over, the King gave magnificent gifts to the Castilian n
2 CONC obles, and each of them returned to his land.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last document citing Rodrigo as ruling Asturias and Castile dates from 19 September 1146. Shortly afterwards he made his last donation to Oña, on 21 September.[1] The last reference to Rodrigo as still living dates to November
2 CONC  1146. According to the Chronicon Burgense he died that year.[1] Surviving royal documents reveal that he spent most of his last year at court.[1
2 CONT
2 CONT ''in: Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodrigo_G%C3%B3mez>''
