2 CONC rds the accession of "Ervigius" and his reign of 7 years[363].  In light of the dubious circumstances of his accession, he married his daughter to his most powerful opponent whom he nominated as his successor, by-passing his own s
2 CONC ons[364].  The sons of King Ervigio must have been minors at the time if the chronology of events shown above is correct.  In 681, he revised and enlarged the Liber iudiciorum compiled by his predecessor King Recesvinto[365].  The
2 CONC  Chronicle of Alfonso III records that Ervigio "died a natural death in Toledo" having ruled six years and four months[366].  The Chronica Regum Visigotthorum records that “Ervigius” reigned for seven years and 25 days, and died “
2 CONC XVII Kal Dec”[367].  '''married  ([662]) LIUBIGOTONA,''' daughter of ---.  Her marriage is confirmed by the charter dated to [680/86] which records that "socer noster Ervigius princeps…domina mea Leuvitona regina" granted "filiam 
2 CONC suam" as wife of "Egicæ regis"[368].  According to Europäische Stammtafeln[369] she was Liubigotona the Visigoth, daughter of Suíntila King of the Visigoths, but the primary source on which this is based has not yet been identifie
2 CONC d.  If this parentage is correct, she would have been considerably older than her husband, given the death of her supposed father in 633.  Salazar y Castro, in his genealogical table of the Visigothic kings, shows "Liubigtobona ni
2 CONC eta de Recaredo" as the wife of King Ervigio and mother of his children[370].  The primary source on which this statement is based are not specified.  The table includes numerous errors and the information should therefore be view
2 CONC ed with caution.  Her husband's successor forced his predecessor's widow to retire to a nunnery[371].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ervigio & his wife had [three or more] children:''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>* a)         '''CIXILO''' ([663/65]-).  The Chronicle of Alfonso III records the marriage of "the nobleman Egica, nephew of Wamba" and "Ervig…his daughter Cixilo"[372].  Her birth date range is estimated from (1) the estimate
2 CONC d birth and marriage dates of her father, based on the arrival of her grandfather in Spain during the reign of King Chindasvinto, and (2) the birth of her son Witiza before her repudiation.  Given this tight birth date range, it i
2 CONC s likely that Cixilo was her father's eldest child.  Her parentage and marriage are confirmed by the charter dated to [680/86] which records that "socer noster Ervigius princeps…domina mea Leuvitona regina" granted "filiam suam" a
2 CONC s wife of "Egicæ regis"[373].  The Chronicle of Alfonso III records her repudiation "when [King Ervigio] ascended the throne"[374].  '''married  ([681/86], repudiated [688]) EGICA, nephew of WAMBA ex-King of the Visigoths,''' son 
2 CONC of --- (-[701/02]).  '''He was elected to succeed his father-in-law in 687 as EGICA King of the Visigoths.''' 
2 CONT >>>>>* b)         '''sons'''.  The primary source which confirms their parentage has not yet been identified, although Falcón records that King Ervigio by-passed his own sons when nominating his son-in-law Egica as his successor[3
2 CONC 75]. 
2 CONT >>>>>* c)         ['''VERMUDO''' .  Salazar y Castro, in his genealogical table of the Visigothic kings, shows "Veremundo Duque de Cantabria" and "Pedro Duque de Cantabria" as the sons of King Ervigio[376].  The primary sources on
2 CONC  which these statements are based are not specified.  The table includes numerous errors and the information should therefore be viewed with caution. 
2 CONT >>>>>* d)         [https://www.geni.com/people/Pedro-I-duque-da-Cantábria/6000000003742102823 '''PEDRO'''] Salazar y Castro, in his genealogical table of the Visigothic kings, shows "Veremundo Duque de Cantabria" and "Pedro Duque 
2 CONC de Cantabria" as the sons of King Ervigio[379].  The primary sources on which these statements are based are not specified:''' the table includes numerous errors, and the information should therefore be viewed with caution'''. [ht
2 CONC tps://www.geni.com/discussions/302919?msg=1788935  See Geni Discussion about this] The Chronicle of Alfonso III records "…Peter, who was the leader of the Cantabrians and was of the royal line" when recording him as father of King
2 CONC  Alfonso I[380].  When recording the succession of "roi Alphonse, fils de Pedro", Ibn-Khaldun states that, according to Ibn-Haiyan, he descended "des Goths" but that "à mon avis cette opinion est erronée"[381].  Two sources are mo
2 CONC re specific concerning Pedro’s Visigoth ancestry.  Firstly, the Historia Silense names "Petrus ex Recaredi…Gotorum principis progenie"[382].  Secondly, the Sebastiani Chronicon records "Petri Ducis ex semine Leuvegildi, et Reccare
2 CONC di Regum" when recording him as father of King Alfonso I[383].  His name in fact suggests maybe a Greek and, by extension, Roman connection.  [Duque de Cantabria: the Chronica Albeldense records him as “Petri Cantabriæ ducis”[384]
2 CONC .  The historical basis for supposing that there was a "Dukedom of Cantabria" under the Visigoths is unclear (see the discussion below in the introduction to Chapter 3.B, Visigoth Duces, concerning territorial jurisdiction of duce
2 CONC s in the Visigothic kingdom).]] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://thebiography.us/en/ervigio-rey-visigodo Biography of Ervigio. Rey visigodo (680-687)]
2 CONT
2 CONT King of the Visigoths in Spain. Origin Greek, his father Artabaces (or Ardebasto) came to Spain expelled from the Byzantine Court. His mother was a Princess of goda, prima Chindasvinto blood. He/She won the favor of Wamba, which a
2 CONC cquired great popularity. He/She married a cousin of this so-called Luivigoto. It trades dignity Royal birth was entitled to claim, so impatient to reach the Crown, he/she did give a drink that paralyzed the unfortunate monarch wh
2 CONC o served as an excuse for tonsorial you and invalidate him as King Wamba, and took advantage of the influence of his friends to be declared King at the expense of Teodofredo, son of Recceswinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was formally elected King on 21 October 680, by the Assembly of nobles and bishops, to happen to Wamba. The episcopate, discontent with Wamba because this had decreed that the clergy were subject to military laws, legitimized t
2 CONC he ascension to the throne of Ervig in the twelfth Council of Toledo (year 681) in which, in addition, be renewed anti-Semitic laws. Also held the XIII Congress in the same city in the year 683.Su legislative work focused on meeti
2 CONC ng the demands of the Church and undo the work of Wamba, who forced the clergy to go to the army if it was required for the defence of the nation; It returned many assets that Wamba had seized and condemned the debts that many nob
2 CONC les and clerics had with the State. Freed also Paulo and other rebels who were sentenced to life imprisonment.It conducted a review of the Liber Iudiciorum (Fuero Juzgo) code with the purpose of facilitating the resolution of case
2 CONC s of difficult interpretation, corrected content laws unclear and added new ones. Notably, influenced by Bishop Julián de Toledo, the hardening of the anti-Jewish legislation, which prohibited, in fact, this religion and yield wit
2 CONC hin a year to convert to Christianity. The code revised with immediate application was published on October 21 of the year 681.Para avoid the rebellion of the supporters of Wamba drew as a successor to Ergica, nephew, who married 
2 CONC his daughter Cixilona and made him swear to his coming he/she would respect property of his mother-in-law and also to the other members of the Royal family.In 684 in the fourteenth Council of Toledo, gave the biggest test of zeal 
2 CONC for orthodoxy calling for the condemnation of the monotelitas.In the year 687, he/she left the Crown and retired to a monastery until his death on 13 November of the same year. His wife Liuvigoto survived him. He/She was succeeded
2 CONC  by his son-in-law Ergica.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Erwig''' (Latin: '''Flavius Ervigius'''; after 642 – 687) was a king of the Visigoths in Hispania (680–687). He was the only Visigothic king to be a complete puppet of the bishops and palatine nobility.
2 CONT According to the 9th-century Chronicle of Alfonso III, Erwig was the son of Ardabast, who had journeyed from Constantinople to Hispania during the time of Chindasuinth, and married Chindasuinth's niece Goda. Erwig had Visigothic, 
2 CONC Hispano-Roman, Frankish, Armenian, Greek, and Roman ancestry.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his predecessor Wamba had taken the monastic habit while on the verge of death, he was forced to retire from the kingship, even though he recovered, and enter a monastery. He appointed Erwig his successor on and the latter w
2 CONC as anointed in Toledo on 31 October 680. Later, 9th-century legends attributed to Erwig the poisoning of the king, who was made a penitent by his supporters while Erwig's supporters raised him to the throne. The bishops of the Twe
2 CONC lfth Council of Toledo, which Erwig opened on 9 January 681, confirmed that the documents of abdication and confirmation of Erwig from Wamba were authentic and contained his own signature. Nonetheless, some historians have seen in
2 CONC  the rapidity of Erwig's unction after the king had received the penitential sacrament evidence for a pre-planned palace coup.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erwig began his reign in a climate of uneasiness concerning the way in which he reached the throne. Probably feeling insecure himself, the nobles and bishops took advantage. Erwig restored to favour those who had been out of it in
2 CONC  the time of Wamba. After the Twelfth Council, the Thirteenth (683) and Fourteenth (684) followed in quick succession. The councils confirmed Erwig's legitimacy for a second time and wrote many laws to protect the life and rule of
2 CONC  the king and his family, including that of his queen, Liuvigoto.
2 CONT
2 CONT After falling seriously ill, Erwig proclaimed his son-in-law Ergica, the husband of his daughter Cixilo, as his heir on 14 November 687 and retired to a monastery as a penitent the next day, after giving leave to his court to retu
2 CONC rn to Toledo with Egica for the anointing and crowning. Sigo gozando leyendo sobre mi numero 31 bisabuelito '''Erwig''' (Latin: '''Flavius Ervigius''')
2 CONT
