2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca de Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infanta_Urraca_de_Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca de Borgoña y Saboya (Coímbra, 1151 – ¿?, 16 de octubre de 1188) fue infanta portuguesa y reina consorte de León. Era hija de Alfonso I Enríquez, primer rey de Portugal, y de su esposa Mafalda de Saboya.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1165 se casó con Fernando II de León con quién tuvo a:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Alfonso (1171–1230), sucesor de su padre con el nombre de Alfonso IX. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Esta unión no evitó que su padre Alfonso I declarara la guerra a su yerno. Esta corta guerra acabó cuando Alfonso fue capturado en Badajoz. Quizás debido a su matrimonio con Urraca, Fernando dejó que Alfonso se fuera. Sin embargo,
2 CONC  la unión de Fernando II y Urraca fue disuelta en 1175 por el Papa, usando el hecho de que Urraca era su prima lejana como justificación.
2 CONT
2 CONT Después de la disolución de esta unión, Urraca volvió a la corte de su padre. Allí murió, cuando tenía 37 años, nueve meses después murió su marido.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca de Portugal, Rainha de Leão
2 CONT
2 CONT Origem: Wikipédia, a enciclopédia livre.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urraca_de_Portugal,_rainha_de_Le%C3%A3o
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca Afonso, infanta de Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 - 16 de Outubro de 1188), infanta portuguesa filha de Afonso I de Portugal e de Mafalda, condessa de Sabóia, sendo irmã do rei Sancho I de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Afonso I, rei de Portugal casou a sua filha, Urraca com Fernando II de Leão, seu primo afastado ( pois Fernando era neto de Urraca de Leão e Castela, tia de Afonso Henriques), em 1166, tendo ela apenas 15 anos e ele já 28, mas dev
2 CONC ido aos laços de parentesco o casamento acabou por ser dissolvido pelo Papa em 1175.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deste casamento nasceu o futuro Afonso IX de Leão (1171), último rei de Leão independente, e ainda os infantes Fernando, Sancha e Dulce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Depois da anulação do seu casamento, Urraca partiu para a corte do pai, em Portugal e veio a falecer em Coimbra em 1188, com apenas 37 anos de idade.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca of Portugal (b. Coimbra, 1151– d. 16 October 1188; pron. IPA: [u'?ak?]), was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II of León (c. 1165) with whom 
2 CONC she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father King Afonso I of Portugal from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due to his marr
2 CONC iage to Urraca, Ferdinand was generous to King Afonso of Portugal, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was dissolved in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justifi
2 CONC cation.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		 This biography of a member of the Portuguese royal family is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urraca_L%C3%B3pez_de_Haro
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; Portuguese pronunciation: [uˈʁakɐ]) was a Portuguese  infanta (princess), daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal  and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand I
2 CONC I of León (c. 1165) with whom she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps du
2 CONC e to his marriage to Urraca, Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was annulled in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justificatio
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT   Queen Regnant  Urraca I Alfonsez of Castilla and Léon  (Spain)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1107 she reigned over her Dowry Galicia and Zamora after the death of her first husband Count Raimond de Bourgogne. The following year she inherited the throne from her father Alfonso VI Fernandez of Castile and Leon (1040-1109
2 CONC ). Her second marriage in the year 1109 to Alfonso I Perez de Aragon (d. 1134) ended in divorce in 1114. Her reign was disturbed by strife among the powerful nobles and especially by constant warfare with her husband, who had seiz
2 CONC ed her lands. She never remarried, though she took several lovers. Another thorn in her side was her half-sister, Tarasa of Portugal and her husband, Enrique, who allied with her estranged husband, then betrayed him when a better 
2 CONC offer came from Urraca's court. After her brother-in-law's death in 1112, her sister still contested ownership of lands. With the aid of her son, Alfonso Raimúndez, Urraca was able to win back much of her domain and ruled successf
2 CONC ully until her death. According to the Chronicon Compostellanum, she died in childbirth in 1126. The father was her lover, Count Pedro González of Lara. She was succeeded by her legitimate son, Alfonso VII Raymundez of Castile and
2 CONC  Leon "Imperator totus Hispaniae" (d. 1157), She lived (1082-1128/29).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://images.google.com/imgres?imgurl=http://www.guide2womenleaders.com/womeninpower/fotos/Urraca-Castilla-Leon.jpg&imgrefurl=http://www.guide2womenleaders.com/womeninpower/European-Queen-Regnants.htm&usg=__4fRETYQdBB4WqW25pTDnT2
2 CONC nhki8=&h=188&w=100&sz=5&hl=en&start=4&tbnid=woS6TICYK7fS5M:&tbnh=102&tbnw=54&prev=/images%3Fq%3Durraca%2Bqueen%2Bof%2Bleon%26gbv%3D2%26hl%3Den%26sa%3DG
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; pron. IPA: [u'ʁakɐ]), was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II of León (c. 1165) with wh
2 CONC om she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due to his marriage to Urraca
2 CONC , Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was dissolved in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justification.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; pron. IPA: [u'ʁakɐ]), was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II of León (c. 1165) with wh
2 CONC om she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due to his marriage to Urraca
2 CONC , Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was dissolved in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justification.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; pron. IPA: [u'ʁakɐ]), was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II of León (c. 1165) with wh
2 CONC om she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due to his marriage to Urraca
2 CONC , Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was dissolved in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justification.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; Portuguese pronunciation: [uˈʁakɐ]) was a Portuguese infanta (princess), daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II 
2 CONC of León (c. 1165) with whom she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due 
2 CONC to his marriage to Urraca, Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was annulled in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justification.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca Lopez de Haro
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca López de Haro and Ruiz de Castro (1170 - 1262) daughter I Lope Diaz de Haro, Lord of Vizcaya and Aldonza Rodriguez de Castro.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was the fourth abbess of the Monastery of rods belonging to the Cistercian order in the town of Cañas, La Rioja (Spain).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lover first and third wife after Ferdinand II of León, married in 1185 or 1187. They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Garcia Fernandez de Leon, born c. 1180 (before marrying their parents), who died in 1184.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Fernandez de Leon called the Cañamero ", the place where he died, born 1188, died in 1220. Lord of Aguilar and Monteagudo. Married in 1210 with Teresa Díaz de Haro, daughter of Diego López de Haro II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Urraca tried to place his son on the throne Sancho lions, wanting to oust the legitimate son Ferdinand II had had with his first wife, the future Alfonso IX, so that he almost provoked a civil war. Alfonso IX, avoiding greater evi
2 CONC ls, marched with his grandfather in Portugal until the death of his father. Upon the death of Ferdinand II in 1188, Alfonso IX became king. Magpie took refuge in Castile and Leon retain the castles of the land, until they were cau
2 CONC ght by Alfonso.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1225 she was appointed abbess of the Monastery of Cañas, construction started in this church, the chapter room, kitchen and dining cilla. Also ordered to build a hospital in Cañas.
2 CONT
2 CONT His tomb lies in the Monastery of Cañas and is considered one of the best memorials of Spain. On September 28th of 1898 was lifting the lid of the tomb to the Episcopal Delegate of the diocese of Calahorra and La Calzada-Logroño, 
2 CONC found the perfectly preserved body.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was declared a saint and is contained in the martyrology Cistercian.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta de Portugal y reina consorte de León por su matrimonio con el rey Fernando II de León. Era hija de Alfonso I Enríquez, primer rey de Portugal, y de su esposa, la reina Mafalda de Saboya. Fue madre del rey Alfonso IX de Leó
2 CONC n y abuela de Fernando III el Santo, rey de Castilla y León.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hija de Alfonso I Enríquez, primer rey de Portugal, y de su esposa, la reina Mafalda de Saboya, fue hermana, entre otros, del rey Sancho I de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emblema de la Orden de San Juan de Jerusalén, en la que ingresó como freira la reina Urraca de Portugal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrajo matrimonio en mayo o junio del año 1165 con el rey Fernando II de León, hijo de Alfonso VII el Emperador, rey de Castilla y León, y de su esposa, la reina Berenguela de Barcelona, siendo la reina Urraca de Portugal la pri
2 CONC mera infanta del reino de Portugal en desposarse con un miembro de la realeza leonesa, y el día 15 de agosto de 1171, en la ciudad de Zamora, nació el único hijo del matrimonio, el infante Alfonso, siendo bautizado en la catedral 
2 CONC de Zamora, y que sucedió a su padre en el trono de León cuando éste falleció.
2 CONT
2 CONT Debido al parentesco que existía entre los reyes de León, pues ambos eran primos segundos, el rey de León se vio obligado a repudiar a su esposa, ya que el matrimonio de ambos fue anulado por el Papa Alejandro III, en el año 1171 
2 CONC ó 1172.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tras haberse declarado nulo su matrimonio, la reina Urraca de Portugal ingresó como freira en la Orden de San Juan de Jerusalén, y se retiró a vivir en los municipios zamoranos que su esposo el rey le concedió al desposarse con el
2 CONC la, y, posteriormente, se retiró al monasterio de Santa María de Wamba, situado en la actual provincia de Valladolid, y que pertenecía a la Orden de San Juan de Jerusalén.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1188 asistió a la coronación de su hijo Alfonso IX de León, que heredó el trono leonés tras la defunción de su padre, ocurrida el día 22 de enero de 1188, y ese mismo año, el día 4 de mayo, la reina Urraca y su hijo Alfonso IX 
2 CONC confirmaron los privilegios concedidos por Fernando II de León a la Orden de Santiago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se desconoce su fecha exacta de defunción, aunque las crónicas de la época coinciden en que falleció en el año 1188, y algunos historiadores señalan que falleció el día 16 de octubre.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sepultura de la reina Urraca de Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT Después de su defunción, el cadáver de la reina Urraca de Portugal recibió sepultura en el Monasterio de Santa María de Wamba, que pertenecía a la Orden de San Juan de Jerusalén.
2 CONT
2 CONT En el interior de la iglesia de Santa María de Wamba, que formó parte de un monasterio desaparecido en la actualidad, se halla ubicada la llamada capilla de la Reina, donde se halla colocado un epitafio, posterior a la defunción d
2 CONC e la reina, en el que se relata que la reina Urraca de Portugal recibió sepultura en dicha iglesia.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Nupcias y descendencia
2 CONT
2 CONT Fruto de su matrimonio con el rey Fernando II de León, hijo de Alfonso VII el Emperador, rey de Castilla y León, nació un hijo:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso IX de León (1171-1230). Sucedió a su padre en el trono de León. Se desposó por primera vez con la infante Teresa de Portugal y Barcelona, hija del rey Sancho I de Portugal y de la reina Dulce de Aragón, pero el matri
2 CONC monio fue anulado debido al grado de parentesco existente entre los cónyuges. Se desposó por segunda vez, en el año 1197, con la infanta Berenguela de Castilla, hija del rey Alfonso VIII de Castilla y de la reina Leonor de Plantag
2 CONC enet. Fruto de su segundo matrimonio nacieron, entre otros, el rey Fernando III el Santo y el infante Alfonso de Molina, padre de la reina María de Molina. Fue sepultado en la Catedral de Santiago de Compostela.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Infanta Urraca of Portugal (Coimbra, 1151 – 16 October 1188; pron. IPA: [u'ʁakɐ]), was a Portuguese infanta, daughter of Afonso I, 1st King of Portugal and his wife Maud of Savoy. She married Ferdinand II of León (c. 1165) with wh
2 CONC om she had Alfonso IX of León. This marriage didn't prevent her father Afonso I from declaring war on his son-in-law. This short war culminated in disaster when Afonso was captured in Badajoz. Perhaps due to his marriage to Urraca
2 CONC , Ferdinand was generous to Afonso, and let him leave. However, the marriage of Ferdinand II and Urraca was dissolved in 1175 by the Pope, using the fact that Urraca was his distant cousin as justification.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of her marriage, Urraca returned to the court of her father and died there, aged only 37, nine months after the death of her former husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
