2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan IV Fergant (died 13 October 1119) was Duke of Brittany, from 1084 until his abdication in 1112. He was also Count of Nantes and Count of Rennes. He was son of Hawise, Duchess of Brittany and Duke Hoel II. He was known as Alan
2 CONC  Fergant, which in Breton means "Alan the Younger". Through his father, he was of the Cornwall dynasty, (Breton: Kerne dynasty)
2 CONT
2 CONT Norman Conflict A traditional rivalry between Brittany and Normandy continued at the close of the 11th century. The Breton-Norman war of 1064–1065 was the result of William I of England's support of rebels in Brittany against Alan
2 CONC 's grandfather, Conan II. Hostilities continued, and by 1086 Conan was forced to abandon his duchy after William I of England invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peace was reached that same year, and in the negotiations that followed Alan IV was forced into marriage with King William I's second daughter Constance.[1] The marriage ceremonies may have taken place in Bayeux in Normandy. Willi
2 CONC am of Malmesbury wrote that Constance was unpopular at the Breton court because of her "sever and conservative" manner.[1] William of Malmesbury also alleged that Alan VI had Constance poisoned to death, but this remained unverifi
2 CONC ed[1] However, Orderic Vitalis wrote that as duchess Constance did all she could to further the welfare of the Bretons, who grieved deeply at her death in 1090.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1092 Alan IV donated property to the abbey of Redon by charter, and by 1093 married Ermengarde of Anjou, as a political alliance with Fulk IV of Anjou to counter Anglo-Norman influence.[1] With Ermengarde he had two sons, Geoff
2 CONC rey, who died young, and Conan, and a daughter, Hawise (who married Count Baldwin VII of Flanders), who was possibly named after Alan's mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crusader In 1098 Alan went on the First Crusade, leaving Ermengarde as his regent, and returned in 1101.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdication and Death Alan IV abdicated in 1112, and he and Ermengarde were separated after this. The former Duke retired to the monastery of Redon, where he died in 1119.
2 CONT
2 CONT His illegitimate son was Brien FitzCount.
2 CONT
2 CONT See also http://patp.us/genealogy/conq/richmond.aspx
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dès le début de son règne, Alain Fergent se heurte à Guillaume le Conquérant, il refuse de lui rendre hommage. Guillaume le Conquérant envahit la Bretagne, ravage le Comté de Rennes et met le siège devant Dol qu'il ne quittera pas
2 CONC  avant de l'avoir prise. Le Duc leva une forte armée et Guillaume le Conquérant leva le siège, laissant un énorme butin aux Bretons. Puis il offrit la main de sa fille en échange de la paix. La duchesse décède trois ans après. Ala
2 CONC in se remarie avec  Ermengarde d'Anjou. Après avoir mis de l'ordre dans son duché, Alain fergent se croise avec l'élite de sa noblesse. En ces cinq années d'absence la Duchesse Ermengarde dirige avec autorité le Duché. Le Duc part
2 CONC icipe à la bataille de Tinchebray en 1106. Il abdique en 1112 en faveur de son fils CONAN et se retire au monastère de Saint-Sauveur à Redon où il meurt le 13 octobre 1119 et y est inhumé. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alain was lso called Alan Comes Brittannie, and also Alain IV "Fergent," and also Alan "the Younger."
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain IV, duc de Bretagne, was the successor of Hoël II, comte de Cornoüaille & de Dol; Count of Cornouille.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain led, along with Aimeri IV, Vicomte de Thouars, the left wing of William the Conqueror's army in the invasion of England in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain first married Constance de Normandie, daughter of King William I (the Conqueror, or the Bastard) Queen Mathilda of Flanders, in 1068.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain was Earl of Richmond between 1068 and 1119. He had large holdings in Yorkshire, and twelve other counties, in 1086. He was Count of Brittany between 1087 and 1112.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain married Ermengarde d' Anjou, daughter of Foulques IV "le Rechin", comte d' Anjou and Hildegarde Lancelote de Beaugency, in 1093. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain went on the First Crusade with Robert of Normandy in 1099. He assisted King Henry I at Tinchebrai in 1106.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alain died a monk on 13 October 1119. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See "My Lines"
2 CONT
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p360.htm#i7818 )
2 CONT
2 CONT from Compiler: R. B. Stewart, Evans, GA
2 CONT
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/index.htm )
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1070 in Bretagne, Indre, FRANCE Death: 13 OCT 1119 in Monastery of Redon, Brittany, FRANCE Event: Note 1086 Listed as a landowner in The Domesday Book (England) Event: Note 1112 Became a monk and resided in the Monaster
2 CONC y of Redon (FRANCE). Event: Title (Facts Pg) BET 1084 AND 1112 duc de Bretagne (Duke of Brittany), Maison de Cornouailles (House of ......), Fergent Event: Name 2 'Alain Fergant', which in Breton means "Alan the Younger". Burial: 
2 CONC 14 OCT 1119 Monastery of Redon Cemetery, Brittany, FRANCE 
2 CONT
2 CONT rittany, Count Alan of - Married daughter of William I, Constance. Also called Earl of Richmond. Head of Bretons in England. Large holdings in Yorks., and also in twelve other counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Domesday Book extract.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan IV of Cornwall (died 1119) was duke of Brittany, from 1084 to 1112. He was also Count of Nantes and Count of Rennes. He was son of Duke Hoel II and Havise of Brittany. He was known as Alain Fergant, which in Breton means "Ala
2 CONC n the Younger".
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan IV had to face an invasion of William I of England and was forced to abandon his duchy in 1086. Peace was made in the same year and Alan married Princess Constance of England, William's daughter. This marriage was forced upon
2 CONC  Alan and he is presumed responsible for Constance's death by poisoning in 1090 -- William of Malmesbury says she was killed because she was too conservative for the Breton court. Alan married again in 1093 with Ermengarde of Anjo
2 CONC u, a formidable woman who became the real ruler of Brittany. By her he had a son Geoffrey, a daughter Havise (married to count Baldwin VII of Flanders) and his heir Conan. In 1098 Alan went on Crusade, leaving Ermengarde as his re
2 CONC gent, and returned in 1101. Alan IV was not a popular duke and was forced to abdicate in 1112. He retired to the monastery of Redon and died there in 1119.
2 CONT
2 CONT His illegitimate son was Brien FitzCount.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: "Wikipedia, free encyclopedia", http://en.wikipedia.org/
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_IV,_Duke_of_Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT Alan IV (born circa 1063; died 13 October 1119) was Duke of Brittany from 1084 until his abdication in 1112. He was also Count of Nantes and Count of Rennes. His parents were Hawise, Duchess of Brittany and Hoel II. He is also kno
2 CONC wn as Alan Fergant. ("Fer gant" in French means "Iron glove".) Through his father, he was of the Breton House of Cornouaille dynasty (Breton: Kerne dynasty). He was the last Duke of Brittany to speak the Breton language.
