1 NOTE
2 CONT 1.  Geoffrey was also known as 'The Plantagenet' and more commonly as 'Plantagenet, Geoffrey V the Fair, Count of Anjou and Maine'.  He was Duke Normandy 1144-1150 abdicating ca 1151.  Geoffrey was the progenitor of the Plantagene
2 CONC t kings.  In the main line of descent, were Henry, II, Richard I, John, Henry, III, Edward I, Edward, II, Edward, III, and Richard, II; through the house of Lancaster, Henry, IV, Henry V, and Henry, VI; and through the house of Yo
2 CONC rk, Edward, IV, Edward V, and Richard, III
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  The Plantagenet surname was originally a nick name, of the English royal house of Anjou or the Angevin dynasty, founded by Geoffrey V, Count of Anjou (1113-51), spouse of Matilda (1102-67), daughter of King Henry I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some say the name is derived from the Latin planta ('sprig') and genistae ('broom plant'), in reference to the sprig that Geoffrey always wore in his cap.  The name Plantagenet, according to Rapin, came from when Fulk the Great,  
2 CONC being stung from remorse for some wicked action, in order to atone for it, went on a pilgrimage to Jerusalem, and was scourged before the Holy Sepulchre with broom twigs.  Maybe the latter happened first and then the former follow
