1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Aymer, Count of Angoulême==
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer III de Taillefer, Comte d'Angoulême (1160-1202)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/MRMP-3HL
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Angoul%C3%AAme-41
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/111263946/aymer-taillefer
2 CONT
2 CONT Of a long line of Count Angoulemes. Aymer and his wife Alice were the parents of Queen Isabella, a great beauty, who married first King John I of England, and after his death Hugh de Lusignan.
2 CONT Find A Grave contributor Anonymous has made a suggestion: Aymer was the son of William and his second wife, Marguerite de Turenne, daughter of Raymond I, Vicomte de Turenne and his wife, Matilde du Perche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emma was William's first wife but was not the mother of his children.
2 CONT
2 CONT en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aymer,_Count_of_Angoul%C3%AAme Aymer, Count of Angoulême]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer I (c. 1160–16 June 1202), the last Taillefer  Count of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer (also Aymar, Adhemar, Ademar, or Adomar; c. 1160 – 16 June 1202) was the last Count of Angoulême of the House of Taillefer. He was the youngest child of Count William VI and Marguerite de Turenne.[1] Two of his elder brother
2 CONC s, Wulgrin III and William VII, became Counts of Angoulême in succession after the death of their father in 1179.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer succeeded his brother in 1186, and soon after was at the court of Richard the Lionheart, then Duke of Aquitaine and thus Aymer's lord, to receive recognition of his accession.[a][3] By 1188, Aymer had married Alice of Courte
2 CONC nay, the daughter of Peter I of Courtenay and thus granddaughter of King Louis VI of France.[b] In that year, Alice gave birth to a daughter, Isabella of Angoulême, who married King John of England in 1200. The marriage alliance w
2 CONC as sealed by two treaties, one public, the other private between Aymer and John. The count remained a steady ally of the kings of England against the rebellious House of Lusignan.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer had a claim to the County of La Marche, where in 1199 or 1200 he was exercising authority, perhaps on behalf of his son-in-law, and issued a charter to some monks of Aubignac.[6] In February 1202 when John was visiting Angou
2 CONC lême to negotiate a treaty with Sancho VII of Navarre, Aymer took him on a tour of the newly consecrated abbey church at La Couronne.[7] The role of Aymer's daughter in John's continued refusal to properly care for his brother Ric
2 CONC hard the Lionheart's widow, Berengaria of Navarre, may explain the Count of Angoulême's proximity to the negotiations between the two kingdoms.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer died in Limoges on 16 June 1202. His daughter and only child succeeded him as Countess of Angoulême. Her title, however, was largely empty since her husband denied her control of her inheritance as well as her marriage dowry
2 CONC  and dower. John's appointed governor, Bartholomew de Le Puy (de Podio), ran most of the administrative affairs of Angoulême until John's death in 1216.[9][c] In 1217 Isabella returned and seized her inheritance from Bartholomew, 
2 CONC who appealed unsuccessfully to the English king for help.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer's widow, Alice, ruled the city of Angoulême until March 1203, when John summoned her to court and granted her a monthly pension of 50 livres d'Anjou in return for her dower rights. She thereafter retired from public life to 
2 CONC her estate at La Ferté-Gaucher, where she was living as late as July 1215, when she issued a charter at Provins using the title Countess of Angoulême.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Cites One Source:''' Vincent, Nicholas (1999). "Isabella of Angouleme:John's Jezebel". In Church, S.D. (ed.). ''King John: New Interpretations''. Boydell Press.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''fr.Wikipédia:''' [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aymar_II_d%27Angoul%C3%AAme Aymar II d'Angoulême]) – Cites several sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Plantagenet Chronicles p. 263
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'Queens of England'
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte d'Angoulême
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aymer (also Aymar, Adhemar or Adomar; c. 1160 – 16 June 1202) was the last Count of Angoulême of the House of Taillefer. He was the third of the six children of Count William IV and Marguerite de Turenne. His two elder brothers, W
2 CONC ulgrin III and William VII, became the Counts of Angoulême in succession after the death of their father in 1179. 
2 CONT Aymer succeeded his brother in 1186, and soon after was at the court of Richard the Lionheart, then Duke of Aquitaine and thus Aymer's lord, to receive recognition of his accession.[1] By 1191, Aymer had married Alice of Courtenay
2 CONC , the daughter of Peter I of Courtenay and thus granddaughter of King Louis VI of France.[2] In 1188, they had a daughter, Isabella of Angoulême, who married King John of England in 1200. The marriage alliance was sealed by two tr
2 CONC eaties, one public, the other private between Aymer and John. The count remained a steady ally of the kings of England against the rebellious House of Lusignan.  Aymer had a claim to the County of La Marche, where in 1199 or 1200 
2 CONC he was exercising authority, perhaps on behalf of his son-in-law, and issued a charter to some monks of Aubignac. In February 1202 when John was visiting Angoulême to negotiate a treaty with Sancho VII of Navarre, Aymer took him o
2 CONC n a tour of the newly consecrated abbey church at La Couronne.[3] The role of Aymer's daughter in John's continued refusal to properly care for his brother Richard the Lionheart's widow, Berengaria of Navarre, may explain the Coun
2 CONC t of Angoulême's proximity to the negotiations between the two kingdoms.  Aymer died in Limoges on 16 June 1202. His daughter and only child succeeded him as Countess of Angoulême. Her title, however, was largely empty since her h
2 CONC usband denied her control of her inheritance as well as her marriage dowry and dower. John's appointed governor, Bartholomew de Le Puy (de Podio), ran most of the administrative affairs of Angoulême until John's death in 1216. In 
2 CONC 1217 Isabella returned and seized her inheritance from Bartholomew, who appealed unsuccessfully to the English king for help.  Aymer's widow, Alice, ruled the city of Angoulême until March 1203, when John summoned her to court and
2 CONC  granted her a monthly pension of 50 livres d'Anjou in return for her dower rights. She thereafter retired from public life to her estate at La Ferté-Gaucher, where she was living as late as July 1215, when she issued a charter at
2 CONC  Provins using the title Countess of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aymer_of_Angoulême
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Aymer III de Taillefer, Comte d'Angoulême (1160-1202)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aymer_of_Angoul%C3%AAme
2 CONT
2 CONT and in French: http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aymar_Taillefer
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer I (c. 1160–16 June 1202), the last Taillefer Count of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] History
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer (or Aymar) was the third of the 6 children of William IV of Angoulême, the Count of Angoulême, and Marguerite of Turenne. His two elder brothers, Wulgrin III of Angoulême and William V of Angoulême became the Counts of Angou
2 CONC lême, respectively, after the death of their father in 1179 (Wulgrin first, then William V succeeding in 1181). Aymer succeeded his brother in 1186 as the Count of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT In that same year, he married Alice of Courtenay, the daughter of Peter of Courtenay (the son of Louis VI of France) and Elizabeth of the House of Courtenay. In 1188, they had a daughter who would play an important role in the his
2 CONC tory of England and France: Isabella of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aymer died in Limoges, France on June 16, 1202. His daughter and only child Isabella succeeded him as the ruler of the county of Angoulême. Her title, however, was largely empty since her husband John, king of England, denied Isab
2 CONC elle control of her inheritance as well as her marriage dowry and dower. John's appointed governor, Bartholomew le Puy, ran most of the administrative affairs of Angoulême until John's death in 1216. In 1217 Isabelle returned and 
2 CONC seized her inheritance from le Puy, who appealed unsuccessfully to the English king for help.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT Plantagenet Chronicles p. 263
2 CONT
2 CONT The book, 'Queens of England'
2 CONT
2 CONT Comte d'Angoulême
2 CONT
