1 NOTE {geni:about_me} >'''William de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby'''
2 CONT >'''Marriage''' Sybil De Braose b: 1146-1150 in Bramber, Sussex, England
2 CONT >Married: 1173-1174 in of Sussex, England
2 CONT >>'''Children'''
2 CONT >>'''1.''' William II De Ferrers b: Abt 1162-1170 in Ferrers, Derbyshire, England
2 CONT >>'''2.''' Agatha de Ferrers b: 1168-1172 in Chartley, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT >>'''3.''' Petronilla De Ferrers b: Abt 1170 m. Hervey Bagot de Stafford
2 CONT >>'''4.''' Henry de Ferrers b: 1176
2 CONT >>'''5.''' Robert de Ferrers b. 1169
2 CONT
2 CONT William I de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby (died 1190) was a 12th century English Earl who resided in Tutbury Castle in Staffordshire and was head of a family which controlled a large part of Derbyshire known as Duffield Frith. He wa
2 CONC s also a Knight Templar.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of Robert de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby and his wife, Margaret Peverel. He succeeded his father as Earl of Derby in 1162. He was married to Sybil, the daughter of William de Braose, 3rd Lord of Bramber and Bert
2 CONC ha of Hereford.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Ferrers was one of the earls who joined the rebellion against King Henry II of England led by Henry's eldest son, Henry the Younger, in the Revolt of 1173–1174, sacking the town of Nottingham. Robert de Ferrers II, his 
2 CONC father, had supported Stephen of England and, although Henry II had accepted him at court, he had denied the title of earl of Derby to him and his son. [2] In addition, William had a grudge against Henry because he believed he sho
2 CONC uld have inherited the lands of Peveril Castle through his mother. These, King Henry had previously confiscated in 1155 when William Peverel fell into disfavour.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the failure of the revolt, de Ferrers was taken prisoner by King Henry, at Northampton on the 31 July 1174, along with the King of Scots and the earls of Chester and Lincoln, along with a number of his Derbyshire underlings a
2 CONC nd was held at Caen. He was deprived of his castles at Tutbury and Duffield and both were put out of commission (and possibly Pilsbury.) In addition to defray the costs of the war Henry levied a so called "Forest Fine" of 200 mark
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He seems to have afterwards regained the confidence of Henry II., and he showed his fidelity to the next Sovereign, (King Richard I.), by accompanying him in his expedition to the Holy Land, and joined the Third Crusade and died a
2 CONC t the Siege of Acre in 1190. [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Confederate General Robert E. Lee and President Thomas Jefferson are descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT President George Washington, George Herbert Walker Bush, George W. Bush and Gen. Douglas MacArthur, are descendants.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William I de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby was born on c. 1138 in Tutbury Castle, Tutbury, Staffordshire, England to Robert II de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby and Margaret Peverell de Ferrers, Countess of Derby.  William married Sybil 
2 CONC de Braose on 1161 in Sussex, England and had 2 children:  William II de Ferrers, 4th Earl of Derby and Pernell de Ferrers.  He passed away on December 31, 1189 in Normandy, France from wounds received at the Seige of Acre.
2 CONT
2 CONT SEE LINK:
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Ferrers,_3rd_Earl_of_Derby]
2 CONT
2 CONT William I de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby is my 27th great grandfather.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Duffield Castle====
2 CONT '''Lecture by William Bland''', at the Temperance Hall, Wirksworth, on Tuesday, January 11th., 1887
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV - THE EARLS DE FERRARS. (g).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Ferrars succeeded his father in 1162. He joined the King's sons in a rebellion against their father, Henry II., and was deprived of his Earldoms of Derby and Nottingham, but afterwards he made submission to the King, an
2 CONC d was pardoned, but according to Dugdale," So little did the King trust him that he forthwith demolished those forts," i.e., the castles of Tutbury and Duffield. Dr. Cox says, "There is great reason for believing, that so far as D
2 CONC uffield Castle was concerned, the order of demolition by Henry II, if ever issued, was certainly never carried out, for it stood for another century." (see footnote) William seems to have afterwards regained the confidence of Henr
2 CONC y II., and he showed his fidelity to the next Sovereign, (King Richard I.), by accompanying him in his expedition to the Holy Land, and taking part in the siege of Acre, where he was killed in 1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Footnote'': Webmaster's Note. From the later excavations by Manby in 1957, it appears that the original de Ferrars castle was built of wood. Perhaps it was this that was destroyed by Henry II, being later rebuilt in stone, which
2 CONC  was then also sacked by Edward I.   [http://www.jjb.uk.com/william/04ferrs.htm]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Born: ca. 1140, Oakham, Rutlandshire, England
2 CONT Christened: Tutbury, Staffordshire, England
2 CONT Military: bet. 1188 and 1191, Third Crusade 
2 CONT Died: 1191  Akko (Acre), Palestine
2 CONT Buried: bef. 21 Oct 1191, Jerusalem
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "In 1174 the Earl of FERRERS and Derby, with other powerful lords, came to Nottingham on behalf of a young Henry, son of Henry II., and took the castle from Richard de LUCY, whom the king had appointed guardian of the realm during
2 CONC  his absence in Normandy."  (Cassell's History of England)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT William I de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby (died 1190) was a 12th-century English Earl who resided in Tutbury Castle in Staffordshire and was head of a family which controlled a large part of Derbyshire known as Duffield Frith. He wa
2 CONC s also a Knight Templar.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the son of Robert de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby and his wife, Margaret Peverel. He succeeded his father as Earl of Derby in 1162. He was married to Sybil, the daughter of William de Braose, 3rd Lord of Bramber and Bert
2 CONC ha of Hereford.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Ferrers was one of the earls who joined the rebellion against King Henry II of England led by Henry's eldest son, Henry the Younger, in the Revolt of 1173–1174, sacking the town of Nottingham. Robert de Ferrers II, his 
2 CONC father, had supported Stephen of England and, although Henry II had accepted him at court, he had denied the title of earl of Derby to him and his son. In addition, William had a grudge against Henry because he believed he should 
2 CONC have inherited the lands of Peveril Castle through his mother. These, King Henry had previously confiscated in 1155 when William Peverel fell into disfavour.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the failure of the revolt, de Ferrers was taken prisoner by King Henry, at Northampton on the 31 July 1174, along with the King of Scots and the earls of Chester and Lincoln, along with a number of his Derbyshire underlings a
2 CONC nd was held at Caen. He was deprived of his castles at Tutbury and Duffield and both were put out of commission (and possibly Pilsbury.) In addition to defray the costs of the war Henry levied a so-called "Forest Fine" of 200 mark
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT He seems to have afterwards regained the confidence of Henry II., and he showed his fidelity to the next Sovereign, (King Richard I.), by accompanying him in his expedition to the Holy Land, and joined the Third Crusade and died a
2 CONC t the Siege of Acre in 1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was succeeded by his son William de Ferrers, 4th Earl of Derby.
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Ferrars Preceptory No.530 is a Knight Templar preceptory named after William de Ferrars. This preceptory is stationed in Burton upon Trent.
2 CONT www.findagrave.com
2 CONT
