2 CONT ----
2 CONT ==Eleanor of Aquitaine
2 CONT Eleanor of Aquitaine, a powerful medieval figure, inherited the vast Duchy of Aquitaine and the County of Poitiers at 15, becoming one of the wealthiest and most influential women in Europe, and later queen of both France and Engl
2 CONC and. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Inheritance and Marriage to [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-VII-the-Young-king-of-France/6000000005791896564?through=6000000003714562119 Louis VII of France]''' (1137-1152)
2 CONT * Eleanor was born around 1122, the daughter of William X, Duke of Aquitaine.  
2 CONT * Upon her father's death in 1137, she inherited the Duchy of Aquitaine, which included the regions of Poitiers, Gascony, Limousin, and Auvergne.  
2 CONT * She was placed under the guardianship of King Louis VI of France and married his son, Louis VII, at the age of 15, becoming Queen of France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216936879833&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aquitaine#/media/File:France_1154-en.svg ''France, Aquitaine and Poitiers in 1154''  by Reigen]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Eleanor and Louis VII had two daughters, but their lack of a son led to tension and eventually their divorce in 1152.   
2 CONT * 25 yr old Eleanor and 27 yr old Louis VII joined the Second Crusade to the Holy Land (1147–1149) after Pope Eugenius III called for European intervention against Muslim forces in the Levant. She traveled with her vassals and a l
2 CONC arge retinue of knights, unusual for a queen. The crusade was plagued by poor leadership and military failures, particularly in Anatolia, where the French forces suffered heavy losses. Eleanor was criticized for her closeness to h
2 CONC er uncle, [https://www.geni.com/people/Raymond-de-Poitiers-prince-of-Antioch/6000000010060961353 Raymond of Antioch], which led to tensions with Louis.The crusade ended in failure, with the French forces unable to reclaim Edessa o
2 CONC r make significant gains in the Holy Land.
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216936944826&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aquitaine#/media/File:Deuxi%C3%A8me_croisade-it.svg ''Route of the Second Crusade'']
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216950915837&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1f/Zengid_territory.png ''Cutaway of part of the territory under Nur al-Din Zengi in 1174 CE]''
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216943664853&size=large}}
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000216943957837&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Eleanor’s marriage to Louis VII deteriorated after the failed crusade, and they had no male heir.Their marriage was annulled in 1152, citing consanguinity (they were distant cousins).
2 CONT
2 CONT * An approximately 30 yr old Eleanor quickly married 19 yr old Henry, Duke of Normandy (later Henry II of England), consolidating their power and forming the Angevin Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Queen of England''' (1154–1173) 32-51 yrs old
2 CONT * Henry II became King of England in 1154, making Eleanor Queen of England.She played a key role in governing the vast Angevin territories, including England and much of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1153-1166 She gave birth to eight children, including future kings Richard the Lionheart and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1156 Death of William (Poitiers or Normandy, August 17, 1153 - Wallingford Castle, Berkshire, April or June 1156)
2 CONT
2 CONT *1168 12 yr old Matilda married 39 yr old Henry the Lion, Duke of Saxony and Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT *1172 17 yr old Henry, known as Henry the Young King, married 14 yr old Margaret of France, Louis VII's daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT * As Henry II’s rule became more controlling, tensions grew between them.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rebellion Against Henry II and Imprisonment''' (1173–1189) 51 to 67 yrs old
2 CONT * Eleanor supported her sons in a revolt against Henry in 1173, seeking more autonomy for their territories.The rebellion failed, and Henry II imprisoned Eleanor for 16 years, keeping her in various castles in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1177 12yr old  Joanna marries William II of Sicily in Winchester,  and 15yr old Eleanor left for Castille and was married to Alfonso VIII in Burgos
2 CONT
2 CONT *1180  Louis VII dies and his son Philip II succeeds
2 CONT
2 CONT *1181 23yr old Geoffrey was married to Constance of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT *1183 Henry died of dysentery at Martel, at age 28 (Bermondsey Palace, February 28, 1155 - Château de Martel, Turenne, June 11, 1183) His dying wishes included a plea for his mother to be set free and that his wife Marguerite be p
2 CONC rovided for.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1184 Eleanor returned to England where her 28 yr old daughter Matilda and son-in-law Henry (now in exile) were able to stay with her at Winchester and then Berkhamsted.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1186 Death of 27 yr old Geoffrey (September 23, 1158 - Paris, August 19, 1186)
2 CONT
2 CONT *1189 Death of 33 yr old Matilda (Windsor Castle, June 1156 - Brunswick, June 28, 1189) just after her own daughter, Eleanor's, marriage  to William IV of the Netherlands
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rule During Richard I’s Reign''' (1189–1199) 67 to 77 yrs old
2 CONT * 1189 56 yr old Henry II died in 1189, and their son, Richard I (the Lionheart), freed 65 yr old Eleanor.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1189 31 yr old Richard is crowned, and Eleanore acts as regent while Richard goes on the Third Crusade. She played a crucial role in securing his ransom when he was captured on his return journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1189 22yr old Prince John married his second cousin 14 yr old cousin Isabella
2 CONT
2 CONT *1190 Once Richard had set off, Eleanor sent Prince John to England while she travelled to Pamplona to fetch 25 yr old Berengaria, daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre. From there, she escorted Berengaria to Sicily, where , in Messina
2 CONC , she and Richard found that Joan, widowed since November 1189, was being held prisoner and had her freed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *1191, Richard and Berengaria together with Joanna left Sicily, with the women on a separate ship, bound for Outremer, but storms diverted them to Cyprus where Richard and Berengaria were married on 12 May at Limasol, and Berengar
2 CONC ia was crowned.They then sailed to the Holy land on 5 June, arriving at Acre on the 8th, which Richard captured.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1192 In the Holy Land, Richard made little progress in his quest to capture Jerusalem, and by late 1192 was forced to arrange a truce with Saladin, and sent Joanna and Berengaria back to Sicily in September, departing from Acre h
2 CONC imself on 9 October. His whereabouts were unknown till January 1193 when Eleanor learned that he had been taken prisoner by Duke Leopold, whom he had slighted on his arrival in Acre.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1194 A ransomed Richard is finally released.
2 CONT
2 CONT *1196 Joan marries Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT *1199 Death of 41 yr old Richard (Beaumont Palace, Oxford, September 8, 1157 - Chalus, April 6, 1199)
2 CONT
2 CONT *1199 Death of 33 yr old Joan (Château d’Angers, Anjou, October 1165 - Fontevrault Abbey possibly in childbirth, September 4, 1199) 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Final Years and Death''' (1199–1204) 77 to 82 yrs old
2 CONT * After Richard’s death in 1199, Eleanor supported her youngest son, John, in his struggle for the throne against her 12 yr old grandson, [https://www.geni.com/people/Arthur-duke-of-Brittany/5365469962760089487?through=53653786626
2 CONC 60125863 Arthur of Brittany]. 
2 CONT * She continued to be involved in the affairs of Aquitaine, where she spent her final years.  
2 CONT * She retired to Fontevraud Abbey, where she died in 1204 at age 82, and was buried in the abbey church at Fontevraud, next to Henry II.   
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT *1214 Death of Eleanor (Domfront, Normandy, October 13, 1162 - Burgos, October 25, 1214)
2 CONT
2 CONT *1216 Death of John (Beaumont Palace, Oxford, December 24, 1166 or 1167 - Newark Castle, Lincolnshire, October 18/19, 1216)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT In a way Eleanor of Aquitaine's life had barely begun after she returned to France from her travels on the Second Crusade. She lived until her eighties, becoming one of the great political and wealthy powers of medieval Europe.  E
2 CONC leanor was wealthy because she was heiress of the duchy of Aquitaine, one of the greatest fiefs in Europe. Aquitaine was like a separate nation with lands extending in southwestern France from the river Loire to the Pyrenees. Elea
2 CONC nor's court was a trend setter in the medieval world, known for its sophistication and luxury. Heavily influenced by the Spanish courts of the Moors, it gave patronage to poets and encouraged the art of the troubadours, some of wh
2 CONC om were believed to be in love with the beautiful Eleanor. One story is that in her effort to shed her rough knights of their unruly ways, she made up a mock trial in which the court ladies sat on an elevated platform and judged t
2 CONC he knights, who read poems of homage to women and acted out proper courting techniques. The men wore fancy clothes - flowing sleeves, pointed shoes - and wore their hair long.
2 CONT
2 CONT During their adventures on the Second Crusade, it became apparent that her marriage with dour, severe King Louis VII of France was ill matched. The marriage was annulled on a technicality, and Eleanor left her two daughters by him
2 CONC  to be raised in the French court. Within a short time Eleanor threw herself into a new marriage, a stormy one to Henry of Anjou, an up and coming prince eleven years younger than she. Their temperaments as well as their wealth in
2 CONC  land were well matched; her new husband became Henry II king of England in 1154.
2 CONT
2 CONT For the next thirteen years Eleanor constantly bore Henry children, five sons and three daughters. (William, Henry, Richard I "the Lionheart", Geoffrey, John "Lackland", Mathilda, Eleanor, and Joan). Richard and John became, in tu
2 CONC rn, kings of England. Henry was given the title "the young king" by his father, although father Henry still ruled. Through tough fighting and clever alliances, and with a parcel of children, Henry and Eleanor created an impressive
2 CONC  empire. As well, Eleanor was an independent ruler in her own right since she had inherited the huge Duchy of Aquitaine and Poitiers from her father when she was 15.
2 CONT
2 CONT However all was not well between Henry and Eleanor. When her older sons were of age, her estrangement from her husband grew. In 1173 she led her three of her sons in a rebellion against Henry, surprising him with this act of aggre
2 CONC ssion so seemingly unusual for a woman. In her eyes it was justified. After two decades of child bearing, putting up with his infidelities, vehemently disagreeing with some of his decisions, and, worst of all, having to share her 
2 CONC independence and power, Eleanor may have hoped that her prize would have been the right to rule Aquitaine with her beloved third son Richard, and without Henry. The rebellion was put down, however, and fifty-year-old Eleanor was i
2 CONC mprisoned by Henry in various fortified buildings for the next fifteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1189, Henry died. On the accession of her son Richard I to kingship, Eleanor's fortunes rose again. When Richard was fighting in the Holy Land she repeatedly intervened to defend his lands - even against her son John. When he w
2 CONC as captured on his way home, she used her considerable influence to help raise the ransom and secure Richard's release. Her relentless work on behalf of her favorite son increased her fame as an extremely able politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * In 1191 went over the Pyrennese to fetch Richard's wife, Berengeria, then travelled the Alps to get to Sicily where Richard was. - over 70 yrs old
2 CONT * In 1199 when she's about 75-80 she's on a political campaign for her son John's kingship, and travels across the Pyrennese to choose a bride for her dead ex-husband'a son from her granddaughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor traveled constantly, even in her old age. Running from one end of Europe to another, she often risked her life in her efforts to maintain the loyalty of the English subjects, cement marriage alliances, and manage her army 
2 CONC and estates. By this time she had many grandchildren. Possibly one of her wisest acts was to travel to Spain to chose and collect her thirteen year old grand daughter [https://www.geni.com/people/Blanche-de-Castille-reine-consort-
2 CONC de-France/6000000001412937510?through=6000000001412881786 Blanche of Castile] to become the bride of Louis VIII of France, the grandson of her first husband Louis VII! Blanche eventually proved a rival to Eleanor in political infl
2 CONC uence and success as queen of France. Eleanor also, when almost seventy, rode over the Pyrenees to collect her candidate to be Richard's wife, ([https://www.geni.com/people/Berengaria-of-Navarre-Queen-consort-of-England/6000000004
2 CONC 076536467?through=5597288619460126496 Berengaria], the daughter of King Sancho the Wise of Navarre). She then traversed the Alps, traveling all the way down the Italian peninsula, to bring Berengaria to Sicily. Berengaria then tra
2 CONC velled to Cyprus, where Richard married her at Limossol on May 12, 1191.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor died in 1204 at her favorite religious house, the abbey of Fontevrault, where she had retreated to find peace during various moments of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =Eleanor of Aquitaine’s grandchildren through her children with  Louis VII of France 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Marie of France, Countess of Champagne (1145–1198) and Henry I, Count of Champagne (1127–1181)'''
2 CONT * Henry II, Count of Champagne (1166–1197) – Married Isabella I of Jerusalem
2 CONT
2 CONT * Marie of Champagne (c. 1174–1204) – Married Baldwin I of Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT * Theobald III, Count of Champagne (1179–1201) – Married Blanche of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT * Scholastica of Champagne (d. 1219) – Possibly unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Alix of France (1150–c. 1195) and Theobald V, Count of Blois (c. 1130–1191)'''
2 CONT * Theobald VI, Count of Blois (c. 1179–1218) – Unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT * Louis I, Count of Blois (d. 1205) – Married Catherine of Clermont
2 CONT
2 CONT =Eleanor of Aquitaine’s grandchildren through her children with Henry II of England
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Henry the Young King (1155–1183)'''
2 CONT * No known children.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Matilda, Duchess of Saxony (1156–1189) and Henry the Lion, Duke of Saxony and Bavaria (c. 1129–1195)'''
2 CONT * Richenza of Saxony (c. 1172–1204) – Married Godfrey VI, Count of Louvain
2 CONT
2 CONT * Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine (1173–1227) – Married Agnes of Hohenstaufen
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lothar of Saxony (1174–1190) – Died unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT * Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor (1175–1218) – Married Beatrice of Swabia
2 CONT
2 CONT * William of Winchester, Lord of Lüneburg (1184–1213) – Married Helen of Denmark
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Richard I of England (1157–1199)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * No known legitimate children
2 CONT * possibly Philippe de Cognac by an unknown mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany (1158–1186) and Constance, Duchess of Brittany (c. 1161–1201)'''
2 CONT * Eleanor, Fair Maid of Brittany (c. 1184–1241) – Never married (imprisoned for life)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Matilda of Brittany (c. 1185–before 1189) – Died young
2 CONT
2 CONT * Arthur I, Duke of Brittany (1187–1203, presumed killed) – Betrothed to Marie of France, but never married
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Eleanor, Queen of Castile (1162–1214) and Alfonso VIII, King of Castile (1155–1214)'''
2 CONT * Berengaria of Castile (c. 1180–1246) – Married Alfonso IX, King of León
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sancho, Prince of Castile (1181–1181) – Died young
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sancho VII of Navarre (1182–1234) – Married (possibly) Constance of Toulouse (marriage uncertain)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Urraca of Castile (1186–1220) – Married Alfonso II, King of Portugal
2 CONT
2 CONT * Blanche of Castile (1188–1252) – Married Louis VIII, King of France
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ferdinand of Castile (c. 1189–1211) – Died unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT * Mafalda of Castile (1191–1204) – Betrothed but never married
2 CONT
2 CONT * Eleanor of Castile (1202–1244) – Married James I, King of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Joan of England (1165–1199) and William II of Sicily (1155-1189)'''
2 CONT * Bohemond (1182 - died young) 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through Joan of England (1165–1199) and Raymond VI, Count of Toulouse (1156–1222)'''
2 CONT * Raymond VII, Count of Toulouse (1197–1249) – Married (1) Sancha of Aragon, (2) Margaret of Lusignan
2 CONT *Jeanne de Toulouse (1198–1255) – Married Bernard II de la Tour, Lord of la Tour.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Through John, King of England (1166–1216) and Isabella of Angoulême (c. 1188–1246)'''
2 CONT * Henry III, King of England (1207–1272) – Married Eleanor of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT * Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall (1209–1272) – Married (1) Isabella of Pembroke, (2) Sanchia of Provence, (3) Beatrice of Falkenburg
2 CONT
2 CONT * Joan of England (1210–1238) – Married Alexander II, King of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT * Isabella of England (1214–1241) – Married Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor
2 CONT
2 CONT * Eleanor of England (1215–1275) – Married (1) William Marshal, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, (2) Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester
