1 OCCU Princess of France, Lady of Tarsus and Mamistra, Countess of Tripoli (1106-1145)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Cecile of France (1097–after 1145) was a daughter of Philip I of France and Bertrade of Montfort. The Historia Regum Francorum Monasterii Sancti Dionysii names "Philippum et Florum et filiam unam" as children of "P
2 CONC hilippus rex [et] Fulconi Rechin Andagavorum comiti uxorem", specifying that the (unnamed) daughter married "Tanchredus Anthiochenus". Her parentage is recorded by William of Tyre, who also records her two marriages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first marriage was arranged while Bohemond I of Antioch was visiting the French court seeking support against Alexios I Komnenos. She sailed for Antioch at the end of 1106 and became Lady of Tarsus and Mamistra, in Cilician Ar
2 CONC menia. Cecile married firstly (late 1106) Tancred, Prince of Galilee, Regent of Antioch, who succeeded in 1111 as Prince of Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT While dying in 1112, Prince Tancred made Pons of Tripoli, son of Bertrand of Toulouse, promise to marry her, and Tancred gave her the fortresses of Arcicanum and Rugia as a dowry. They married in 1115. In 1133, Pons was besieged a
2 CONC t his castle of Montferrand by Zengi, atabeg of Mosul, in 1133, and Cecile appealed to her half-brother, King Fulk of Jerusalem, to come to his aid. Zengi abandoned the siege, but during a second siege in 1137, Pons was captured a
2 CONC nd killed. He was succeeded by his son with Cecile, Raymond II. Cecile died in 1145.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children with Pons
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond II of Tripoli
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip (1126-42)
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes (died before Mar 1183), married Renaud II, Lord of Marqab
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecile_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
