1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#Constancedied1176
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond V, Count of Toulouse
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, Raymond married Constance of France, daughter of king Louis VI of France and his second wife Adélaide de Maurienne. Constance was the widow of Eustace IV of Boulogne. Because Raymond was related to her within prohibited d
2 CONC egrees, they were separated by ecclesiastical authority in 1165. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond VI, who succeeded his father
2 CONT
2 CONT Aubri, died 1180
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide or Azalais of Toulouse, who married Roger II Trencavel in 1171 and died in 1199
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin, born 1165, executed on the orders of Raymond VI in 1214
2 CONT
2 CONT Laura died 1230 married Odo of Comminger d.1215
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constance_of_France_%281124%E2%80%931176%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of France, Countess of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Constance of France (1124–1176))
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess consort of Boulogne and Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1140-1153
2 CONT
2 CONT 1154-1165
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Eustace IV, Count of Boulogne
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond V, Count of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond VI of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Azalais of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Aubri of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT Laura of Toulose
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Capet
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Louis VI of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Adélaide de Maurienne
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	c.1124
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	c.1176 (aged 51-52)
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of France (1124-1176) was the only daughter of Louis VI of France and his second wife Adélaide de Maurienne. She is also known as Constance Capet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 First marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance's elder siblings included: Louis VII of France, Philip of France, Henri of France, Robert I of Dreux, another Philip of France and Peter of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Humbert II, Count of Savoy and Gisela of Burgundy. Her paternal grandparents were Philip I of France and Bertha of Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] First marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1140, Constance married Eustace IV, Count of Boulogne. Eustace was knighted in 1147, at which date he was probably from sixteen to eighteen years of age. In 1151 he joined Constance's brother, Louis in an abortive raid upon Nor
2 CONC mandy, which had accepted the title of the Empress Matilda (another of many Matildas of the era), and was now defended by her husband, Geoffrey of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had no children and Eustace died in 1153, aged between 17-26.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, Constance remarried after being a widow for a year. She married Raymond V, Count of Toulouse, being his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond was implicated in the Cathar crisis. In 1176, Guilhem, the Bishop of Albi organized the council of Lombers, attended by both clerics and princes, including Raymond of Trencavel, Raymond V, and Constance, and the representa
2 CONC tives of the Cathars. The crisis later led to the Albigensian Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond and Constance had five children together, they were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Raymond VI, who succeeded his father
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Aubri, died 1180
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Adelaide or Azalais of Toulouse, who married Roger II Trencavel in 1171 and died in 1199
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Baldwin, born 1165, executed on the orders of Raymond VI in 1214
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Laura died 1230 married Odo of Comminger d.1215
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Raymond was related to Constance within prohibited degrees, they were separated by ecclesiastical authority in 1165. Raymond later married Richeza of Poland, who had been widowed twice before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died in 1176, aged 51–52 by which time her brother, Louis was King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Translation from French Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 16 July 2010 at 10:57.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of France (1124-1176) was the only daughter of Louis VI of France and his second wife Adélaide de Maurienne. She is also known as Constance Capet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance's elder siblings included: Louis VII of France, Philip of France, Henri of France, Robert I of Dreux, another Philip of France and Peter of Courtenay.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Humbert II, Count of Savoy and Gisela of Burgundy. Her paternal grandparents were Philip I of France and Bertha of Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1140, Constance married Eustace IV, Count of Boulogne. Eustace was knighted in 1147, at which date he was probably from sixteen to eighteen years of age. In 1151 he joined Constance's brother, Louis in an abortive raid upon Nor
2 CONC mandy, which had accepted the title of the Empress Matilda (another of many Matildas of the era), and was now defended by her husband, Geoffrey of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT The couple had no children and Eustace died in 1153, aged between 17-26.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1154, Constance remarried after being a widow for a year. She married Raymond V, Count of Toulouse, being his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond was implicated in the Cathar crisis. In 1176, Guilhem, the Bishop of Albi organized the council of Lombers, attended by both clerics and princes, including Raymond of Trencavel, Raymond V, and Constance, and the representa
2 CONC tives of the Cathars. The crisis later led to the Albigensian Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond and Constance had five children together, they were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Raymond VI, who succeeded his father
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Aubri, died 1180
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Adelaide or Azalais of Toulouse, who married Roger II Trencavel in 1171 and died in 1199
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Baldwin, born 1165, executed on the orders of Raymond VI in 1214
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Laura died 1230 married Odo of Comminger d.1215
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Raymond was related to Constance within prohibited degrees, they were separated by ecclesiastical authority in 1165. Raymond later married Richeza of Poland, who had been widowed twice before.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance died in 1176, aged 51–52 by which time her brother, Louis was King of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The book, 'Kings & Queens of Great Britain'
