1 OCCU Senhor da Torre de Vasconcelos, Señor de la Torre de Vasconcelos, Senhor da Torre de Vasconcelos e Vila de Amares, Sr. da Casa de seus pais e da Torre de Vasconcelos
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wikipedia [http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_Martins_da_Torre PT], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_Martins,_Lord_of_the_Tower_of_Vasconcelos EN], [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_Martins_da_Torre FR]
2 CONT
2 CONT Foi um [http://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobre nobre] português e senhor da Torre de Vasconcelos [ : [http://www.panoramio.com/map/#lt=41.624072&ln=-8.355017&z=0&k=2&a=1&tab=1&pl=all Panoramio] ] e da vila de [http://pt.wikipedia.org/
2 CONC wiki/Amares Amares].
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Martins, Lord of the Tower of Vasconcelos, was a Portuguese 12th century noble knight, son of Martim Moniz[1] (legendary figure of the Siege of Lisbon in 1147) and Teresa Afonso.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the Lord of the Tower of Vasconcelos and of the village of Amares, in the north of Portugal, in the modern district of Braga. The said tower, seat of the preeminent family of the Vasconcelos, had previously belonged to the 
2 CONC Order of the Knights Templar but was recruited to the Order of the Knights of Santiago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Martins married Teresa Soares da Silva, and had a son and a daughter[2]:
2 CONT
2 CONT João Peres de Vasconcelos, “O Tenreiro”, the first to use the surname Vasconcelos and the one to give it continuity;[3] married to Maria Soares Coelho.[4] Sancha Peres de Vasconcelos, married first to Mendo Afonso, and secondly to
2 CONC  João Gomes Barreto.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Eugénio de Castro (1933), Os Meus Vasconcelos, Coimbra, Coimbra Editora, p.7.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Eugénio de Castro (1933), Os Meus Vasconcelos, Coimbra, Coimbra Editora, p.7.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. José Augusto de Sotto Mayor Pizarro (1999), Linhagens Medievais Portuguesas, Porto, Universidade Moderna, 1st vol., p.481.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Cristovão Alão de Morais & Carvalhos de Basto (1997), Pedatura Lusitana, 2nd ed., Braga, 3rd vol., p.9.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Eugénio de Castro (1933), Os Meus Vasconcelos, Coimbra, Coimbra Editora, p.8.
