1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Blanca de Navarra (1177-1229) De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanca_de_Navarra_%281177_-_1229%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca de Navarra (1177 – 13 de marzo de 1229) fue infanta de Navarra y condesa consorte de Champaña (1199–1201). Era hija de Sancho VI de Navarra y de Sancha de Castilla (hija ésta de Alfonso VII) y hermana de Sancho VII de Navar
2 CONC ra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se casó el 1 de julio de 1199 con Teobaldo III de Champaña. Tuvo un hijo: Teobaldo I de Navarra (1201–1253), rey de Navarra y conde de Champaña.
2 CONT
2 CONT A partir de 1201, y tras la muerte de su marido, Blanca gobernó el condado de Champaña como regente. La regencia estuvo llena de dificultades, el cuñado de Blanca, Enrique II de Champaña, dejó una deuda muy elevada a la que ella n
2 CONC o pudo hacer frente después de la muerte de su esposo. Se cuestionó la legitimidad del infante en el momento de heredar el condado. Blanca fue desafiada por la hija de Enrique II, Felipa y su marido Erard de Brienne, uno de los co
2 CONC ndes más influyentes de la región. En 1215 se produjo la guerra entre los Brienne por la sucesión del condado, que no se resolvió hasta que Teobaldo II llegó a la mayoría de edad en 1222.
2 CONT
2 CONT Para finalizar las disputas Blanca tuvo que pagar una sustanciosa cantidad a los Brienne y asegurar su estabilidad. Se vio obligada a pactar la dote matrimonial de su sobrina Alicia, hija de Enrique II, al casarse en 1230 con el r
2 CONC ey de Chipre, vendiendo los derechos del condado de Blois, de Sancerre y Chateaudun al rey de Francia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Su hermano, Sancho VII de Navarra, fue el último rey de la dinastía Ximena. Tras la Batalla de las Navas de Tolosa, casado, pero sin hijos legítimos, se retiró a un convento y Blanca de Navarra tuvo que gobernar como regente el Re
2 CONC ino de Navarra. Tras la muerte de Berenguela de Navarra, que no tuvo descendencia de Ricardo Corazón de León, las Cortes de Navarra lideradas por el partido de los francos en confrontación con la Navarreria presionaron al anciano 
2 CONC Sancho VII para nombrar heredero suyo al hijo de Blanca, hermana de Berenguela.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Blanche de Navarre (1170s-1229) was Countess-consort of Champagne, then Regent of Champagne, and finally also regent of her native kingdom of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family She was the youngest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre (d. 1194) and Sancha of Castile. Her maternal grandparents were Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona. Her eldest brother Sancho VII of Navarre succe
2 CONC eded their father as king of Navarre, and was the last male descendant of the first dynasty of Kings of Navarre, the Pamplona dynasty, dying childless. Her brother Ramiro of Navarre was Bishop of Pamplona, and her elder sister Ber
2 CONC engaria of Navarre married Richard I of England and Aquitaine, the northern neighbour of their kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life Blanca married Count Theobald III of Champagne, who died young in 1201, leaving her pregnant. When she gave birth to a son, he immediately became count Theobald IV of Champagne (1201-53). Blanca ruled the county as reg
2 CONC ent until Theobald turned 21 in 1222. The regency was plagued by a number of difficulties. Blanca's brother-in-law, count Henry II had left behind a great deal of debt, which had not been paid off when Theobald III died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, their son Theobald IV's right to the succession of Champagne was challenged by Henry's daughter Philippa and her husband, Erard I of Brienne, Count of Ramerupt and one of the more powerful Champagne nobles. The confli
2 CONC ct with the Briennes broke into open warfare in 1215, in what became known as the Champagne War of Succession, and was not resolved until after Theobald came of age in 1222. At that time Theobald and Blanca bought out their rights
2 CONC  for a substantial monetary payment. Blanca had also arranged the dowry of Henry II's elder daughter Alice of Champagne, when she married the young Hugh I of Cyprus. In the 1230s, in order to settle with Alice, Theobald IV had to 
2 CONC sell his overlordship over the counties of Blois, Sancerre, and Chateaudun to Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca also took over administration of the kingdom of Navarre when her brother Sancho VII went into retirement ("El Encerrado"). Blanca died in 1229, her brother in retirement remaining as King of Navarre and her son Theobald con
2 CONC tinuing as Count of Champagne. Their eldest sister, Berengaria of Navarre, Queen of England, died without issue in 1232, leaving Sancho as the sole surviving child of Sancho VI. When he died in 1234, Blanca's son Theobald IV of Ch
2 CONC ampagne was recognised as the next King of Navarre. Theobald had married twice during Blanca's lifetime, but had not succeeded in producing children by the time of her death. However, he later had children by his third wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanca_S%C3%A1nchez_of_Navarre"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanca_Sánchez_of_Navarre
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Blanca Sánchez of Navarre From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT   (Redirected from Blanca of Navarre (daughter of Sancho VI)) For other persons of this name see Blanca of Navarre (disambiguation). Blanche de Navarre (1170s-1229) was Countess-consort of Champagne, then Regent of Champagne, and 
2 CONC finally also regent of her native kingdom of Navarre. She was the youngest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre (d. 1194) and Sancha of Castile. Her maternal grandparents were Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona. Her el
2 CONC dest brother Sancho VII of Navarre succeeded their father as king of Navarre. Her brother Ramiro of Navarre was Bishop of Pamplona, and her elder sister Berengaria of Navarre married Richard I of England and Aquitaine, the norther
2 CONC n neighbour of their kingdom. Blanca married Count Theobald III of Champagne, who died young in 1201, leaving her pregnant. When she gave birth to a son, he immediately became count Theobald IV of Champagne (1201-53). Blanca ruled
2 CONC  the county as regent until Theobald turned 21 in 1222. The regency was plagued by a number of difficulties. Blanca's brother-in-law, count Henry II had left behind a great deal of debt, which had not been paid off when Theobald I
2 CONC II died. Further, their son Theobald's right to the succession of Champagne was challenged by Henry's daughter Philippa and her husband, Erard I of Brienne, Count of Ramerupt and one of the more powerful Champagne nobles. The conf
2 CONC lict with the Briennes broke into open warfare in 1215, and was not resolved until after Theobald came of age in 1222. At that time Theobald and Blanca bought out their rights for a substantial monetary payment. Blanca had also ar
2 CONC ranged the dowry of Henry II's elder daughter Alice of Champagne, when she married the young Hugh I of Cyprus. In the 1230s, in order to settle with Alice, Theobald IV had to sell his overlordship over the counties of Blois, Sance
2 CONC rre, and Chateaudun to Louis IX of France. Blanca's brother Sancho VII of Navarre was the last male descendant of the first dynasty of Kings of Navarre, the Pamplona dynasty, and was childless. Sancho went into retirement ("El Enc
2 CONC errado") at some point, when Blanca took administration of the kingdom. Blanca died in 1229, her brother in retirement remaining as King of Navarre and her son Theobald continuing as Count of Champagne. Their eldest sister, Bereng
2 CONC aria of Navarre, Queen of England, died without issue in 1232, leaving Sancho as the sole surviving child of Sancho VI. When he died in 1234, Blanca's son Theobald IV of Champagne was recognised as the next King of Navarre. Theoba
2 CONC ld had married twice during Blanca's lifetime, but had not succeeded in producing children by the time of her death. However, he later had children by his third wife.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Blanche de Navarre (1170s-1229) was Countess-consort of Champagne, then Regent of Champagne, and finally also regent of her native kingdom of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the youngest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre (d. 1194) and Sancha of Castile. Her maternal grandparents were Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her eldest brother Sancho VII of Navarre succeeded their father as king of Navarre. Her brother Ramiro of Navarre was Bishop of Pamplona, and her elder sister Berengaria of Navarre married Richard I of England and Aquitaine, the n
2 CONC orthern neighbour of their kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca married Count Theobald III of Champagne, who died young in 1201, leaving her pregnant. When she gave birth to a son, he immediately became count Theobald IV of Champagne (1201-53). Blanca ruled the county as regent until Th
2 CONC eobald turned 21 in 1222.
2 CONT
2 CONT The regency was plagued by a number of difficulties. Blanca's brother-in-law, count Henry II had left behind a great deal of debt, which had not been paid off when Theobald III died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further, their son Theobald's right to the succession of Champagne was challenged by Henry's daughter Philippa and her husband, Erard I of Brienne, Count of Ramerupt and one of the more powerful Champagne nobles.
2 CONT
2 CONT The conflict with the Briennes broke into open warfare in 1215, and was not resolved until after Theobald came of age in 1222. At that time Theobald and Blanca bought out their rights for a substantial monetary payment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca had also arranged the dowry of Henry II's elder daughter Alice of Champagne, when she married the young Hugh I of Cyprus. In the 1230s, in order to settle with Alice, Theobald IV had to sell his overlordship over the counti
2 CONC es of Blois, Sancerre, and Chateaudun to Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca's brother Sancho VII of Navarre was the last male descendant of the first dynasty of Kings of Navarre, the Pamplona dynasty, and was childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho went into retirement ("El Encerrado") at some point, when Blanca took administration of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca died in 1229, her brother in retirement remaining as King of Navarre and her son Theobald continuing as Count of Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their eldest sister, Berengaria of Navarre, Queen of England, died without issue in 1232, leaving Sancho as the sole surviving child of Sancho VI. When he died in 1234, Blanca's son Theobald IV of Champagne was recognised as the n
2 CONC ext King of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald had married twice during Blanca's lifetime, but had not succeeded in producing children by the time of her death. However, he later had children by his third wife.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She was the youngest daughter of Sancho VI of Navarre (d. 1194) and Sancha of Castile. Her maternal grandparents were Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona. Her eldest brother Sancho VII of Navarre succeeded their fat
2 CONC her as king of Navarre, and was the last male descendant of the first dynasty of Kings of Navarre, the Pamplona dynasty, dying childless. Her brother Ramiro of Navarre was Bishop of Pamplona, and her elder sister Berengaria of Nav
2 CONC arre married Richard I of England and Aquitaine, the northern neighbour of their kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca married Count Theobald III of Champagne, who died young in 1201, leaving her pregnant. When she gave birth to a son, he immediately became count Theobald IV of Champagne (1201-53). Blanca ruled the county as regent until Th
2 CONC eobald turned 21 in 1222. The regency was plagued by a number of difficulties. Blanca's brother-in-law, count Henry II had left behind a great deal of debt, which had not been paid off when Theobald III died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthermore, their son Theobald IV's right to the succession of Champagne was challenged by Henry's daughter Philippa and her husband, Erard I of Brienne, Count of Ramerupt and one of the more powerful Champagne nobles. The confli
2 CONC ct with the Briennes broke into open warfare in 1215, in what became known as the Champagne War of Succession, and was not resolved until after Theobald came of age in 1222. At that time Theobald and Blanca bought out their rights
2 CONC  for a substantial monetary payment. Blanca had also arranged the dowry of Henry II's elder daughter Alice of Champagne, when she married the young Hugh I of Cyprus. In the 1230s, in order to settle with Alice, Theobald IV had to 
2 CONC sell his overlordship over the counties of Blois, Sancerre, and Chateaudun to Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca also took over administration of the kingdom of Navarre when her brother Sancho VII went into retirement ("El Encerrado"). Blanca died in 1229, her brother in retirement remaining as King of Navarre and her son Theobald con
2 CONC tinuing as Count of Champagne. Their eldest sister, Berengaria of Navarre, Queen of England, died without issue in 1232, leaving Sancho as the sole surviving child of Sancho VI. When he died in 1234, Blanca's son Theobald IV of Ch
2 CONC ampagne was recognised as the next King of Navarre. Theobald had married twice during Blanca's lifetime, but had not succeeded in producing children by the time of her death. However, he later had children by his third wife. 
