0 @N6000000002187750669@ NOTE Íñigo I of Pamplona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Íñigo I Íñiguez Arista, Eneko Enekones Aritza/Haritza/Aiza, Wannaqo ibn Wannaqo (Arabic: ???? ?? ?????) (c. 790 – 851 or 852). In Basque Aritza means oak. Eneko Aritza was the first King of Pamplona (c. 824 – 851 or 852). He was a
1 CONC lso apparently count of Bigorre and Sobrarbe.[citation needed] His origin is obscure, but his patronymic indicates that he was the son of an Íñigo.[1] It has been speculated that he was kinsman of García Jiménez, who in the late 8
1 CONC th century succeeded his father Jimeno in resisting Carolingian expansion into Vasconia. He is also speculated to have been related to the other Navarrese dynasty, the Jiménez.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother also married Musa ibn Fortún ibn Qasi, by whom she was mother of Musa ibn Musa ibn Qasi, head of the Banu Qasi and Moslem king of Tudela, one of the chief lords of Valley of the Ebro.[3] Due to this relationship, Íñigo 
1 CONC and his kin frequently acted in alliance with Musa ibn Musa and this relationship allowed Eneko to extend his influence over large territories in the Pyrenean valleys.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family came to power through struggles over of Frankish and Muslim influence in Spain. In 799, pro-Frankish assassins murdered Mutarrif ibn Musa, governor of Pamplona, the brother of Musa ibn Musa ibn Qasi. In 824, the Frankis
1 CONC h counts Aeblus and Aznar Sánchez made another expedition against Pamplona. This led Eneko to overthrow the Frankish underlings.[citation needed] He was pronounced "King of Pamplona" in that city by the people. Eneko was a Christi
1 CONC colae princeps (Christian prince), according to Eulogio de Córdoba.[citation needed] However, his kingdom combined both Moslem and Christian to maintain independence against outside powers. All was not peace between the Moors and 
1 CONC the Basques, however.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 841, Eneko fell victim to paralysis in battle against the Norse with Musa ibn Musa.[citation needed] His son García acted as regent, in concert with Fortún Íñiguez, "the premier knight of the realm", the king's brother and also
1 CONC  half-brother of Musa. They joined Musa ibn Musa in an uprising against the Caliphate of Córdoba. Abd-ar-Rahman II, emir of Córdoba, launched reprisal campaigns in the succeeding years. In 843, Fortún Íñiguez was killed, and Musa 
1 CONC unhorsed and forced to escape on foot, while Eneko and his son Galindo escaped with wounds and several nobleman, most notably Velasco Garcés defected to Abd-ar-Rahman. The next year, Eneko's own son, Galindo Íñiguez and Musa's son
1 CONC  Lubb ibn Musa went over to Córdoba, and Musa was forced to submit. Following a brief campaign the next year, 845, a general peace was achieved.[4] In 850, Musa again rose in open rebellion, supported again by Pamplona,[5] and env
1 CONC oys of Induo (thought to be Eneko) and Mitio,[6] "Dukes of the Navarrese", were received at the French court. Eneko died in the Muslim year 237, which is late 851 or early 852, and was succeeded by García Íñiguez.[7]
1 CONT
1 CONT The name of the wife (or wives) of Eneko is not reported in contemporary records, although chronicles from centuries later assign her the name of Toda or Oneca.[8] There is also scholarly debate regarding her derivation, some hypo
1 CONC thesizing that she was daughter of Velasco, lord of Pamplona (killed 816), and others making her kinswoman of Aznar I Galíndez[9]. He was father of the following known children:[10]
1 CONT
1 CONT Assona Íñiguez, who married her father's half-brother, Musa ibn Musa ibn Fortún ibn Qasi, lord of Tudela and Huesca 
1 CONT García Íñiguez, the future king 
1 CONT Galindo Íñiguez, fled to Córdoba where he was father of Musa ibn Galindo, Wali of Huesca in 860, assassinated in 870 in Córdoba 
1 CONT a daughter, wife of Count García el Malo (the Bad) of Aragón. 
1 CONT The dynasty founded by Eneko reigned for about 80 years, being supplanted by a rival dynasty in 905. However, due to intermarriages, subsequent kings of Navarre descend from Eneko.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Lacarra de Miguel, José María. "Textos navarros del Códice de Roda". Estudios de Edad Media de la Corona de Aragon. 1:194-283 (1945). 
1 CONT Lévi-Provençal, Evariste. "Du nouveau sur le Royaume de Pampelune au IXe Siècle". Bulletin Hispanique. 55:5-22 (1953). 
1 CONT Lévi-Provençal, Evariste and Emilio García Gómez. "Textos inéditos del Muqtabis de Ibn Hayyan sobre los orígines del Reino de Pamplona". Al-Andalus. 19:295-315 (1954). 
1 CONT Mello Vaz de São Payo, Luiz. "A Ascendência de D. Afonso Henriques". Raízes & Memórias 6:23-57 (1990). 
1 CONT Pérez de Urbel, Justo. "Lo viejo y lo nuevo sobre el origin del Reino de Pamplona". Al-Andalus. 19:1-42 (1954). 
1 CONT Sánchez Albernoz, Claudio. "Problemas de la historia Navarra del siglo IX". Princípe de Viana, 20:5-62 (1959). 
1 CONT Settipani, Christian. La Noblesse du Midi Carolingien, Occasional Publiucations of the Unit for Prosopographical Research, Vol. 5. (2004) 
1 CONT Stasser, Thierry. "Consanguinity et Alliances Dynastiques en Espagne au Haut Moyen Age: La Politique Matrimoniale de la Reinne Tota de Navarre". Hidalguia. No. 277: 811-39 (1999) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Lacarra 
1 CONT ^ Lacarra 
1 CONT ^ Íñigo and brother Fortún are explicitly called brother of Musa ibn Musa on their mother's side by chroncler Ibn Hayyan. The order of the maternal marriages has been subject to speculation, with Lévi-Provençal and Pérez de Urbel 
1 CONC having the widowed mother of Íñigo marrying Musà ibn Fortún, while Sánchez Albernoz argued that the Christian marriage came after the Muslim. 
1 CONT ^ Lévi-Provençal and García Gómez; Sánchez Albernoz 
1 CONT ^ ibid 
1 CONT ^ Identified by Pérez de Urbel with Jimeno I, but Sánchez Albernoz rejects this. 
1 CONT ^ Lévi-Provençal and García Gómez; Sánchez Albernoz. It has been suggested that either Jimeno I or his son García Jiménez served as regent following the death of Íñigo I, but there is no evidence of this. 
1 CONT ^ Settipani 
1 CONT ^ Mello Vaz de São Payo;Stasser. These identifications are based on the names given in subsequent generations, but Sánchez Albernoz wrote of the danger of assuming such name usage demonstrate specific familial linkages. 
1 CONT ^ Lacarra;Lévi-Provençal and García Gómez; Sánchez Albernoz 
1 CONT
1 CONT
