1 CONT 824–851/852 Succeeded by
1 CONT García Íñiguez 
0 @N6000000002187801328@ NOTE Íñigo I of Pamplona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Íñigo I Íñiguez, called Arista in Spanish and Aritza or Aiza in Basque (his Basque name was Eneko {Íñigo}) (c.781–852) was the first king of Pamplona (810 or 824-851 or 852). He was also apparently count of Bigorre and Sobrarbe. H
1 CONC e was the son of Íñigo Jiménez and Oneca. His elder brother (or kinsmen of some sort), García Jiménez, held a veritable principality in Vasconia. He was the father of the other Navarrese dynasty, the Jiménez. This was a time of co
1 CONC nflict with the Vikings, according to Moorish chronicles Calixto Garcia Iñiguez (Vasque Enekones=Castillian Iñiguez) (son of Iñigo Aritza [1], known as XXX in these chronicles) was captured by these northlanders and released for a
1 CONC n immense ransom [2]; later [3] Muza Ben Muza (son in law of Iñigo Arista) allies with Iñigo Arista defeated these Norsemen in battle, although Iñigo was paralized in this battle [4].
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his father, his mother married secondly Musá ibn Musá ibn Fortún of the Banu Qasi, Moslem king of Tudela, one of the chief lords of Valley of the Ebro. This marriage made Eneko influential over large territories
1 CONC  in the Pyrenean valleys.
1 CONT
1 CONT The family of Velasco was the chief rival of Eneko and the Banu Qasi and the chiefest of allies of the Franks in Spain. In 799, pro-Frankish assassins murdered Mutarrif ibn Muza, governor of Pamplona, who belonged to the Banu Qasi
1 CONC . A Velasco moved into the vacuum and the Frankish influence spread westwards in Iberia. In 824, the Frankish counts Elbe and Aznar made another expedition against Pamplona. This led Eneko to overthrow the Frankish underlings. He 
1 CONC was pronounced "King of Pamplona" in that city by the people. Eneko was a Christicolae princeps (Christian prince), according to Eulogio de Córdoba. However, his kingdom combined both Moslem and Christian to maintain independence 
1 CONC against outside powers. All was not peace between the Moors and the Basques, however. Abd-ar-Rahman II, emir of Córdoba, made reprisals against Pamplona.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 841, Eneko fell victim to paralysis. His son García acted as regent.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eneko died in 851 or 852, and apparently was succeeded by his kinsman Jimeno Garcés, lord of Álava, and apparently a ruler in Vasconia. Eneko, however, also left a son, the aforementioned García, who managed to become the sole kin
1 CONC g in 860. The Basque dynasty (in the form of the Jiménez line) ruled Navarre from the 9th century to 1234. Eneko's own male line lost the throne in 905. Later kings of Pamplona were Eneko's descendants through the female line.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eneko married Oneca Velázquez, daughter of Velasco, lord of Pamplona, and had following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Assona Íñiguez, wife of Musá ibn Musá ibn Fortún, lord of Tudela and Huesca 
1 CONT García Íñiguez, the future king 
1 CONT Galindo Íñiguez, father of Musa ibn Galindo, Wali of Huesca in 860, assassinated in 870 in Córdoba 
1 CONT Nunila, wife of Count García el Malo (the Bad) of Aragón. 
1 CONT As a trivial and interesting irony of history, it may be noted that the famous general of the Cuban Wars of Independence, Calixto García Íñiguez, [5] was a descendent on his mother's side of Íñigo Arista, and was held captive in, 
1 CONC and escaped from, Pamplona in the 19th Century.
1 CONT
1 CONT New Title King of Pamplona
1 CONT 824–851 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Jimeno I 
0 @N6000000003051124410@ NOTE He was leader of the revolt against the Franks, and elected King.
0 @N6000000003949741623@ NOTE
1 CONT Todd A. Farmerie  Newsgroups: soc.genealogy.medieval Date: Thu, 24 Mar 2005 16:09:05 -0700
1 CONT Subject: Re: Muslim ancestors of Gateway Ancestors
1 CONT No implication concerning religion can be made based solely on him being a Basque, and if any were to be reached, this would be the wrong one. This was on the boundary of muslim control, and it was a patchwork of religious prefere
1 CONC nces.  Aznar Galindez, another Basque leader, was almost certainly christian (closely allied with Louis the Pious), as were the Basque leaders to the north (better known as Gascons).  The local Romano-Basque Banu Qasi were muslim,
1 CONC  but they seem to have been the exception.  Further, unlike the Banu Qasi, which used a mizture of muslim and christian names, the names found in this family - Inigo, Fortun, Garcia, Sancho, Velasco, Oneca, Jimena, Sancha, Toda, U
1 CONC rraca, Aznar (even Auria) are all Basque or Latin in origin.  What we know is that Inigo Arista was maternal half-brother of Muza ibn Muza ibn Qasi, but we do not know the religion of their common mother, nor even the order of mar
1 CONC riage (although based on the death of Muza ibn Fortun in 788, it seems likely that Inigo was the second husband, but this is not without problems either (his grandson was active on an embassy in the 840s.  It is reported in muslim
1 CONC  sources that Inigo suffered from paralysis (it is unclear if it was traumatic or stroke-related) and his son Garcia was militarily active from the early 840s, perhaps in lieu of his father.  A brother (of Inigo and Muza) Fortun I
1 CONC niguez was a well respected leader, but was killed about this time.  Inigo's death is reported in (IIRC) in the muslim year that spanned 851/52
