0 @N6000000002187931074@ NOTE Raymond II of Rouergue
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Raymond II (also Ramon or Raimundo and sometimes numberred Raymond I) (died 961 or 965) was the count of Rouergue and Quercy from 937 to his death. Under Raymond, Rouergue achieved a suzerainty over neighbouring counties and he su
1 CONC ccessfully titled himself marchio (margrave) of Septimania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Raymond's lordship extended over Albi and Nîmes and, at least around 960, as far north as the Limousin. Raymond was the head of his family, which also ruled Toulouse. Even in his time, his family appeared to be declining. He wille
1 CONC d a Gascon fief to Duke Sancho V and allowed it to become allodial after his death. Nonetheless, his power was such that he could command lands as far north as Auvergne and was the most powerful lord of Aquitaine, even holding a "
1 CONC vicar's court" in the Limousin.
1 CONT
1 CONT His will of 961 is presereved. In it, not only does he reference the aforementioned lands in Auvergne and Gascony, but he mentions seventeen castles and a rocheta. Some castles were given to his wife and heirs and some to the chur
1 CONC ches of Albi and Cahors and to various abbeys.
1 CONT
1 CONT Raymond married Bertha, daughter of Boso of Tuscany. He was succeeded by his son Raymond III.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Lewis, Archibald R. The Development of Southern French and Catalan Society, 718-1050. [1] [2] 
