0 @N6000000000437438592@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Viscount
0 @N6000000000437050244@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Count Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of Barcelona, Girona And Osana
0 @N6000000000945078089@ NOTE Greve av Barcelona 912 - 954. 
1 CONT Ved sin fars død arvet han antagelig brevskapet Besalù og senere, ved sin bror Wifred
1 CONT II's død, også grevskapet Barcelona i 912. Det sies at han styrte grevskapet i 40 år.
1 CONT Enkelte forfattere forveksler Sunier med hans bror, Sunifredo, greve av Urgel, og med
1 CONT hans sønn, Borrell II.
1 CONT Sunier lot bygge borgen Olérandulo (Olérdola) i 929. Av en donasjon han gjorde i 934 til
1 CONT kirken i Gerona, fremgår det at han også var greve av Gerona på den tid.
1 CONT I 945-46 overlot han regjeringen til sine sønner Borrell og Miron, og gikk i kloster.
0 @N6000000002187927874@ NOTE Sunifred II, Count of Barcelona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sunifred II (c.870-950) or, in Spanish, Suñer II and, in Catalan, Sunyer II, was count of Barcelona, Gerona, Urgell and Ausona from 911 to 948. He was the son of Wilfred the Hairy and younger brother of his predecessor in Barcelon
1 CONC a, Wilfred II Borrel.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was associated with his brother in the government of their father's counties after their father's death (897). He did not reign independently until his brother's death (911), however.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of his uncle, Count Radulf I of Besalú, in 913 or 920, there emerged a conflict between Sunifred and his brother Count Miró II of Cerdagne over the succession to the county of Besalú. In exchange for total renunciat
1 CONC ion of all claims on Barcelona, Sunifred gave up his on Besalú.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 925, Sunifred married for the second time to Richilda of Tolouse, daughter of the count of Rouergue, with whom he had four sons and a daughter: Ermengol (925), Miró (926), Borrel (927), Adelaide (928), and William (929).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the domestic political arena, Sunifred protected the church and strengthened her institutions, conceding her territories and tributes. He stimulated the repopulation of Ausona.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the realm of foreign affairs, he abandoned defensive actions and took up the fight actively against the Moors. He fought battles with them in Lérida and Tarragona. At the same time, he managed to retain diplomatic relations wit
1 CONC h Córdoba, repopulating the county of Penedés as far as Olérdola (929).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 912, the Moorish governor of Lérida attacked and destroyed the Barcelonan army under Sunifred in the valley of Tárrega. But in 914, Sunifred's counterattack successfully pushed them back again.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the intervening period, 936 to 937, he lead an expedition against the Moslems of the littoral. It appears to have been a success: the Moslem dead were excessive and the quadi of Valencia was among them. The Moors temporaril
1 CONC y abandoned Tarragona (then converted into a no-man's land) and were forced to pay tribute to Tortosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 947, he retired to the monastic life and ceded the government of the realms to his sons: Borrell II and Miró I.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in the Monastery of La Grassa (in Conflent) in 950.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Wilfred II Count of Barcelona Succeeded by:
1 CONT Borrel II
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Wilfred I Count of Urgell Succeeded by:
1 CONT Miró I
0 @N6000000002187927947@ NOTE Wilfred II Borrel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Wilfred II or Borrel I (Guifré II Borrell I in Catalan) was count of Barcelona, Gerona, and Ausona from 897 to 911, after his father, Wilfred the Hairy. His mother was Guinedilda.
1 CONT
1 CONT At his father's death, the patrimony were divided between his sons Wilfred, Sunifred, and Miró. Upon Wilfred's death in 911, his counties passed to Sunyer, his younger brother who was a minor at their father's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT none Count of Barcelona
1 CONT 897–911 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sunyer II
1 CONT Count of Gerona
1 CONT 897–911
1 CONT Count of Ausona
