0 @N6000000002187927414@ NOTE Chintila
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Chintila was king of the Visigoths in Hispania (636-639/640). He succeeded Sisenand in a time of weakness and reigned until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was elected and confirmed by a convention of bishops and nobles in accordance with the 75th canon of the IV Council of Toledo. With his election, nothing changed and instability reigned. He never solved the many problems which 
1 CONC plagued his time in office and, as the chroniclers of the age tell us, this included rebellions in Septimania and Galicia. In the three years of his reign, he permitted the bishops wide authority and they were the monarchs de fact
1 CONC o, if not de jure.
1 CONT
1 CONT He dedicated his time to councils, the V Council of Toledo in June 636 and the VI Council of Toledo in June 638. They coverred many topics and legistaled many new regulations. The king had to be chosen from among the nobility; nev
1 CONC er a tonsurado (cleric), member of the servil classes (peasants), or foreigners. They dictated the penalties for insurrection and determined that property acquired justly by the king could not be confiscated by his successor. Fina
1 CONC lly, they outlawed noncatholics within the frontiers of the kingdom, which resulted in many forced conversions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Chintila died in 639 or 640 of natural causes and was followed by Tulga.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sisenand King of the Visigoths
1 CONT 636–640 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Tulga
