0 @N6000000003949681037@ NOTE Geoffroy appeared in a charter of Franco, bishop of Paris, dated 26 May 1028, at which time he and his full brother Liétaud were called the heirs of their maternal half-brother Aubri, count of Gâtinais [see discussion below]. At s
1 CONC ome point after that, he succeeded as count of Gâtinais (Château-Landon). He was evidently still living in 1042, and deceased by 1045. His marriage to Ermengarde, the heiress of Anjou, led to the acquisition of Anjou by his sons, 
1 CONC making him a direct male-line ancestor of the "Plantagenet" dynasty. For "Férreol" as a possible nickname of Geoffroy, see below under his possible daughter who married Joscelin I de Courtenay. Because of different interpretations
1 CONC  of the evidence, there is no "standard" numbering of the counts of Gâtinais named Geoffroy. The numbering here follows the outline of Settipani (1997), 233-4
0 @N6000000005076814789@ NOTE Geoffrey, Comte de Gâtinais gained the title of Comte de Gâtinais.1
1 CONT Child of Geoffrey, Comte de Gâtinais
1 CONT Hildegarde de Gâtinais+ 1
