1 CONT He became Duke of Gascony in 1052 during his older brother William VII's rule. Gascony had come to Aquitanian rule through William V's marriage to Prisca (a.k.a Brisce) of Gascony, the sister of Duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony.
1 CONT
1 CONT William VIII was one of the leaders of the allied army called to help Ramiro I of Aragon in the Siege of Barbastro (1064). This expedition was the first campaign organized by the papacy, namely Pope Alexander II, against a Muslim 
1 CONC city, and the precursor of the later Crusades movement. Aragon and its allies conquered the city, killed its inhabitants and collected an important booty.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Aragon lost the city again in the following years. During William VIII's rule, the alliance with the southern kingdoms of modern Spain was a political priority as shown by the marriage of all his daughters to Iberian king
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT He married three times and had at least five children. After he divorced his second wife due to infertility, he remarried to a much younger woman who was also his cousin. This marriage produced a son, but William VIII had to visit
1 CONC  Rome in the early 1070s to persuade the pope to recognize his children from his third marriage as legitimate.
1 CONT
1 CONT First wife: Garsende of Périgord, daughter of Count Aldabert II of Périgord and his wife Alausie, herself the second daughter of duke Sans VI Guilhem of Gascony (divorced November 1058), no children. She became a nun at Saintes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Second wife: Matoeda (divorced May 1068)
1 CONT Agnes (1052-1078), married Alfonso VI of Castile
1 CONT
1 CONT Third wife: Hildegarde of Burgundy (daughter of duke Robert I of Burgundy)
1 CONT Agnes (d.1097), married Peter I of Aragon
1 CONT William IX of Aquitaine, his heir
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_VIII_of_Aquitaine>
