0 @N6000000002187911584@ NOTE Hugh VI of Lusignan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh VI of Lusignan (c. 1039 – 1110), known as "le diable" (The Devil) was the son of Hugh V of Lusignan and Almodis de la Marche, and a participant in the First Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite his piety, Hugh was in constant conflict with the abbey of St. Maixent. On numerous occasions his disputes with the monks grew so violent that the duke of Aquitaine, the bishops of Poitiers and Saintes, and Pope Paschal II
1 CONC  were forced to intervene. From these conflicts Hugh was dubbed "le diable", the devil, by the monks of St. Maixent.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1086 the Castilian army was destroyed in battle by the Almoravids. Hugh's Catalan half-brother, Berenguer Ramon II, Count of Barcelona was threatened by the Almoravids. Hugh VI undertook an expedition to Spain in 1087 along wit
1 CONC h another half-brother, Raymond IV of Toulouse, to assist the count of Barcelona.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh took the cross for the First Crusade, along with his brothers Raymond and Berenguer. He participated in the Crusade of 1101.
1 CONT
1 CONT From his marriage to Ildégarde de Thouars, Hugh had a son and successor, Hugh VII of Lusignan.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Boso III Count of La Marche
1 CONT 1091–1110 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hugh VII
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Riley Smith, Jonathan. The First Crusaders, 1095-1131, 1998
