0 @N6000000002187750384@ NOTE Ramiro II of Aragon
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramiro II of Aragon 'the Monk' (ca. 1075 - August 16, 1154, at Huesca) was king of Aragon from 1134 until 1137.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1134, when his brother Alfonso the Battler died heirless, Ramiro was bishop of Barbastro-Roda. He temporarily gave up his monastic vows in order to secure the succession to the crown. Although Ramiro had to put up with the loss
1 CONC  of Navarre, which had formed part of his late brother's dominions but in 1134 became independent under García Ramírez, he fought off two other claimants to the throne, one Pedro de Atarés, an illegitimate connection of the royal 
1 CONC line, and the other, Alfonso VII, king of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT The reign of Ramiro the Monk, as he is known, only lasted three years. However, in that short time he managed to put down the rebellion of his nobles. In order to produce an heir, he married Agnes, daughter of Duke William IX of A
1 CONC quitaine. Once wed, his wife bore a daughter, Petronila, who was betrothed to Ramon Berenguer IV at age two. Immediately following this betrothal, Ramiro abdicated in her favour and returned to monastic life.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alfonso I King of Aragon
1 CONT 1134-1137 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Petronila 
0 @N6000000002176683818@ NOTE Monk and bishop-elect of Barbastro when he succeeded elder brother Alfonso I.
1 CONT With the aid of Ramon Berenguer IV, the revolt of the Aragonese nobles was
1 CONT crushed in 1135. He later abdicated on November 13, 1137 in favor of his
1 CONT daughter and husband very shortly after they were married on August 11th of
1 CONT that year.
