0 @N6000000004533941842@ NOTE Complete Peerage, Appendix I.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Peverel the younger, one of the principal supportors of KINGSTEPHEN, was a commander at the Battle of the Standard and was takenprisoner at the Battle of Lincoln. His estates were forfeited for atime, and his castle of Not
1 CONC tingham was committed by THE EMPRESS [MAUD]to William Paynel. He recovered it in 1143. His wives were Odonna andAVICE DE LANCASTRIA, who was presumably a daughter of Count Roger thePoitevin (cognomine Pictaviensis), Lord of the ho
1 CONC nour of Lancaster, byhis wife Aumodis, Countess of La Marche.  He had a son Henry, and adaughter, Margaret, eventually, or in her issue, his heir, and a wifeof Robert, Earl of Ferrers.
1 CONT
1 CONT About March 1152/3 HENRY, Duke of Normandy [the future King HENRY II]and count of Anjou, by a charter expedited at Devizes, formulated whathe was prepared to give to [RANULF DE GERNON] the Earl of Chester, asthe price of his suppo
1 CONC rt; not indeed, even to the half of the kingdom,but yet no inconsiderable portion of it. It ought to be unnecessary tohave to state that these extensive grants never took effect.   Ninemonths afterwards, in Dec. 1153, THE EARL die
1 CONC d, poisoned, as men said,by William Peverel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Shortly after his accession to the throne, KING HENRY II visitedNottinghamshire, in order, as the annalists state, to disinheritWilliam Peverel for having poisoned THE EARL, but more probably topunish him for what had previously b
1 CONC een termed his wickedness andtreason. William, on THE KING's approach, retired to one of hisreligious foundations where he became a monk. The honor of Peverelremained in the Crown for nearly half a century.  [Complete Peerage,Appe
1 CONC ndix I, pp. 762-5 (English/non-Latin portion of text)]
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The following information was contained in a post-em by Curt Hofemann,curt_hofemann@yaoo.com:
1 CONT
1 CONT of Nottingham, the younger; one of the chief supporters of KingStephen [Ref: Watney #779]
1 CONT 1137: a commander at the battle of The Standard [Ref: Watney #779]
1 CONT
1 CONT Peverel, William. Perhaps illegitimate son of William I by hismistress; took name of Peverel from stepfather who married her. Largeholdings in Notts. and Derby. Also in 6 other counties. [Ref: DomesdayOnline: http://www.domesdaybo
1 CONC ok.co.uk/index.html]
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Willelm Peurel (sic) de Nottingham, Keats-Rohan has this to say:Norman, major tenant-in-chief centred upon Nottinghamshire andDerbyshire. There is no direct evidence of any relationship withRanulf Peverel . . . but it is highly
1 CONC  likely that they were relatedand possibly quite closely. . . . By his wife Adeline he had issue twosons named William of whom one predeceased him and a daughter Adeliz .. . died 1113/4 . . . on the honour of Peverel see HKF i, 14
1 CONC 6ff.[Honors and Knights' Fees, W. Farrer, 3 vols, Manchester, 1923-5][Ref: Renia Simmonds <renia.s@ntlworld.com> 18 Mar 2002 message toGen-Medieval]
1 CONT
1 CONT [Note: the above text from CP, Appendix I (as transcribed by JimStevens) was attached here, with several omissions and mistakes.  Ihave corrected the text to agree with what is stated in CP and placedit above.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Cokayne's "Complete Peerage" (Derby, p. 192), identifies him asMARGARET's father. [Ref: Jim Stevens:http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~jast/index.html]  Note: Ihave removed Jim's "RIN" (ID #'s) from the above & there are sev
1 CONC eralsentences that may have been miscopied/reworded as they don't makesense = see where I have added (sic)... Curt
1 CONT
1 CONT Regards,
1 CONT Curt
