0 @N6000000002187911467@ NOTE Hugh VIII of Lusignan
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh VIII of Lusignan was the eldest son of Hugh VII and of Sarrasine de Lezay. He became lord of Lusignan and Count of La Marche on his father's death in 1151. Born some time after 1125, he died in 1171.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Bourgogne de Rancon, dame de Fontenay, daughter of Geoffroi Fossessie, seigneur de Taillebourg: she died in 1169. In 1164 he went on pilgrimage to the Holy Land and participated in the Battle of Harim, where he was take
1 CONC n prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT His children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Hugues, who died in 1169, leaving an infant son Hugues who was to become Hugh IX of Lusignan.
1 CONT     Amalric, born about 1145, died 1205. He succeeded his younger brother Guy as ruler of Cyprus; later he was crowned king of Cyprus, the first of the Lusignan dynasty, and eventually also became king of Jerusalem.
1 CONT     Guy, died 1194. He was regent and afterwards king of Jerusalem. After the loss of Jerusalem he became lord of Cyprus.
1 CONT     Raoul I of Issoudun, born 1160, died (at Acre, Palestine) in 1217.
1 CONT     Pierre, born after 1162.
1 CONT     Guillaume, born after 1163. 
1 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Battle of Harim
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Battle of Harim
1 CONT Part of the Crusades
1 CONT Date: August 12, 1164
1 CONT Location: Harim
1 CONT Result: Crusader defeat
1 CONT Combatants
1 CONT Aleppo, Mosul Principality of Antioch,
1 CONT County of Tripoli
1 CONT Commanders
1 CONT Nur ad-Din Raymond III of Tripoli,
1 CONT Bohemund III of Antioch,
1 CONT Joscelin III of Edessa,
1 CONT Hugh VIII of Lusignan
1 CONT Strength
1 CONT Unknown Unknown
1 CONT Casualties
1 CONT Unknown 10 000?
1 CONT Raymond, Bohemund, Joscelin, and Hugh taken captive
1 CONT
1 CONT The Battle of Harim was fought on August 12, 1164 between the forces of Nur ad-Din and the armies of the County of Tripoli and the Principality of Antioch.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1163 King Amalric I of Jerusalem led an invasion of Egypt, leaving the crusader states open to attack from the east. Nur ad-Din took advantage of this to invade Tripoli, but he was taken by surprise by the Knights Templar at al
1 CONC -Buqaia and was almost killed himself. He then moved north to Antioch, with assistance from his brother Qutb ad-Din in Mosul, his other vassals from Aleppo and Damascus, and the Ortoqids of the Jazira, and besieged the fortress of
1 CONC  Harim in 1164. As William of Tyre says, "he stationed his engines around it in the customary manner and began to assault the place with a fury which permitted the inhabitants no rest."
1 CONT
1 CONT Reginald of St. Valery, lord of Harim, called for help, and Raymond III of Tripoli, Bohemund III of Antioch, and Joscelin III of Edessa arrived to relieve the siege. They were joined by Constantine-Colman, the Byzantine governor o
1 CONC f Cilicia, and Thoros and Mleh of Armenia, as well as Godfrey Martel, brother of William IV of Angoulême, and Hugh VIII of Lusignan, both of whom had recently arrived on pilgrimage. Nur ad-Din prepared to give up the siege when th
1 CONC ey arrived, but the crusaders, inspired by the victory at al-Buqaia, and "regardless of the rules of military discipline...recklessly dispersed and roved hither and yon in pursuit of the foe." Nur ad-Din's troops defended against 
1 CONC their charge and led a counterattack, pushing the crusaders into a swamp, and they were massacred "like victims before the altar." It is possible that Nur ad-Din was only feigning a retreat in order to draw the crusaders into an a
1 CONC mbush, but abandoning a siege when a relief army arrived was a standard tactic and Nur ad-Din presumably had no way of knowing the crusaders would follow him; William's assertion that this was a reckless move is further evidence o
1 CONC f this. Thoros and Mleh, who had advised against an attack, fled the field, but Colman, Hugh, Raymond, Bohemund, and Joscelin were captured and imprisoned in Aleppo. According to Ibn al-Athir, 10 000 crusaders were killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nur ad-Din resumed the siege and captured Harim a few days later. With Amalric absent in Egypt, all three crusader states were now without their rulers, but Nur ad-Din did not want to attack Antioch itself for fear of provoking a 
1 CONC Byzantine response, as the Principality was technically an Imperial fief. Nur ad-Din went on to besiege Banias, and Amalric returned home in 1165 to relieve Nur ad-Din's pressure on Antioch. Bohemund was released from captivity in
1 CONC  1165 but Raymond remained in prison until 1173.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Steven Runciman, A History of the Crusades, vol. II: The Kingdom of Jerusalem. Cambridge University Press, 1952.
1 CONT     * William of Tyre, A History of Deeds Done Beyond the Sea, trans. E.A. Babcock and A.C. Krey. Columbia University Press, 1943.
1 CONT     * Amin Maalouf, The Crusades Through Arab Eyes, trans. Jon Rothschild. Al-Saqi, 1984.
1 CONT     * R.C. Smail, Crusading Warfare, 1097-1193. Cambridge University Press, 1956 (2nd ed., 1995).
0 @N6000000003949823593@ NOTE Hugh VIII of Lusignan, who departed for Palestine in 1163 and was captured in 1164 at the battle of harim, died in the Holy land (Letter of Amauri,  patriarch of Antioch, to King Louis VII of France, in Recueil des Historiens  des
1 CONC  Gaules et de la France, vol XVI, 61-62). He married Burgundia  (Bourgogne), daughter of Geoffroy of Rancon, who witnessed a charter to the  abbey of Nouaillé with their sons Hugh, Robert, Geoffrey and Peter (Chartes de l¹abbaye d
1 CONC e Nouaillé de 578 à 1200, in Archives Historiques du Poitou,  vol 49, p 333-334).
1 CONT  After his father departure, Hugh, the eldest son, acted as lord of Lusignan.  He gave a charter to the abbey of Becheron with the consent of Geoffrey his brother and Burgundia his mother (Cartulaire de l¹abbaye de Notre Dame de l
1 CONC a  Merci dieu autrement dite de becheron, in Archives Historiques du Poitou,
1 CONT  vol 34, p 66) and in a grant to the abbey of absie he is called Hugh son of  Burgundia (Cartulaires et chartes de l¹abbaye de l¹Absie, in Archives  Historiques du Poitou, vol 25, p 129-130). He died before March 1170 : his  broth
1 CONC er Geoffrey made a grant to the abbey of Absie in the presence of his mother Burgundia, for the soul of Hugh who had died earlier (ibidem, p  132-133). 
