0 @N6000000003494695919@ NOTE The following is from the Grolier Multimedia Encyclopedia Online. Grolier Interactive Inc. <http://gi.grolier.com/encyclopedial>. January 2, 1998:
1 CONT
1 CONT Aquitaine (Latin: Aquitania), an area roughly corresponding to the southwestern third of France, was one of the three divisions of ancient Gaul. After 500 years of Roman rule, it fell to the Visigoths in the 5th century and then t
1 CONC o the Franks when they defeated the Visigoths in 507. The Franks rarely maintained strong control over Aquitaine, and in about 725 it was raided by the Muslim conquerors of Spain. The Frankish leader Charles Martel crushed these i
1 CONC nvaders in 733, and Aquitaine became part of the Carolingian empire. 
1 CONT In the 9th century the leading counts and other nobility gradually freed themselves of royal control. Bernard Plantevelue (r. 868-86) and his son, William I (r. 886-918), whose power was based in Auvergne, called themselves dukes 
1 CONC of Aquitaine, but their state disintegrated. William V (r. 995-1030) founded a new duchy of Aquitaine based in Poitou. It reached its zenith under William VIII (r. 1058-86). When William X died (1137), his daughter Eleanor of Aqui
1 CONC taine married Louis VII of France, whom she divorced in 1152 to marry Henry II of England. She maintained an elegant chivalric court at Poitiers. Her sons, Richard I and John, and their successors as kings of England were dukes of
1 CONC  Aquitaine (later known as Guienne). 
1 CONT The French conquered Poitou in 1224 and other parts of Aquitaine in the next century. English victories during the Hundred Years' War enabled Edward III to reconstruct the old duchy in the 1360s, but France finally conquered the r
1 CONC emainder of it in 1453. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Author:  John B. Henneman
