0 @N6000000000232618552@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulk_of_Jerusalem
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulk of Jerusalem
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Fulk
1 CONT Count of Anjou, King of Jerusalem
1 CONT Reign 1131-1143
1 CONT Coronation 1131
1 CONT Born 1089/92
1 CONT Died 13 November 1143
1 CONT Place of death Acre
1 CONT Buried Church of the Holy Sepulchre Jerusalem
1 CONT Predecessor Baldwin II
1 CONT Successor Melisende
1 CONT Baldwin III
1 CONT Consort Melisende (1105-1164)
1 CONT Issue With Ermengarde of Maine
1 CONT Geoffrey (1113–1151)
1 CONT Sibylla (1112–1165)
1 CONT Alice (1107–1154
1 CONT Elias (11??-1151)
1 CONT With Melisende of Jerusalem
1 CONT Baldwin (1130–1162)
1 CONT Amalric (1136–1174)
1 CONT Father Fulk IV of Anjou (1043–1109)
1 CONT Mother Bertrade de Montfort (c.1070-1117)
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulk V (1089/1092 – November 13, 1143), also known as Fulk the Younger, was Count of Anjou from 1109 to 1129, and King of Jerusalem from 1131 to his death.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Count of Anjou
1 CONT     * 2 Crusader and King
1 CONT     * 3 Securing the borders
1 CONT     * 4 Death
1 CONT     * 5 Family
1 CONT     * 6 Sources
1 CONT     * 7 Historical Fiction
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Count of Anjou
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulk was born between 1089 and 1092, the son of Count Fulk IV of Anjou and Bertrade de Montfort. In 1092, Bertrade deserted her husband and bigamously married King Philip I of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT He became count of Anjou upon his father's death in 1109, at the age of approximately twenty. He was originally an opponent of King Henry I of England and a supporter of King Louis VI of France, but in 1127 he allied with Henry wh
1 CONC en Henry arranged for his daughter Matilda to marry Fulk's son Geoffrey of Anjou. Fulk went on crusade in 1120, and become a close friend of the Knights Templar. After his return he began to subsidize the Templars, and maintained 
1 CONC two knights in the Holy Land for a year.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Crusader and King
1 CONT The Kingdom of Jerusalem and the other Crusader states (in shades of green) in 1135 CE, during the reign of Fulk.
1 CONT The Kingdom of Jerusalem and the other Crusader states (in shades of green) in 1135 CE, during the reign of Fulk.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1127 Fulk was preparing to return to Anjou when he received an embassy from King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Baldwin II had no male heirs but had already designated his daughter Melisende to succeed him. Baldwin II wanted to safeg
1 CONC uard his daughter's inheritance by marrying her to a powerful lord. Fulk was a wealthy crusader and experienced military commander, and a widower. His experience in the field would prove invaluable in a frontier state always in th
1 CONC e grip of war.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Fulk held out for better terms than mere consort of the Queen; he wanted to be king alongside Melisende. Baldwin II, reflecting on Fulk's fortune and military exploits, acquiesced. Fulk abdicated his county seat of Anjou 
1 CONC to his son Geoffery and left for Jerusalem, where he married Melisende on June 2, 1129. Later Baldwin II bolstered Melisende's position in the kingdom by making her sole guardian of her son by Fulk, Baldwin III, born in 1130.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulk and Melisende became joint rulers of Jerusalem in 1131 with Baldwin II's death. From the start Fulk assumed sole control of the government, excluding Melisende altogether. He favored fellow countrymen from Anjou to the native
1 CONC  nobility. The other crusader states to the north feared that Fulk would attempt to impose the suzerainty of Jerusalem over them, as Baldwin II had done; but as Fulk was far less powerful than his deceased father-in-law, the north
1 CONC ern states rejected his authority. Melisende's sister Alice of Antioch, exiled from the Principality by Baldwin II, took control of Antioch once more after the death of her father. She allied with Pons of Tripoli and Joscelin II o
1 CONC f Edessa to prevent Fulk from marching north in 1132; Fulk and Pons fought a brief battle before peace was made and Alice was exiled again.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Jerusalem as well, Fulk was resented by the second generation of Jerusalem Christians who had grown up there since the First Crusade. These "natives" focused on Melisende's cousin, the popular Hugh II of Le Puiset, count of Jaf
1 CONC fa, who was devotedly loyal to the Queen. Fulk saw Hugh as a rival, and it did not help matters when Hugh's own stepson accused him of disloyalty. In 1134, in order to expose Hugh, Fulk accused him of infidelity with Melisende. Hu
1 CONC gh rebelled in protest. Hugh secured himself to Jaffa, and allied himself with the Muslims of Ascalon. He was able to defeat the army set against him by Fulk, but this situation could not hold. The Patriarch interceded in the conf
1 CONC lict, perhaps at the behest of Melisende. Fulk agreed to peace and Hugh was exiled from the kingdom for three years, a lenient sentence.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, an assassination attempt was made against Hugh. Fulk, or his supporters, were commonly believed responsible, though direct proof never surfaced. The scandal was all that was needed for the queen's party to take over the g
1 CONC overnment in what amounted to a palace coup. Author and historian Bernard Hamilton wrote that the Fulk's supporters "went in terror of their lives" in the palace. Contemporary author and historian William of Tyre wrote of Fulk "he
1 CONC  never attempted to take the initiative, even in trivial matters, without (Melisende's) consent". The result was that Melisende held direct and unquestioned control over the government from 1136 onwards. Sometime before 1136 Fulk 
1 CONC reconciled with his wife, and a second son, Amalric was born.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Securing the borders
1 CONT
1 CONT Jerusalem's northern border was of great concern. Fulk had been appointed regent of the Principality of Antioch by Baldwin II. As regent he had Raymund of Poitou marry the infant Constance of Antioch, daughter of Bohemund II and A
1 CONC lice of Antioch, and niece to Melisende. However, the greatest concern during Fulk's reign was the rise of Atabeg Zengi of Mosul.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1137 Fulk was defeated in battle near Barin but allied with Mu'in ad-Din Unur, the vizier of Damascus. Damascus was also threatened by Zengi. Fulk captured the fort of Banias, to the north of Lake Tiberias and thus secured the 
1 CONC northern frontier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fulk also strengthened the kingdom's southern border. His butler Paganus built the fortress of Kerak to the south of the Dead Sea, and to help give the kingdom access to the Red Sea, Fulk had Blanche Garde, Ibelin, and other forts
1 CONC  built in the south-west to overpower the Egyptian fortress at Ascalon. This city was a base from which the Egyptian Fatimids launched frequent raids on the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Fulk sought to neutralise this threat.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1137 and 1142, Byzantine emperor John II Comnenus arrived in Syria attempting to impose Byzantine control over the crusader states. John's arrival was ignored by Fulk, who declined an invitation to meet the emperor in Jerusalem
1 CONC .
1 CONT The death of Fulk, as depicted in MS of William of Tyre's Historia and Old French Continuation, painted in Acre, 13C. Bib. Nat. Française.)
1 CONT The death of Fulk, as depicted in MS of William of Tyre's Historia and Old French Continuation, painted in Acre, 13C. Bib. Nat. Française.)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1143, while the king and queen were on holiday in Acre, Fulk was killed in a hunting accident. His horse stumbled, fell, and Fulk's skull was crushed by the saddle, "and his brains gushed forth from both ears and nostrils", as 
1 CONC William of Tyre describes. He was carried back to Acre, where he lay unconscious for three days before he died. He was buried in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Jerusalem. Though their marriage started in conflict, Melisende m
1 CONC ourned for him privately as well as publicly. Fulk was survived by his son Geoffrey of Anjou by his first wife, and Baldwin III and Amalric I by Melisende.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to William, Fulk was "a ruddy man, like David... faithful and gentle, affable and kind... an experienced warrior full of patience and wisdom in military affairs." His chief fault was an inability to remember names and fa
1 CONC ces.
1 CONT
1 CONT William of Tyre described Fulk as a capable soldier and able politician, but observed that Fulk did not adequately attend to the defense of the crusader states to the north. Ibn al-Qalanisi (who calls him al-Kund Anjur, an Arabic 
1 CONC rendering of "Count of Anjou") says that "he was not sound in his judgment nor was he successful in his administration." The Zengids continued their march on the crusader states, culminating in the fall of the County of Edessa in 
1 CONC 1144, which led to the Second Crusade (see Siege of Edessa).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1110, Fulk married Ermengarde of Maine (died 1126), the daughter of Elias I of Maine. Their four children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Geoffrey of Anjou
1 CONT    2. Sibylla of Anjou (1112–1165, Bethlehem), married in 1123 William Clito (div. 1124), married in 1134 Thierry, Count of Flanders
1 CONT    3. Alice (or Isabella) (1107–1154, Fontevrault), married William Adelin; after his death in the White Ship she became a nun and later Abbess of Fontevrault.
1 CONT    4. Elias II of Maine (died 1151)
1 CONT
1 CONT His second wife was Melisende, Queen of Jerusalem
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Baldwin III of Jerusalem
1 CONT    2. Amalric I of Jerusalem
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Orderic Vitalis
1 CONT     * Robert of Torigny
1 CONT     * William of Tyre
1 CONT     * Medieval Women, edited by Derek Baker, the Ecclesiastical History Society, 1978
1 CONT     * Payne, Robert. The Dream and the Tomb, 1984
1 CONT     * The Damascus Chronicle of Crusades, trans. H.A.R. Gibb, 1932.
1 CONT
1 CONT ==========
0 @N6000000000269768269@ NOTE Geoffrey V, Count of Anjou From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Plantagenet, surname, originally nickname, of the English royal house of Anjou or the Angevin dynasty, founded by Geoffrey IV, count of Anjou (1113-51), husband of Matilda (1102-67), daughter of King Henry I of England. The name i
1 CONC s derived from the Latin planta (sprig) and genista (broom plant), in reference to the sprig that Geoffrey always wore in his cap. Reigning from 1154 to 1485, the Plantagenet kings, in the main line of descent, were Henry II, Rich
1 CONC ard I, John, Henry III, Edward I, Edward II, Edward III, and Richard II; through the house of Lancaster, Henry IV, Henry V, and Henry VI; and through the house of York, Edward IV, Edward V, and Richard III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey V (Godefroi) (August 24, 1110 – September 7, 1151), Count of Anjou, Touraine and Maine, and later Duke of Normandy by marriage, called Le Bel ("The Fair"), Martel ("The Hammer") or Plantagenet, was the father of King Henr
1 CONC y II of England, and thus the forefather of the Plantagenet dynasty of English kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey was the eldest son of Fulk, Count of Anjou and King-Consort of Jerusalem. Geoffrey's mother was Eremburge of La Flèche, heiress of Maine. Geoffrey received his nickname for the yellow sprig of broom blossom (genêt is the 
1 CONC French name for the 'genista', or Broom shrub) he wore in his hat as a badge.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Henry I of England, having heard good reports on Geoffrey's talents and prowess, sent his royal legates to Anjou to negotiate a marriage between Geoffrey and his own daughter, Matilda. Consent was obtained from both parties, 
1 CONC and the fifteen-year-old Geoffrey was knighted in Rouen by King Henry in preparation for the wedding. Interestingly, there was no opposition to the marriage from the Church, despite the fact that Geoffrey's sister was the widow of
1 CONC  Matilda's brother (only son of King Henry) which fact had been used to annul the marriage of another of Geoffrey's sisters to the Norman pretender William Clito.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Pentecost 1127, Geoffrey married Empress Matilda, the daughter and heiress of King Henry I of England, by his first wife, Edith of Scotland and widow of Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor. The marriage was meant to seal a peace be
1 CONC tween England/Normandy and Anjou. She was eleven years older than Geoffrey, very proud of her status as an Empress (as opposed to being a mere Countess). Their marriage was a stormy one with frequent long separations, but she bore
1 CONC  him three sons and survived him.
1 CONT
1 CONT The year after the marriage Geoffrey's father left for Jerusalem (where he was to become king), leaving Geoffrey behind as count of Anjou. John of Marmoutier describes Geoffrey as handsome, red-headed, jovial, and a great warrior;
1 CONC  however, Ralph of Diceto alleges that his charm concealed his cold and selfish character.
1 CONT
1 CONT When King Henry I died in 1135, Matilda at once entered Normandy to claim her inheritance. The border districts submitted to her, but England chose her cousin Stephen of Blois for its king, and Normandy soon followed suit. The fol
1 CONC lowing year, Geoffrey gave Ambrieres, Gorron, and Chatilon-sur-Colmont to Juhel de Mayenne, on condition that he help obtain the inheritance of Geoffrey's wife. In 1139 Matilda landed in England with 140 knights, where she was bes
1 CONC ieged at Arundel Castle by King Stephen. In the "Anarchy" which ensued, Stephen was captured at Lincoln in February, 1141, and imprisoned at Bristol. A legatine council of the English church held at Winchester in April 1141 declar
1 CONC ed Stephen deposed and proclaimed Matilda "Lady of the English". Stephen was subsequently released from prison and had himself recrowned on the anniversary of his first coronation.
1 CONT
1 CONT During 1142 and 1143, Geoffrey secured all of Normandy west and south of the Seine, and, on 14 January 1144, he crossed the Seine and entered Rouen. He assumed the title of Duke of Normandy in the summer of 1144. In 1144, he found
1 CONC ed an Augustine priory at Chateau-l'Ermitage in Anjou. Geoffrey held the duchy until 1149, when he and Matilda conjointly ceded it to their son, Henry, which cession was formally ratified by King Louis VII of France the following 
1 CONC year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey also put down three baronial rebellions in Anjou, in 1129, 1135, and 1145-1151. He was often at odds with his younger brother, Elias, whom he had imprisoned until 1151. The threat of rebellion slowed his progress in Norma
1 CONC ndy, and is one reason he could not intervene in England. In 1153, the Treaty of Westminster allowed Stephen should remain King of England for life and that Henry, the son of Geoffrey and Matilda should succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey died suddenly on September 7, 1151. According to John of Marmoutier, Geoffrey was returning from a royal council when he was stricken with fever. He arrived at Château-du-Loir, collapsed on a couch, made bequests of gifts
1 CONC  and charities, and died. He was buried at St. Julien's Cathedral in Le Mans France. Geoffrey and Matilda's children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II of England (1133-1189) Geoffrey, Count of Nantes (1 June 1134 Rouen- 26 July 1158 Nantes) died unmarried and was buried in Nantes William, Count of Poitou (1136-1164) died unmarried Geoffrey also had illegitimate children
1 CONC  by an unknown mistress (or mistresses): Hamelin; Emme, who married Dafydd Ab Owain Gwynedd, Prince of North Wales; and Mary, who became a nun and Abbess of Shaftesbury and who may be the poetess Marie de France. Adelaide of Anger
1 CONC s is sometimes sourced as being the mother of Hamelin.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first reference to Norman heraldry was in 1128, when Henry I of England knighted his son-in-law Geoffrey and granted him a badge of gold lions (or leopards) on a blue background. (A gold lion may already have been Henry's own 
1 CONC badge.) Henry II used two gold lions and two lions on a red background are still part of the arms of Normandy. Henry's son, Richard I, added a third lion to distinguish the arms of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References John of Marmoutier Jim Bradbury, "Geoffrey V of Anjou, Count and Knight", in The Ideals and Practice of Medieval Knighthood III Charles H. Haskins, "Normandy Under Geoffrey Plantagenet", The English Historical Re
1 CONC view, volume 27 (July 1912), pp. 417-444
1 CONT
1 CONT a.k.a. Geoffrey V "Plantagenet", and Duke of Normandy. Had 3 sons, eldest became Henry II
