1 CONC  military court began adopting the trappings of oriental splendour.  In 1120 King Baldwin II moved out of the al-Aqsa mosque and erected a royal palace next to the "Tow of David," the citadel of Jerusalem overlooking the Jaffa Gat
1 CONC e on the west side of the city.
1 CONT In early 1127,l King Baldwin II and the barons had decided to offer his eldest daughter, Melissande to Fulk of Anjoy.  Baldwin had four daughters and so sons.  When he died, in 1131, Melissande and Fulk took his crown.  Baldwin's 
1 CONC wife was Morphia, daughter of Gabriel of Melitene (Malatya).
1 CONT There was a revolt against King Fulk in 1134.  Queen Melissande and her son, Baldwin III, struggled between 1140 and 1152.
1 CONT Phillip of Bavlus, eldest son of Guy de Milly (in Picardy), took up military activities on behalf of Queen Melissande.  The schism between the queen and her two sons became more overt after 1150.  It was evildent that together wit
1 CONC h Amalric--the queen's youngest son and Rohard the elder-Phillip was one of Melissande's stongest supporters.  He was thus put on the losing side when Baldwin III removed the queen from power in Aplril 1152.  After losing her thro
1 CONC ne, Melissande was often sick.  Her last illness, in 1161, killed her.  she died September 11, 1161, in Bethany Convent, where her niece was Abbess.  Melissande was buried in the Tomb of the Virgin in the valley of Jehoshaphat.
1 CONT After his wounding, there is no mention of Hugh de las Puisit in any of the books.  
