0 @N6000000002248112830@ NOTE Baldwin was crowned on Christmas day in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, the boy-king standing beside his mother, who was crowned at the same time to emphasize her position as regent and controlling power of the kingdom.  The pri
1 CONC nces and nobility of the kingdom were all present at the long and complicated ceremony, which was conducted by William, Patriarch of Jerusalem.  Afterward there was a coronation feast.  The boy-king looked the part to perfection. 
1 CONC  William of Tyre, who was about the same age and knew him well, could find few faults in him.  The boy-king had inherited his father's vigor and his mother's beauty.  Unlike his father, who could never remember names, Baldwin III 
1 CONC remembered the names of his lowliest servants and of everyone he met.  He had a passion for books and rode a horse magnificently.  He talked easily and well, possessed a sharp wit, permitted himself and others great freedom of spe
1 CONC ech, and never refused an audience.  As a youth he had many mistresses but after his marriage he remained faithful to his wife.  He drank little. "Excess, " he liked to say, "was the touchstone for the worst crimes."
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: The Dream and the Tomb by Robert Payne  page 150
