0 @N6000000002187903975@ NOTE Robert de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert II de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby, a younger but eldest surviving son of Robert de Ferrers, 1st Earl of Derby, and his wife Hawise, succeeded his father as Earl of Derby in 1139. (William the older brother had been murdered 
1 CONC in London some time before) He was head of a family which controlled a large part of Derbyshire including an area later known as Duffield Frith.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known of Robert's life, other than his generosity to the church. In 1148, he established Merevale Abbey in Warwickshire, England, where he requested to be buried in an ox hide. The stone effigies of Robert and his wife, 
1 CONC Margaret Peverel, lie in the gatehouse chapel of Merevale Abbey, near the village of Atherstone.[1]
1 CONT Margaret Peverel and Robert de Ferrers' effigy in Merevale Abbey
1 CONT Margaret Peverel and Robert de Ferrers' effigy in Merevale Abbey
1 CONT
1 CONT He founded the Priory of Derby, which later moved to Darley Abbey, and its Abbot was granted many privileges in Duffield Forest and Chase.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT He continued his father's attempts to play a role in the civil war commonly called The Anarchy that arose because of the contesting claims of Empress Matilda and Stephen of England. The family's support for Stephen led to him bein
1 CONC g awarded the revenues of the Borough of Derby in 1139, though in 1149 Stephen then granted the Borough to the Earl of Chester[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT He finally threw in his lot with the future Henry II after Tutbury Castle was besieged in 1153.[3] However when Henry came to the throne in 1154, he withdrew de Ferrers' right to use the title of Earl or to receive the "third penn
1 CONC y" on the profits of the county.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died in 1162 and was succeeded by his son William de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. ^ Bland, W., 1887 Duffield Castle: A lecture at the Temperance Hall, Wirksworth Derbyshire Advertiser
1 CONT    2. ^ Turbutt, G., (1999) A History of Derbyshire. Volume 2: Medieval Derbyshire, Cardiff: Merton Priory Press
1 CONT    3. ^ Michael Jones, 'Ferrers, Robert de, first Earl Ferrers (d. 1139)’, Oxford Dictionary of National Biography, Oxford University Press, 2004 [ accessed 28 Oct 2007]
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Robert I de Ferrers Earl of Derby
1 CONT 1139–1162 Succeeded by
1 CONT William I de Ferrers
