0 @N6000000000232590261@ NOTE ==========
1 CONT William de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Ferrers, 3rd Earl of Derby (also spelled "de Ferrer"), the son of Robert de Ferrers, 2nd Earl of Derby, succeeded his father as Earl of Derby in 1162. He was married to Sybil de Braose.
1 CONT
1 CONT William de Ferrers was one of the earls who joined the rebellion against King Henry II of England led by Henry's eldest son, Henry the Younger, in 1173. William had a grudge against Henry because he believed he should have inherit
1 CONC ed the lands of Peverel of the Peak through his mother. However when William Peverel fled the country in 1155, King Henry had confiscated these lands.
1 CONT
1 CONT William was taken prisoner by King Henry and held at Caen. He later joined the Third Crusade and died at the Siege of Acre in 1190.
1 CONT
1 CONT References: Warren, W.L. 1973. Henry II. Eyre Methuen. ISBN 0-413-25580-8
1 CONT
1 CONT Peerage of England
1 CONT Preceded by Robert de Ferrers Earl of Derby 1162–1190 
1 CONT Succeeded by William de Ferrers
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_de_Ferrers%2C_3rd_Earl_of_Derby"
1 CONT ==========
0 @N6000000000337439215@ NOTE Source #1:   George Edward Cokayne, "The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant or Dormant," New Edition, Revised and Much Enlarged, Edited by The Hon. Vicary Gibbs and H. A. D
1 CONC oubleday (London:  The St. Catherine Press, 1926), Vol IV, pp. 192-194.
1 CONT
1 CONT He died on Crusade at the siege of Acre in Palestine, 1190.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 165 
