0 @N6000000002187590260@ NOTE Aenor de Châtellerault
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Aenor of Châttellerrault, duchess of Aquitaine (c. 1103, Châttellerault, Vienne, France – Talmont, March 1130) was the mother of Eleanor of Aquitaine, arguably the most powerful woman in Europe of her generation. The daughter of V
1 CONC iscount Aumary Châttellerault by his concubine, Dangerosa de L' Isle Bouchard, Aenor married the pious William X of Aquitaine, and had three children with him:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor of Aquitaine, 
1 CONT Petronilla of Aquitaine, and 
1 CONT Willian Aigret (who died during infancy) 
1 CONT The county "Châtelherault" later became a title belonging to the Dukes of Hamilton.
1 CONT
1 CONT   This French biographical article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
0 @N6000000001783266511@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT She was the daughter of Viscount Aimery I of Châttellerault and his wife, Dangereuse de L' Isle Bouchard (d. 1151). Aenor married William X of Aquitaine, the son of her mother's lover, and had three children with him:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor of Aquitaine, Duchess of Aquitaine, and wife of both Louis VII of France, and Henry II of England.
1 CONT Petronilla of Aquitaine, wife of Raoul I, Count of Vermandois.
1 CONT William Aigret (who died at the age of four)
1 CONT
1 CONT The county "Châtelherault" later became a title belonging to the Dukes of Hamilton.
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aenor_de_Ch%C3%A2tellerault>
0 @N6000000001783266497@ NOTE Basic Life Information
