1 CONT Henry II
1 CONT 1154-1189
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was born in 1133 and was married to Eleanor, Duchess of
1 CONT Aquitaine immediately after her divorce from Louis VII, King of France.
1 CONT They had nine children: William, Henry, Matilda, Richard, Geoffrey,
1 CONT Philip, Eleanor, Joan and John.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the first of fourteen hereditary kings, who were later refered to
1 CONT in the history oracles as Plantagenets. Henry was the son of the Count of
1 CONT Anjou, whose family emblem was the 'plante genet', a yellow flowering
1 CONT broom.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was with the land bequeathed by the Count to Henry and his auspicious
1 CONT marriage to Eleanor, which gained him a vast amount of lands in France.
1 CONT These lands exceeded the lands owned in France by the King of France,
1 CONT himself. In those times, the King of France ruled from Paris and its surrounding areas.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had lands reaching for 1000 miles, and it was this vast domain, which was called the Angevin Empire. Henry was the first king to demonstrate that he was truly a sovereign, and he ended all the anarchy and demonstration of st
1 CONC rengths throughout his lands. He devoted himself to the internal security of his land and promoted domestic and foreign trade. Productivity doubled during his reign. He revolutionised the law system, and even sat over cases himsel
1 CONC f. He was a notable lawyer, and he built up the system of English Common Law, and began to develop the traditional jury system. He was a gifted administrator.
1 CONT Henry's notable failure was his attempt to curb the power and strength of the Church, particularly
1 CONT in the case of Thomas Beckett, who had been his wild pal until he was made Archbishop of Canterbury. His death did little for Henry's popularity.
1 CONT He was unfortunate in love, relentlessly and romantically pursuing the hand of his wife, Eleanor,
1 CONT who became a selfish spoilt lady, and who turned her sons against their own father. A rebellion by the eldest son, Henry was crushed, and Eleanor was placed under house arrest for fifteen years. The other brothers placed continual
1 CONC  pressure on their father, in alliances with the King of France. Henry died a lonely and griefstricken man deserted by all of those he had loved and honoured.
1 CONT
1 CONT
