0 @N6000000002188193429@ NOTE Shaiba ibn Hashim
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Shaiba ibn Hashim (Arabic: ????? ??? ?????) (c. 500 – 578), better known as Abdul Muttalib, since he was raised up by his uncle Muttalib. He is the grandfather of the Islamic prophet Muhammad and Ali, the first Shia Imam and fourt
1 CONC h Sunni Caliph.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Zamzam
1 CONT     * 3 The Year of the Elephant
1 CONT     * 4 Descendants of Shaiba ibn Hashim
1 CONT     * 5 See also
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT His father was Hashim ibn Abd al-Manaf and his mother was Salma bint Amr from the tribe of an-Najjar in Yathrib. On his fathers side he belonged to the distinguished Banu Hashim clan, a subgroup of the Quraysh tribe of Mecca which
1 CONC  traced their genealogy to Ismail and Ibrahim. In 497 his father died while doing business in Gaza, Palestine before he was born.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was given the name "Shaiba", meaning old man in Arabic, because he was born with a few white hairs. After his fathers death he was raised in Yathrib with his mother and her family until about the age of eight, when his uncle Mu
1 CONC ttalib ibn Abd al-Manaf came to take him to Mecca. Upon first arriving in Mecca, the people assumed the unknown child was Muttalib's slave, giving him the name `Abdu'l-Muttalib (slave of Muttalib). When Muttalib died, Shaiba succe
1 CONC eded him as the chief of the Banu Hashim clan. It is not possible to give the whole history of `Abdu'l-Muttalib, but two important events will be included: the recovery of Zamzam and the attempted attack on the Kaaba by Abraha, th
1 CONC e governor of Ethiopia in Yemen.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Zamzam
1 CONT
1 CONT Hundreds of years ago, Zamzam was filled up and nobody knew its location. One day, `Abdu'l-Muttalib had a series of four dreams directing him to Zamzam's location. `Abdu'l-Muttalib, with his eldest son, Harith ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONC , dug the location where Zamzam is today, finding water after four days of effort. At this success, the Quraish argued that since the well was the property of Ismail, it belonged to the whole tribe. `Abdu'l-Muttalib rejected their
1 CONC  claim, saying that it was given to him by Allah.
1 CONT
1 CONT They agreed to present their case to a wise woman of the tribe of Sa'd in Syria. During the trip, `Abdu'l-Muttalib's water reserves were depleated and his group suffering from thirst. The leaders of the other parties refused to gi
1 CONC ve them water and `Abdu'l-Muttalib advised his group to dig graves, so that when someone died others could bury him.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next day, `Abdu'l-Muttalib exhorted his companions that it was cowardice to succumb to death. He mounted his camel and its foot hit the earth producing a stream of water. The different caravans drank from the fountain and said
1 CONC : "Allah has decided between you and us. By Allah, we will never dispute with you about Zamzam. The same Allah who has created this fountain here in this desert for you has given Zamzam to you."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The Year of the Elephant
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Muslim tradition, the Ethiopian governor of Yemen, Abraha al-Ashram, envied the Kaaba's reverence among the Arabs and, being a Christian, he built a cathedral in Sanaa and ordered pilgrimage be made there. The order w
1 CONC as ignored and someone desecrated the cathedral. Abraha decided to avenge this act by demolishing the Kaaba and he advanced with an army towards Mecca.
1 CONT
1 CONT There were many elephants in Abraha's army and the year came to be known as 'Amul-Fil (Year of the Elephant), beginning a trend for reckoning the years in Arabia which was used until Umar ibn al ­Khattab replaced it with the Islam
1 CONC ic Calendar.
1 CONT
1 CONT When news of the advance of Abraha's army came, the Arab tribes of Quraish, Banu Kinanah, Banu Khuza'a and Banu Hudhayl united in defense of the Kaaba. A man from the Himyar tribe was sent by Abraha to advise them that Abraha only
1 CONC  wished to demolish the Kaaba and if they resisted, they would be crushed. `Abdu'l-Muttalib told the Meccans to seek refuge in the hills while he with some leading members of Quraish, remained within the precincts of the Kaaba. Ab
1 CONC raha sent a dispatch inviting `Abdu'l-Muttalib to meet with Abraha and discuss matters. When `Abdu'l-Muttalib left the meeting he was heard saying, "The Owner of this House is its Defender, and I am sure He will save it from the a
1 CONC ttack of the adversaries and will not dishonor the servants of His House."
1 CONT
1 CONT It is recorded that when the Abraha's forces neared the Kaaba the soldiers of Allah appeared in the form of a dark cloud of small birds who destroyed Abrah's army with raining pebbles from their beaks. Abraha was seriously wounded
1 CONC  and he retreated towards Yemen but died on the way.
1 CONT
1 CONT This event is referred to in the Qur'an, chapter 105 Al-Fil:
1 CONT
1 CONT Have you not seen how your Lord dealt with the owners of the Elephant? Did He not make their treacherous plan go astray? And He sent against them birds in flocks, striking them with stones of baked clay, so He rendered them like s
1 CONC traw eaten up. (Qur'an, 105)
1 CONT
1 CONT This conflict occurred in 570, the same year Muhammad was born.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Descendants of Shaiba ibn Hashim
1 CONT
1 CONT Shaiba ibn Hashim married Fatimah bint Amr and Halah bint Wahab, and fathered many sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT From Fatimah bint Amr:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Harith ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONT    2. ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib - Father of Muhammad
1 CONT    3. Az-Zubayr ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONT    4. Abu Talib ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib - Father of Ali, the first Shia Imam & fourth Sunni Caliph
1 CONT
1 CONT From Halah bint Wahab:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Hamza ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONT
1 CONT Uncategorized:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Saffiyah bint ‘Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONT    2. `Abbas ibn `Abd al-Muttalib
1 CONT    3. Abu Lahab
1 CONT
1 CONT ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Abd al-Muttalib and Aminah bint Wahab were the parents of the Muhammad. Abdallah died four months before Muhammad's birth, and Aminah bint Wahab was taken care of by Shaiba. Aminah also died five years later and Sh
1 CONC aiba ibn Hashim died in 578 when Muhammad was eight. He was then taken care of by his uncle Abu Talib (the father of Ali), a prominent Quraysh chief and custodian of the Kaaba.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Family tree of Shaiba ibn Hashim
1 CONT     * Sahaba
1 CONT     * Muhammad
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
0 @N6000000002188193523@ NOTE Abd-Allah ibn Abd-al-Muttalib
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT ‘Abd Allah ibn ‘Abd al-Mu??alib (Arabic: ??????? ?? ??? ????????)? (545-570) was the father of Muhammad, the prophet of Islam. He was the son of Shaiba ibn Hashim (`Abd al-Muttalib), and was married to Aminah bint Wahab. He died o
1 CONC n a caravan trip between Medina and Mecca.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was famed for his beauty. [citation needed] His name included the word Allah before Islam was founded. During the period before Islam, pagan Arabs called God as Allah in Arabic (and in Aramaic).
1 CONT
1 CONT His full lineage is:
1 CONT '''??????? ?? ??? ?????? ??? ???? ?? ??? ???? ?? ??? ?? ???? ?? ??? ?? ??? ?? ??? ?? ???? ??? ??? ?? ???? ?? ????? ?? ????? ?? ????? ?? ????? ?? ????? ?? ??? ?? ???? ?? ??? ?? ?????'''
1 CONT
1 CONT In Latin script, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abdul Muttalib (Shaiba) ibn Hashim (Amr) ibn Abd Manaf (al-Mughira) ibn Qusai (Zaid) ibn Kilab ibn Murra ibn Ka`b ibn Lu'ay ibn Ghalib ibn Fahr (Quraish) ibn Malik ibn an-Nadr (Qais) ibn Kinana ibn 
1 CONC Khuzaimah ibn Mudrikah (Amir) ibn Ilyas ibn Mudar ibn Nizar ibn Ma`ad ibn Adnan. (ibn means "son of" in Arabic; alternate names of people with two names are given in parentheses.)
