1 CONT In 1154 Louis VII married Constance of Castile, daughter of Alfonso VI I (1126-57), King of Castile. She, too, failed to give him a son and h eir, bearing two more daughters: Marguerite of France (1158-1197), mar ried (1) Henry th
1 CONC e Young King; (2) King Béla III of Hungary (1172-96) ; Adelaide or Alys (4 October 1160 -  ca. 1220), engaged to Richard Io f England; she married William III Talvas, Count of Ponthieu.  Henry I I and Louis were reconciled in Augu
1 CONC t 1154.  As part of the peace proce ss with Henry II of England (1154-89), Louis VII imprudently pledged h is daughter, Marguerite, in the treaty of Gisors (1158) to Henry, Henr y II's eldest son, promising as a dowry the Norman V
1 CONC exin and Gisors. In 1157 Louis conducted a military campaign against Etienne de Sancerr e.
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance died in childbirth on 4 October 1160, and five weeks later L ouis VII married Adèle of Champagne. Henry II, to counterbalance the a dvantage this would give the King of France, had the marriage of thei r children (Henry 
1 CONC "the Young King" and Marguerite) celebrated at once . Louis VII understood the danger of the growing Angevin power, howeve r, through indecision and lack of fiscal and military resources compar ed to Henry II's, Louis VII failed t
1 CONC o oppose Angevin hegemony effectiv ely. One of the few military successes of Louis VII, in 1159, was his expedition in the south to aid Raymond V, Count of Toulouse who had be en attacked by Henry II. At the same time the emperor 
1 CONC Frederick I (115 2-90) in the east was making good the imperial claims on Arles. When t he schism broke out, Louis VII took the part of the Pope Alexander II I (1159-81), the enemy of Frederick I, and after two comical failures of
1 CONC  Frederick I to meet Louis VII at Saint Jean de Losne (on the 29th o f August and the 22nd of September 1162), Louis VII definitely gave hi mself up to the cause of Alexander III, who lived at Sens from 1163 t o 1165. Alexander II
1 CONC I gave the King, in return for his loyal support, the golden rose.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, in 1165 Adèle gave birth to them much longed-for son, along w ith a two daughters a few years later. Louis VII's and Adèle's childre n were: Philip II Augustus (August 22, 1165 - 1223); Agnes of France ( 1171-1240), who w
1 CONC as betrothed to Alexius II Comnenus (1180-83) but mar ried (1) Andronicus I Comnenus (1183-85); Theodore Branas (1204); Loui s VII received Thomas Becket and tried to reconcile him with King Henr y II.  on 6 January 1169, Louis me
1 CONC t with Henry II and his sons Henry a nd Richard at the castle of Montmirial to enshrine the Treaty of Montm irial.  Louis VII sided with Thomas Becket as a way to weaken Henry I I politically.  In 1173, Louis also supported Henry 
1 CONC II's rebellious so ns and in conjunction with Geoffrey moved against the Vexin.  But the rivalry between Henry II's sons and Louis VII's own indecisiveness con tributed to the break up of the coalition (1173-74).  by July of 1173 
1 CONC , Louis had withdrawn his forces back into France.  An attack on Norma ndie in July of 1174 was made, but it was doomed to failure.  Finally in 1177 the Pope intervened to bring the two Kings to terms at Vitry.
