0 @N6000000001783211446@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis VII, called the Younger or the Young (French: Louis le Jeune; 1120 - 18 September 1180), was King of France, the son and successor of Louis VI (hence his nickname). He ruled from 1137 until his death. He was a member of the 
1 CONC House of Capet. His reign was dominated by feudal struggles (in particular with the Angevin family), and saw the beginning of the long feud between France and England. It also saw the beginning of construction on Notre-Dame de Par
1 CONC is and the disastrous Second Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis married three times. By Eleanor of Aquitaine, he had:
1 CONT
1 CONT Marie, married Henry I of Champagne
1 CONT Alix, married Theobald V of Blois
1 CONT
1 CONT By Constance of Castile:
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite of France (1158-97), married (1) Henry the Young King; (2) King Béla III of Hungary (1172-96)
1 CONT Alys (4 October 1160 - c. 1220), engaged to Richard I of England; she married William III Talvas, Count of Ponthieu
1 CONT
1 CONT By Adele of Champagne:
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip II Augustus (August 22, 1165 - 1223)
1 CONT Agnes of France (1171-1240), who was betrothed to Alexius II Comnenus (1180-83) but married (1) Andronicus I Comnenus (1183-85); (2) Theodore Branas (1204)
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Nearing the end of his life, Louis' third wife bore him a son and heir, Philip II Augustus. Louis had him crowned at Reims in 1179, in the Capetian tradition (Philip would in fact be the last King so crowned). Already stricken wit
1 CONC h paralysis, King Louis VII himself was not able to be present at the ceremony. He died on September 18, 1180 at the Abbey at Saint-Pont, Allier and is interred in Saint Denis Basilica
1 CONT wikipedia
